When a rider $r$ requests a trip from origin $\textit{org}_r$, destination $\textit{dst}_r$ and departure time $t_r$ (such request may arrive directly at time $t_r$ or in advance), the following HTTP query is sent to the OTP server:

\lstset{language=url}
\begin{lstlisting}
http://localhost:8803/otp/routers/current/plan?
?fromPlace="+str(origin[0])+"%2C"+str(origin[1])
&toPlace="+str(destination[0])+"%2C"+str(destination[1])+"
&time="+str(hours)+"%3A"+str(minutes)+str(am_or_pm)+
&date=07-20-2022
&numItineraries=10
&mode=TRANSIT%2CWALK&maxWalkDistance=2500.032
\end{lstlisting}

\paragraph{}
Here, origin[0] and origin[1] correspond to the longitude and latitude coordinates of the generated rider. We also have the same case for destination[0] and destination[1]. Hours, minutes and am\_or\_pm are variables that express his departure time. We have chosen 10 itineraries so that a single rider can have different options. 
