\begin{corollary}\label{cor:convex-projection2}
  Let $P$ be a convex polygon with five consecutive vertices $D_1, A, B, C, D_2$ (where possibly $D_1 = D_2$)
  such that the interior angle at $B$ is larger than the interior angles at $D_1$ and $D_2$.
  Let $\ell$ be the line through $B$ parallel to $AC$, $Q_1$ be the intersection of $D_1A$ with $\ell$, and $Q_2$ be the intersection of $D_2C$ with $\ell$.
  Then $\min\{|Q_1B|, |Q_2B|\} \le |AC|$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
  By convexity of $P$, $D_1$ is below $D_2 A$ and so $|Q_1B|$ is at most the distance from $B$ to the intersection of $D_2 A$ with $\ell$.
  The inequality thus follows from Lemma~\ref{lem:convex-projection} applied to $D_2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:convex-polygon-refolding}]
  It suffices to show that, for $n \ge 4$, any doubly covered convex $n$-gon can be reduced to a doubly covered convex $(n-1)$-gon by an $O(1)$-step refolding,
  because then we can reduce both polygons to triangles in $O(n)$ steps,
  and \cite[Theorem~2]{Refolding_CGTA} shows there is a 3-step refolding between any pair of doubly covered triangles with the same area.

  Let $P$ be a convex $n$-gon where $n \ge 4$, and let $B$ be a vertex of $P$ with the largest interior angle.
  By Corollary~\ref{cor:convex-projection2} we can label the nearby vertices of $B$ by $A, C, D$
  such that $A, B, C, D$ are consecutive and $|QB| \le |AC|$ where $Q$ is the intersection of $DC$ with $\ell$, the line through $B$ parallel to $AC$.
  Let $P'$ be the polygon obtained from $P$ by replacing $\triangle ABC$ by a rectangle with base $\seg{AC}$ of the same area,
  and let $P''$ be the polygon obtained from $P$ by replacing $\triangle ABC$ by $\triangle AQC$.
  By Lemma~\ref{lem:acute}, there is an $O(1)$-step refolding between the double covers of $P$ and $P'$;
  it applies because the interior angle of $B$ is at least $90\degree$.
  Similarly, one of Lemmas~\ref{lem:acute}~or~\ref{lem:scalene} (using $|QB| \le |AC|$) shows that there is an $O(1)$-step refolding between the double covers of $P''$ and $P'$.
  Thus there is an $O(1)$-step refolding between double covers of $P$ and $P''$.
  But because $D,C,Q$ are collinear, $P''$ is a convex $(n-1)$-vertex polygon.
\end{proof}

\section{Transformation Between Tree-Shaped Polycubes}
\label{sec:polycube-transformation}

Next we consider \defn{tree-shaped $n$-cubes}, that is,
polyhedral manifolds formed from $n$ unit cubes in 3D
\defn{joined} face-to-face in a tree structure (forming a tree dual graph).
Here, when two cubes get joined together at a common face,
we remove that face from the manifold,
preserving that the manifold is homeomorphic to a disk.
(This notion of ``join'' is a higher-dimensional analog of gluing.)
Thus every tree-shaped $n$-cube has surface area $6n-2(n-1) = 4n+2$.

We allow two cubes to be adjacent even if they are not glued together,
in which case there are two surface squares in between.
If there are no such touching cubes, we call the tree-shaped $n$-cube
\defn{well-separated}.
When the $n$-cubes are not well-separated,
we further allow multiple cubes to occupy the same location in space,
in which case we call the tree-shaped $n$-cube \defn{self-intersecting}.

All cubes of a tree-shaped $n$-cube naturally lie on a cubical grid.
Define \defn{grid cutting} to be cutting restricted to edges of the
cubical grid, and \defn{grid refolding} to be grid cutting followed by gluing
that results in another tree-shaped $n$-cube.

\begin{theorem} \label{thm:polycube}
  Any two tree-shaped $n$-cubes have an $O(n^2)$-step grid refolding,
  where all intermediate manifolds are possibly self-intersecting
  tree-shaped $n$-cubes.
  If the given tree-shaped $n$-cubes do not self-intersect and
  are well-separated, then the intermediate manifolds do not self-intersect.
\end{theorem}

To transform between two given tree-shaped polycubes $\manifold P$ and
$\manifold Q$, we mimic the ``sliding cubes'' model of
reconfiguring modular robots made up of $n$ cubes,
which was recently solved in optimal $O(n^2)$ steps \cite{abel2024universal}.
This model defines two types of operations
(see Figure~\ref{fig:polycube-transform-intro}):
\begin{enumerate}
\item \defn{Slide} a cube along a flat surface of neighboring cubes by 1 unit.
\item \defn{Rotate} a cube around the edge of an adjacent cube.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{figure}
	\centering
	\includegraphics[scale=0.3]{polycube_transformation_intro.pdf}
	\caption{Two different ways an individual cube can move on a surface of a polycube: (1) sliding and (2) rotating.}
	\label{fig:polycube-transform-intro}
\end{figure}

We will show to perform slide and rotate operations for a \defn{leaf} cube,
that is, a leaf of the dual tree in a tree-shaped polycube.
In this case, sliding can be viewed as moving a leaf cube to a new parent,
and rotating can be viewed as the leaf cube attaching to a different location
of the same parent.

To slide a leaf cube,
we perform the following refolding step,
illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:polycube-transform-slide}:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Cut $AB$, $BE$, $ED$, $FG$, $GJ$, and $IJ$.
  These cuts free up the leaf cube to move into the adjacent location,
  as drawn in the intermediary figure in
  Figure~\ref{fig:polycube-transform-slide}.
  %~and~\ref{fig:polycube-transform-slide-2}.
\item Glue $AB'$ to $E'B'$, $FG'$ to $J'G'$, $E'D$ to $AB$,
  $J'I$ to $FG$, $DE$ to $BE$, and $IJ$ to $GJ$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{figure}
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width = 0.9\linewidth]{polycube_transformation_slide.pdf}
	\caption{Sliding a leaf cube.}
	\label{fig:polycube-transform-slide}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:polycube-transform-slide-2} shows an extension of sliding.
Here the leaf cube $IDEJCHGB$ does not move,
but it changes its parent from the cube attached below to the cube
attached on its left, effectively traversing the reflex corner.
The same refolding step as sliding applies in this case.

\begin{figure}
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width = 0.9\linewidth]{corner_slide.pdf}
  \caption{"Sliding" a leaf cube in a reflex corner.}
  \label{fig:polycube-transform-slide-2}
\end{figure}

To rotate a leaf cube around an edge
we perform the following refolding step,
illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:polycube-transform-rotate}:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Cut $BA$, $AD$, $DE$, $GF$, $FI$, and $IJ$.
  Similar to the sliding procedure, these cuts free up the leaf cube to move,
  as shown in the intermediary figure in
  Figure~\ref{fig:polycube-transform-rotate}.
\item Glue $AB$ to $AD$, $FG$ to $FI$, $BA'$ to $DE$,
  $GF'$ to $IJ$, $A'D'$ to $ED'$, and $F'I'$ to $JI'$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{figure}
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width = 0.9\linewidth]{polycube_transformation_rotate.pdf}
  \caption{Rotating a leaf cube over the edge of a polycube.}
  \label{fig:polycube-transform-rotate}
\end{figure}

By combining these three operations, we can follow a simple algorithm for
transforming a given $n$-cube $\manifold P$ into a $1 \times 1 \times n$ line:
%
\begin{enumerate}
\item Fix one leaf cube as the \defn{root} cube~$c_1$.
  Assume by symmetry that the root cube's unique neighbor
  is in the down direction.
\item For $i = 2, 3, \dots, n$:
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item Assume $c_1, \dots, c_{i-1}$ have been arranged into an upward line,
    with $c_i$ being a current leaf.
  \item Take a leaf cube $c_i$ that is not $c_{i-1}$
    (given that there are always at least two leaves).
  \item Slide and rotate $c_i$ around the boundary of the rest of the tree
    until it reaches the root cube $c_1$, and then slide it up the line
    to place it immediately above $c_{i-1}$.
  \end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}

This algorithm requires $O(n^2)$ steps: potentially each of the $n$ cubes
needs to traverse the surface area of the tree-shaped $n$-cube, which is $O(n)$.
It may also cause self-intersection, because it blindly follows the surface of
the tree-shaped $n$-cube, so it may place the moving leaf cube on top of
an adjacent cube in the case of touching cubes.
If the tree-shaped $n$-cube is well-separated, though,
then this simple algorithm avoids self-intersection.

To transform between two tree-shaped $n$-cubes $\manifold P$ and $\manifold Q$,
we apply the algorithm above separately
to each of $\manifold P$ and $\manifold Q$,
perform the refolding steps on $\manifold P$ to transform it into a line,
and then perform the reverse refolding steps on $\manifold Q$ to transform
the line into it.  (Note that each refolding step is reversible.)
Thus we have proved Theorem~\ref{thm:polycube}.

It is tempting to apply the (much more complicated)
$O(n^2)$-step algorithm of Abel, Akitaya, Kominers, Korman, and Stock
\cite{abel2024universal}, which has the advantage of avoiding self-intersection
without any assumption of well-separation.
Unfortunately, sliding and rotating nonleaf cubes seems more difficult.
One approach is to transform one spanning tree into another
(probably increasing the number of steps),
but it is not even clear whether this can be done by
leaf reparenting operations.

It also seems likely that some of these moves can be done in parallel
in the same refolding step, leading to fewer refolding steps.
Some models of modular robotics have parallel reconfiguration algorithms
that move a linear number of robots in each round
\cite{aloupis2008reconfiguration,aloupis2009linear}.
It remains open whether we can get similarly good bounds
in the cube sliding model or the leaf-focused sliding-by-refolding model.

\section{Transformation Between Polyhedral Manifolds}
\label{sec:polyhedral-manifolds-transformation}

In this section, we prove the main result of the paper:

\begin{theorem} \label{thm:polyhedral-manifolds}
  Any two polyhedral manifolds $\manifold P, \manifold Q$
  of the same surface area have a 2-step refolding.
  If $\manifold P$ and $\manifold Q$ have no boundary,
  then so does the intermediate manifold $\manifold I$.
  If $\manifold P$ and $\manifold Q$ are embedded in 3D,
  then so is the intermediate manifold $\manifold I$.
\end{theorem}

Sections~\ref{sec:abstract-transformation}--\ref{sec:embeddable-polyhedra-refolding}
are devoted to the proof.
Then Section~\ref{sec:n-polyhedral-manifold-transform} will generalize
to $n$ manifolds:

\begin{corollary} \label{cor:polyhedral-manifolds}
  For any $n$ polyhedral manifolds $\manifold P_1, \dots, \manifold P_n$
  of the same surface area, there is another polyhedral manifold $\manifold I$
  such that $\manifold P_i$ and $\manifold I$ have a common unfolding
  for all~$i$.
  If manifolds $\manifold P_i$ have no boundary,
  then so does~$\manifold I$.
  If manifolds $\manifold P_i$ are embedded in 3D,
  then so is~$\manifold I$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Abstract Intermediate Polyhedron}
\label{sec:abstract-transformation}

We start by proving the first two claims in
Theorem~\ref{thm:polyhedral-manifolds},
ignoring the requirement of embeddability of the
intermediate manifold $\manifold I$.
This serves as a useful warmup and an overview of our methods.

We start by computing a \defn{common dissection} of the given manifolds
$\manifold P$ and $\manifold Q$ of equal surface area, that is,
a subdivision of each surface into polygons that match in
the sense that, for some perfect pairing of $\manifold P$'s polygons
with $\manifold Q$'s polygons, there is an isometry between paired polygons.
Solutions to this dissection problem go back to the early 1800s
\cite{lowry1814solution,wallace1831elements,bolyai1832tentamen,gerwien1833zerschneidung}.
See \cite{HingedDissections_DCG} for a more algorithmic description,
and pseudopolynomial bounds on the number of pieces.
We can further assume that the dissection is a triangulation,
where triangles meet edge-to-edge,
by triangulating each polygon in the dissection.

\begin{figure}
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width = 0.95\linewidth]{non-embeddable_gluing.pdf}
  \caption{2-step refolding of each triangle $\triangle A E I$ from $\manifold P$ to $\manifold Q$, via an abstract intermediate manifold~$\manifold I$.}
  \label{fig:abstract-triangle}
\end{figure}

Now consider an arbitrary triangle $\triangle AEI$
in the common triangulation of $\manifold P$ and $\manifold Q$,
as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:abstract-triangle}.
We divide each edge of this triangle $\triangle AEI$
into four line segments of equal length:
$AE$ divides into $AB=BC=CD=DE$, $EI$ divides into $EF=FG=GH=HI$, and
$IA$ divides into $IJ=JK=KL=LA$.%
%
\footnote{%
  For orientable surfaces, it would be enough to divide each edge
  into only two equal line segments, but the symmetry of four segments
  enables even non-orientable (flipped) gluings;
  it will also be useful when we make $\manifold I$ embeddable.
}
%
The (abstract) intermediate manifold $\manifold I$ glues this triangle to
other triangles using a mixture of the gluings
from $\manifold P$ and $\manifold Q$:
the outer edges $AB$, $DE$, $EF$, $HI$, $IJ$, and $LA$ are glued as in
$\manifold P$, while the inner edges $BC$, $CD$, $FG$, $GH$, $JK$, and $KL$
are glued as in $\manifold Q$.

In more detail, our 2-step refolding algorithm from $\manifold P$ to
$\manifold Q$ works as follows:
%
\begin{description}
\item[Cut 1:] For each triangle $\triangle AEI$,
cut the inner edges $BC$, $CD$, $FG$, $GH$, $JK$, and $KL$
(represented by blue dotted lines in Figure~\ref{fig:abstract-triangle}).
Because no cut fully separates an entire edge from its mate in $\manifold P$,
this cutting preserves connectivity.

\item[Glue 1:] For each triangle $\triangle AEI$,
glue the inner edges $BC$, $CD$, $FG$, $GH$, $JK$, and $KL$
to their neighbors in $\manifold Q$ when they exist
(as in Figure~\ref{fig:abstract-triangle}, middle),
producing the (abstract) intermediate manifold~$\manifold I$.
If $\manifold P$ and $\manifold Q$ have no boundary,
i.e., every edge is glued to another edge,
then so will $\manifold I$.
(In fact, we can achieve this property of $\manifold I$ more generally
by gluing together boundary edges of $\manifold I$ of equal length.)

\item[Cut 2:] For each triangle $\triangle AEI$,
cut the outer edges $AB$, $DE$, $EF$, $HI$, $IJ$, and $LA$
(represented by purple dashed lines in Figure~\ref{fig:abstract-triangle}).
Because no cut fully separates an entire edge from its mate in $\manifold Q$,
this cutting preserves connectivity.
(If we glued together boundary edges during the previous step, Glue~1,
cut those edges also.)

\item[Glue 2:] For each triangle $\triangle AEI$,
glue the outer edges $AB$, $DE$, $EF$, $HI$, $IJ$, and $LA$
to their neighbors in $\manifold Q$ when they exist
(as in Figure~\ref{fig:abstract-triangle}, right).
Now every edge is fully glued as in $\manifold Q$,
so we have arrived at manifold $\manifold Q$.
\end{description}

\subsection{Embeddable Intermediate Polyhedron via Burago--Zalgaller Theorem}

The above construction of an abstract intermediate manifold $\manifold I$
is actually already guaranteed to be embeddable in 3D whenever the input
manifolds $\manifold P$ and $\manifold Q$ are so embedded.
Because $\manifold P$ and $\manifold Q$ are embedded,
they either are both orientable or both have boundary.
Our construction for $\manifold I$ inherits either property.
Then we can apply a powerful result of Burago and Zalgaller:

\begin{theorem}[{\cite[Theorem~1.7]{Burago-Zalgaller-1996}}]
  Every polyhedral manifold that is either orientable or has boundary
  admits an isometric piecewise-linear $C^0$ embedding into 3D.
\end{theorem}

Unfortunately, this embedding is quite complicated.
See \cite{Saucan-2012} for a description.
Thus we develop an alternative solution that involves modifying
the 2-step refolding and intermediate manifold.

\subsection{Explicit Embeddable Intermediate Polyhedron}
\label{sec:embeddable-polyhedra-refolding}

Next we describe the modifications necessary to make the intermediate
manifold $\manifold I$ relatively easy to embed in 3D, assuming that
the input manifolds $\manifold P$ and $\manifold Q$ are so embedded.
The main idea is to cut each triangle further
so that the area next to each edge can unravel into a long doubly covered
``strip'' that lets it reach far away to its neighbor in the other manifold.
To enable a particularly clean construction
(shown in Figure~\ref{fig:single-triangle}),
we need to guarantee that most triangles are acute.

In more detail, our construction of the intermediate manifold $\manifold I$
works as follows:
%
\begin{enumerate}
\item
  First we construct a common \emph{nonobtuse} triangulation
  of manifolds $\manifold{P}$ and $\manifold{Q}$,
  by taking the common dissection described in
  Section~\ref{sec:abstract-transformation} and
  subdividing according to the method of Saraf~\cite{saraf2009acute}.
  In the language of \cite[Theorem 2.3]{saraf2009acute}, we are given a
  ``subdivided polyhedral surface'' (the common dissection),
  and we want to ``subtriangulate'' the polygons into nonobtuse triangles
  that fit together into a proper triangulation of the entire surface
  (where triangles meet at whole edges, never partial edges).
  This algorithm has two additional properties that we exploit:
  %
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item The algorithm subdivides the edges of the input polygons,
    and then triangulates the interior of each input polygon
    without introducing any more vertices on the edges.
    The subdivision of each edge depends only on the length of the edge
    and global parameters (the minimum angle and the minimum edge length
    in the input polygons).
    In particular, the subdivision and triangulation depends on the geometry
    of the input polygons, but not on the combinatorial information
    of which faces are glued together.
    Thus, the subdivision and triangulation will be the same
    when applied to both $\manifold{P}$ and $\manifold{Q}$,
    so we obtain a common nonobtuse triangulation.
  \item The algorithm to triangulate each input polygon first builds a layer
    of triangles around the boundary, based on properties of the edge
    subdivision algorithm, and then fills in the remaining interior
    with nonobtuse triangles (mostly by starting with a nearly square
    rectangular grid).
    This algorithm guarantees that the triangles sharing an edge with the
    boundary of the input polygon are all strictly acute.
    Thus we can assume that all right triangles in the common triangulation
    have the same neighboring triangles
    in both $\manifold{P}$ and~$\manifold{Q}$;
    only acute triangles have differing neighbors
    between $\manifold{P}$ and $\manifold{Q}$.
  \end{enumerate}

\begin{figure}[h]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width = 0.9\linewidth]{single_triangle_folded.pdf}
  \caption{Acute and right triangles crease/cut patterns (left) and resulting 3D structure after folding (right).}
  \label{fig:single-triangle}
\end{figure}

\item
  For every triangle in the common triangulation as viewed on~$\manifold P$,
  we collapse the triangle according to a crease pattern
  drawn in Figure~\ref{fig:single-triangle}.
  Here, $X'$ is a point on $XO$ very close to $O$.
  For an acute triangle $\triangle AEI$
  (Figure~\ref{fig:single-triangle}, top left),
  we mountain fold the perpendicular bisectors $OC$, $OG$, and $OK$
  by $180^\circ$, which meet at the circumcenter~$O$,
  and valley fold the lines $OA$, $OE$, and $OI$ connecting $O$ to the
  triangle vertices, each by $60^\circ$.
  Then we can collapse the structure into a 3D rabbit-ear-like doubly covered
  structure shown in Figure~\ref{fig:single-triangle}~(top right).
  For a right triangle $\triangle AEI$
  (Figure~\ref{fig:single-triangle}, bottom left),
  the circumcenter $O$ lands on the midpoint $K$ of the hypotenuse,
  and we need only three folds:
  two mountain folds on the perpendicular bisectors $CK$ and $GK$
  on the sides adjacent to the right angle,
  and a valley fold from the circumcenter $O = K$ to the right-angle vertex.

\item
  For every acute triangle in the common triangulation
  as viewed on~$\manifold P$,
  we cut along the pattern of green lines
  drawn in the top row of Figure~\ref{fig:single-triangle}.
  In the 3D rabbit-ear-like doubly covered folding, these cuts make a
  zig-zag strip out of the material near the middle half of each edge,
  enabling the material to fold far away while remaining doubly covered.
  Because of the double covering,
  we can glue the cuts together between the two layers,
  so that we do not introduce any boundary in manifold~$\manifold I$.

\item
  Similar to the abstract manifold from
  Section~\ref{sec:abstract-transformation},
  we cut along the inner edges $BC$, $CD$, $FG$, $GH$, $JK$, and $KL$,
  and then glue them to their neighbors in~$\manifold Q$.
  But now the green cuts allow for this connection to be stretched
  around the rest of the manifold to avoid collisions (as detailed below).

\item
  To reduce the number of connections from $\manifold P$
  that we need to preserve, we also cut along many of the outer edges
  $AB$, $DE$, $EF$, $HI$, $IJ$, and $LA$ of the triangles $\triangle AEI$.
  Specifically, we construct a spanning tree on the dual graph of the
  common triangulation as viewed on~$\manifold P$,
  and then cut all outer edges between triangles that are not adjacent
  in the spanning tree.
  Then we glue these edges to each other locally within their triangle
  ($AB$ to $DE$, $EF$ to $HI$, and $IJ$ to $LA$),
  so that we do not introduce any boundary in manifold~$\manifold I$.
\end{enumerate}

Although we described the construction of $\manifold I$ in terms of
multiple cut and glue steps, it is in fact formed by performing all
cut steps followed by all glue steps, as detailed below.
The cut steps preserve connectivity because of the spanning tree of the
dual graph of the common triangulation viewed on~$\manifold P$.

\begin{lemma}
  \label{lem:intermediate-embeddibility}
  The manifold $\manifold{I}$ constructed above can be embedded in 3D
  (in fact, \defn{flat folded} into a stack of 2D layers) with zero volume.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
  To construct the embedding of $\manifold I$, we first show how to combine
  the rabbit-ear-like doubly covered folding of each triangle from
  Figure~\ref{fig:single-triangle} into a single structure that achieves
  all connections in $\manifold P$.
  Refer to Figure~\ref{fig:embeddability-proof}.

\begin{figure}[h]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width = 0.9\linewidth]{embeddability.pdf}
  \caption{Flat folding the intermediate manifold $\manifold I$
    without self-intersection.
    We do not attempt to represent the zig-zag strips and their folding.}
  \label{fig:embeddability-proof}
\end{figure}

  \begin{enumerate}
  \item
    As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:embeddability-proof}a,
    we start with the common nonobtuse triangulation as viewed on~$\manifold P$,
    draw all the perpendicular bisector folds (blue lines)
    which also act as the dual graph,
    and cut all but a spanning tree of this dual graph (red lines).
    We also choose a leaf in the spanning tree (highlighted red)
    to serve as the root of the tree,
    so that every node has at most two children.

  \item
    As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:embeddability-proof}b,
    we fold each triangle into the rabbit-ear-like doubly covered structure
    from Figure~\ref{fig:single-triangle}, but flattened into the plane.
    Now we see how connections between neighboring triangles work:
    the foldings share the folded-in-half common edge $CFB$
    and live on opposite sides of that line.
    Figure~\ref{fig:embeddability-proof}c shows a
    zoomed-in view of two adjacent folded triangles.

  \item
    Figure~\ref{fig:embeddability-proof}d
    shows a parent triangle and its up to two children triangles.
    We recursively construct an embedding of each child rooted subtree,
    and then combine them by keeping the layers of each child subtree
    completely above or below the layers of the other child subtree
    as well as the parent triangle.
    Thus we obtain an embedding of the parent's rooted subtree.
    In the end, all vertices of the triangles collapse down to a
    single central point $C$,
    and all circumcenters $O_i$ will be scattered around $C$
    and connected to $C$ via an edge.

  \end{enumerate}

  Next we extend the zig-zag arms of the triangles to reach their neighbors
  in~$\manifold Q$.  These arms end at the edges $C O_i$.
  By folding the zig-zag arms into a long straight strip, we can extend
  the arms arbitrarily far (by adding appropriately many zig-zags).
  By extending the arms sufficiently far,
  they can exit the folded tree structure described above.
  Then we can navigate the arms to meet each other according to the edge
  pairing in the common triangulation as viewed on~$\manifold Q$.
  Although ugly, this is possible without self-intersection and
  without adding any boundary.
\end{proof}

Our 2-step refolding algorithm from $\manifold P$ to $\manifold I$ to
$\manifold Q$ works as follows:

\begin{description}

\item[Cut 1:]~
\begin{itemize}
\item Cut manifold $\manifold{P}$ along all edges except a spanning tree
  of the dual graph of the common nonobtuse triangulation as viewed
  on~$\manifold P$.
  These cuts preserve connectivity by the spanning-tree property.
\item For every acute triangle in the common triangulation,
  cut the green lines drawn in Figure~\ref{fig:single-triangle}~(left).
  Because no edge gets fully cut and no cut divides a face in two,
  connectivity is preserved.
\end{itemize}

\item[Glue 1:] For each triangle $\triangle AEI$:

\begin{itemize}
\item For each green zig-zag edge in Figure~\ref{fig:single-triangle},
  glue it to its reflection across the mountain line of symmetry,
  forming a doubly covered zig-zag strip.
  In particular, glue the boundaries:
  $BB'$ to $DD'$, $FF'$ to $HH'$, $JJ'$ to $LL'$.
\item If the following edges are not already attached via the spanning tree,
glue $AB$ to $ED$, $EF$ to $IH$, and $IJ$ to $AL$.
\item Glue the inner edges $BC$, $CD$, $FG$, $GH$, $JK$, and $KL$
  across to corresponding edges of the neighboring triangle in~$\manifold Q$.
\end{itemize}

\end{description}

The second cut and glue steps are similar to the abstract construction
in Section~\ref{sec:abstract-transformation}:

\begin{description}

\item[Cut 2:] For each triangle $\triangle AEI$:
  \begin{itemize}
  \item Cut the outer edges $AB$, $DE$, $EF$, $HI$, $IJ$, and $LA$.
  \item Cut the green edges, including the boundaries
    $BB'$, $DD'$, $FF'$, $HH'$, $JJ'$, and $LL'$.
  \end{itemize}

\item[Glue 2:] For each triangle $\triangle AEI$:
  \begin{itemize}
  \item Glue the outer edges $AB$, $DE$, $EF$, $HI$, $IJ$, and $LA$
    to their neighbors in $\manifold Q$ when they exist.
  \item Glue the green edges back together to form the triangle on the left
    of Figure~\ref{fig:single-triangle}.
  \end{itemize}

  We arrive at manifold $\manifold Q$.

\end{description}

\subsection{Full Example}

In this section, we demonstrate our 2-step refolding algorithm
with a simple nontrivial example, shown in Figure~\ref{fig:2-step-example}:
a triangular bipyramid $\manifold{P}$ to flat hexagon~$\manifold Q$.

\begin{figure}
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width = 0.6\linewidth]{2-step-example-target.pdf}
  \caption{Goal for the example: transform the triangular bipyramid
    $\manifold{P}$ into the flat hexagon~$\manifold{Q}$.}
  \label{fig:2-step-example}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:2-step-intermediary} shows the intermediate manifold
$\manifold{I}$ for these $\manifold P$ and $\manifold Q$.
In both the flat unfolding on the left and the folded form on the right,
we can see the gluings for both $\manifold{P}$ and $\manifold{Q}$
via solid and dashed lines respectively.

\begin{figure}
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width = 0.9\linewidth]{2-step-example-I-schematic.pdf}
  \caption{Illustration of the intermediate manifold $\manifold{I}$
    in its flat state (left) and folded state (right), with folds and cuts
    for each triangle in accordance with Figure~\ref{fig:single-triangle}.}
  \label{fig:2-step-intermediary}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width = 0.8\linewidth]{2-step-example-physical.pdf}
	\caption{Physical example of transformation shown in Figure~\ref{fig:2-step-example}}
	\label{fig:2-step-physical-example}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:2-step-physical-example} shows photographs of a physical
execution of this refolding. The refolding steps are as follows:

\begin{description}

\item[Cut 1:]

Starting with $\manifold{P}$ in Figure~\ref{fig:2-step-physical-example}a,
we edge-unfold $\manifold{P}$ into an unfolding
(Figure~\ref{fig:2-step-physical-example}b).
Then we further fold and cut each individual triangle in accordance
with Figure~\ref{fig:single-triangle} to obtain
Figure~\ref{fig:2-step-physical-example}c.
For the purposes of this demonstration, however, we cut
only the necessary zig-zags into the physical paper.

\item[Glue 1:]

Next we glue the zig-zag strips to their target location
as defined on $\manifold Q$ to obtain $\manifold I$,
as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:2-step-physical-example}d.

\item[Cut 2:]

To transform from $\manifold I$ to $\manifold Q$,
we first cut all the connections from $\manifold P$ in~$\manifold I$,
which allows for the shape to be re-arranged into something close to the
target manifold $\manifold Q$ (Figure~\ref{fig:2-step-physical-example}e).
Then we cut the previously glued together zig-zag edges.
The result can flatten out to the full manifold $\manifold Q$,
as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:2-step-physical-example}f.

\item[Glue 2:]

Finally, glue all the matching edges together to form $\manifold Q$.

\end{description}

\subsection{Transformation Between $n$ Polyhedral Manifolds}
\label{sec:n-polyhedral-manifold-transform}

In this section, we extend our techniques to
prove Corollary~\ref{cor:polyhedral-manifolds}:
2-step refolding between $n$ polyhedral manifolds
$\manifold P_1, \manifold P_2, \dots, \manifold P_n$
via the same intermediate manifold $\manifold I$.

\begin{figure}
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width = 0.8\linewidth]{multiple_transformation.pdf}
	\caption{Intermediate manifold $\manifold I$ for 2-step folding between $n$ polyhedral manifolds $\manifold P_1, \manifold P_2, \dots, \manifold P_n$, and the cut-and-glue step to transform to manifold~$\manifold{P}_n$.}
	\label{fig:n-polyhedral-manifold-transformation}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:n-polyhedral-manifold-transformation}
illustrates the abstract case.
Instead of dividing each edge into four equal segments,
we divide them into $2 n$ equal segments,
where the $i$th successive nested pair of segments attach to
the triangle's neighbor in $\manifold P_i$.
Then we apply similar constructions
to Section~\ref{sec:embeddable-polyhedra-refolding}
for each acute triangle, as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Find the circumcenter and fold into a rabbit-ear-like doubly covered
  structure, just as in Figure~\ref{fig:single-triangle}.
\item Cut the zig-zag pattern shown in Figure~\ref{fig:single-triangle}
  for each pair of $\manifold P_i$ edges on each edge of the triangle,
  for $i > 1$.  (For $\manifold P_1$, we can avoid these cuts,
  like $\manifold P$ in Figure~\ref{fig:single-triangle}.)
\item Extend each zig-zag strip to attach to its neighbor in $\manifold{P}_i$.
\end{enumerate}

To transform from this intermediate manifold $\manifold I$
to any target $\manifold{P}_i$, we cut all connections except for the
$\manifold{P}_i$ connections, and glue all cut edges
according to~$\manifold{P}_i$.

\section{Conclusion}

In this paper, we showed a transformation algorithm between any two manifolds with two cut-and-glue refolding steps. When transforming between manifold~$\manifold{P}$ and manifold~$\manifold{Q}$, we go through an intermediate embeddable polyhedron which is not necessarily convex. We also showed two simpler refolding algorithms for doubly covered polyhedra and tree-shaped polycubes.

Many open questions remain:
\begin{itemize}
\item Are there examples where 1-step refolding is impossible?
\item If the two given polyhedra are convex, is there a finite-step refolding where the intermediate polyhedra are also convex? \cite[Section 25.8.3]{Demaine-O'Rourke-2007}
\item Can we extend our polycube result to avoid self-intersection without well-separation, or to support non-tree-shaped polycubes of the same surface area?
\item Can we improve the number of refolding steps needed for the doubly covered polygon or polycube refolding algorithms?
\end{itemize}

