Figure~\ref{fig: case} illustrates the roles of self-proving and the checker during data extraction. This is a level 3 extraction case, meaning the target data is not directly found in the table and requires computation during extraction. In this example, simple addition is needed to obtain the total value of wheat production. Similar calculation processes include averaging, etc. Level 3 data extraction requires the model to have semantic understanding of the data and numerical computation abilities, making it relatively difficult during data extraction. Consequently, Level 3 hit rates are generally low in Table~\ref{tab:main-results}.

During data extraction, the checker, as an independent agent, supervises the data extractor and provides corresponding suggestions. If the checker rejects a result, the process reverts to the data extractor, and the suggestions serve as additional input to guide more accurate extraction. In actual experiments, the probability of checker feedback being triggered is approximately 12\%, with the majority of rejections resolved within one cycle. We set the maximum feedback loop to 3 times.

% Tables 1 and 2 demonstrate that LLM methods still exhibit a significant performance gap compared to humans in meta-analysis tasks, primarily reflected in the following aspects: First, in the paper screening stage, LLM models tend to provide homogeneous evaluations for most papers, often being overly lenient and assigning generally high scores. This makes it difficult for the models to identify truly high-quality and highly relevant papers. The proposed MeX framework addresses this issue by combining individual review and comparative review, thereby increasing the diversity of review outcomes.

% Second, LLM models show low hit rates in the data extraction stage. On the one hand, most LLM models experience a decline in reasoning and analytical capabilities when handling large-scale data, leading to inaccurate and incomplete extraction of required data. MeX mitigates this by decomposing the data extraction task into two subtasks: first identifying which sections contain useful data and then extracting data from those sections-reducing task complexity. On the other hand, traditional LLM data extraction lacks an iterative refinement mechanism, while many errors can be corrected through simple checks. By introducing an additional checker, MeX significantly improves the final quality of data extraction.



