			\end{figure}
		\end{minipage}
	\end{center}

	Partially ordered multisets, or \emph{pomsets}~\cite{Pratt86pomsets}, can represent executions of a parallel program.
	We consider in this article the class of \emph{series-parallel} pomsets admitting a linear description made of letters as well as the sequential ${\cdot}$ and parallel $\parallel$ composition operators.
	Figure~\ref{fig:sp_pomset} displays the Hasse diagram of the pomset $a (b \parallel b) c (ba \parallel bb)$.
	This model is not only richer than linear words, but may also be exponentially more succinct:
	were we to consider the interleaving semantics on $n$ parallel threads labelled by $n$ distinct letters $a_1, \ldots, a_n$, a single pomset $a_1 \parallel \ldots \parallel a_n$ describes the $n!$ possible linearized traces of the interwoven threads.

	\emph{Active learning} consists in inferring a formal model of a black-box system that can be dynamically queried.
	Under \emph{the Minimally Adequate Teacher} (MAT) framework, interactions with the black-box system are twofold:
	\emph{membership queries} that consist in asking whether a given trace can be generated by the system, and \emph{equivalence queries} to determine whether a given automaton (known as the hypothesis) accurately represents all executions of the system, returning a counter-example if the answer is negative.

	One of the earliest active learning algorithms is Angluin's $L^*$~\cite{DBLP:journals/iandc/Angluin87} for rational languages.
	It infers the finite set of Myhill-Nerode equivalence classes of the target language by exploring a set of \emph{representatives} of these classes and maintaining a set of \emph{distinguishers} that separates them.
	Van Heerdt et al.~\cite{DBLP:conf/fossacs/HeerdtKR021} applied $L^*$ to the class of \emph{recognizable} pomset languages accepted by algebraic structures known as \emph{pomset recognizers}.
	This extension is made possible by the Myhill-Nerode quotient space of recognizable pomset languages being finite.

	However, various improvements have been brought to the original $L^*$ algorithm over the years.
	The length $m$ of a counter-example returned by the MAT being arbitrarily long, it may end up dominating the learning process;
	Rivest and Schapire~\cite{DBLP:journals/iandc/RivestS93} therefore introduced an algorithm that infers a new equivalence class in $\bigo(\log(m))$ membership queries.
	Moreover, the use of equivalence queries makes little practical sense as it implies that the MAT knows the very formal model of the system we are trying to infer;
	Chow~\cite{DBLP:journals/tse/Chow78} thus proved that a finite test suite could subsume equivalence of finite automata, provided a bound on the size of the target model is known beforehand.
	Finally, new algorithms such as TTT~\cite{isberner14ttt}, $L^\#$~\cite{vaandrager22lsharp}, or $\Llambda$~\cite{DBLP:conf/birthday/HowarS22} have been shown to significantly reduce the number of membership queries performed.

	Our motivation is adapt and extend these state-of-the-art techniques to the active learning of pomset recognizers. Our new contributions are the following:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item We introduce a new counter-example handling algorithm. Its complexity depends on the depth of the counter-example's syntactic tree, rather than its number of nodes.

		\item We extend Howar et al.'s $\Llambda$ algorithm~\cite{DBLP:conf/birthday/HowarS22} to recognizable pomset languages. $\Llambda$ has been proven to be competitive with state-of-the-art active learning algorithms for rational languages and maintains a prefix (resp. suffix) closed set of representatives (resp. distinguishers), further reducing the influence of the counter-example's maximal length.

		\item We make use of redundancy-free discrimination trees~\cite{kearns94clt}, in the sense that we only perform membership queries that contribute to the distinction of states.

		\item In a similar fashion to Chow's $W$-method~\cite{DBLP:journals/tse/Chow78}, we design a finite test suite that can conditionally replace equivalence queries.
	\end{itemize}


	\subsection{Related works}

	Different classes of pomsets~\cite{DBLP:journals/iandc/FahrenbergJSZ22, DBLP:journals/dm/Fishburn85, VTL82:SPDigraphs} accepted by different automata~\cite{DBLP:conf/concur/FahrenbergJSZ22, lodaya98kleene, lodaya01kleene} have been developed in the literature, reflecting various communication models and interpretations of concurrency.	
	Automata over series-parallel pomsets, referred to as \emph{branching automata}, were first introduced by Lodaya and Weil~\cite{LW98:Algebra,LW00:sp}, as well as a generalization of regular expressions~\cite{lodaya98kleene, lodaya01kleene} to pomset languages and a logical characterization \cite{DBLP:conf/mfcs/Bedon13}.
	In \cite{DBLP:journals/jlp/KappeBLSZ19}, a different class of automata for pomsets, known as \emph{pomset automata}, was introduced.
	It was later shown in \cite{DBLP:conf/fsttcs/Bedon21} that branching and pomset automata are effectively equivalent.

	From an algebraic perspective, Lodaya and Weil defined \emph{SP-algebras} (from which pomset recognizers are derived): sets equipped with two inner products, one associative, the other associative and commutative.
	Pomset recognizers may be understood as a special case of deterministic bottom-up tree automata;
	as a consequence, \textbf{(\romannumeral 1)} equivalence of pomset recognizers is decidable.
	Lodaya and Weil proved that languages recognized by SP-algebras \textbf{(\romannumeral 2)} adhere to a Myhill-Nerode-like theorem and are also recognized by branching automata, but that \textbf{(\romannumeral 3)} the converse does not hold.
	Van Heerdt et al.~\cite{DBLP:conf/fossacs/HeerdtKR021} further provided a translation of pomset recognizers to pomset automata.
	Thanks to \textbf{\romannumeral 1} and \textbf{\romannumeral 2}, it is possible to learn pomset recognizers, but the same algorithms do not apply to pomset automata due to \textbf{\romannumeral 3}.

	The original $L^*$ active learning algorithm~\cite{DBLP:journals/iandc/Angluin87} maintains a table structure called an \emph{observation table} that it fills by calling membership queries.
	The rows of the table help identify the Myhill-Nerode classes of the target language and their representatives.
	TTT~\cite{isberner14ttt} instead features a \emph{discrimination tree}: this sparser structure has been experimentally shown to reduce the number of queries needed.
	$L^\#$~\cite{vaandrager22lsharp} operates directly on a trie that stores membership queries and tries to establish apartness, a constructive form of non-equivalence.
	$\Llambda$~\cite{DBLP:conf/birthday/HowarS22} relies on partition refinement; its peculiarity is not adding substrings of counter-examples to the underlying data structure.
	The last three algorithms have all been shown to be competitive and a net improvement over $L^*$.
	We chose to focus on $\Llambda$ thanks to its generic, unifying framework;
	$L^\#$'s prefix-closed trie structure is intrinsically tied to words, hence total orders, and TTT heavily relies on a complex automata-theoretic process to refine its discrimination tree that can seldom be generalized to pomsets.



	\section{Preliminary Definitions}


	\subsection{Series-parallel pomsets}

	We consider a non-empty finite set $\Sigma$ of \emph{letters} (or \emph{labels}) called the \emph{alphabet}.

	\begin{definition}[Poset]
		A partially ordered set or \emph{poset} $(A, <, \ell)$ consists of a finite set $A$ (called the \emph{carrier}), a strict partial order $<$ on $A$, and a labelling map $\ell : A \to \Sigma$.
	\end{definition}

	Two posets $(A, <_A, \ell_A)$ and $(B, <_B,\ell_B)$ are said to be isomorphic if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B$ preserving ordering and labelling.

	\begin{definition}[Pomset~\cite{DBLP:journals/tcs/Gischer88}]
		A partially ordered multiset or \emph{pomset} is an equivalence class for the isomorphism relation on posets.
	\end{definition}

	For convenience's sake, we will treat a representative of such a pomset as if it were the entire class. The empty pomset is denoted $\varepsilon$, and the singleton pomset labelled by $a \in \Sigma$.
	We say that a pomset $\mathfrak{B}$ is a subpomset of $\mathfrak{A}$ if $\mathfrak{B}$ can be embedded in $\mathfrak{A}$, that is, if there exists an injection from $B$ to $A$ preserving labelling and ordering.

	Pomsets can be composed sequentially (in a similar fashion to words) or in parallel fashion.
	Let $\mathfrak{A} = (A, {<_A}, \ell_A)$ and $\mathfrak{B} = (B, {<_B}, \ell_B)$ be two pomsets such that $A$ and $B$ are disjoint (an assumption that applies to the rest of this article).
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item Their \emph{parallel composition} $\mathfrak{A} \parallel \mathfrak{B}$ is $(A \cup B, {<_A} \cup {<_B}, \ell_B \cup \ell_B)$. The two pomsets are juxtaposed but cannot be compared.
		\item Their \emph{sequential composition} (or concatenation) $\mathfrak{A} \cdot \mathfrak{B}$ is $(A \cup B, {<_A} \cup {<_B} \cup (A \times B), \ell_A \cup \ell_B)$.
		Every element of $B$ is greater than every element of $A$.
	\end{enumerate}

	Parallel composition is associative and commutative; sequential composition is merely associative.
	Both operations share $\varepsilon$ as neutral element.

	\begin{definition}[Series-parallel pomsets]
		The set $\SP$ of series-parallel pomsets is the smallest set of pomsets containing $\{\varepsilon\}$ and $\Sigma$ closed under sequential and parallel composition.
	\end{definition}

	\begin{center}
		\begin{minipage}{0.61\linewidth}
			\setlength{\parindent}{15pt}
			A pomset $\mathfrak{A}$ is said to be \emph{N-free} if the pomset $(\{x_1, x_2, x_3, x_4\}, \{x_1 < x_2, x_3 < x_2, x_3 < x_4\}, \ell)$ for some labelling $\ell$ is not a subpomset of $\mathfrak{A}$. Intuitively, the pattern shown in Figure \ref{fig:N_pattern} does not appear in the pomset's Hasse diagram.
			It is well-known that series-parallel pomsets coincide with N-free pomsets~\cite{VTL82:SPDigraphs}.
		\end{minipage}
		\hfill
		\begin{minipage}{0.35\linewidth}
			\begin{figure}[H]
				\centering
				\begin{tikzpicture}[shorten >=5pt, scale=0.9]
					\node (x1) {$a$};
					\node (x2) [right of=x1] {$b$};
					\node (x3) [below of=x1] {$c$};
					\node (x4) [right of=x3] {$d$};

					\path[shorten <= 5pt, line width=0.1pt]
					(x1.center) edge node {} (x2.center)
					(x3.center) edge node {} (x2.center)
					(x3.center) edge node {} (x4.center);
				\end{tikzpicture}
				\caption{A N pattern.}
				\label{fig:N_pattern}
			\end{figure}
		\end{minipage}
	\end{center}

	We denote $\SPplus = \SP \setminus \{\varepsilon\}$. For the remainder of this paper, we will refer to series-parallel pomsets as merely pomsets.

	The set of \emph{syntactic terms} (or simply terms) $\terms$ over $\Sigma$ is the set of full binary trees whose inner nodes are labelled by operators in $\{\cdot, \parallel\}$ and whose leaves are labelled by $\Sigma \cup \{\varepsilon\}$.
	We associate each term with its isomorphic \emph{linear description} obtained by performing a prefix traversal.
	We may omit the symbol $\cdot$, consider that $\cdot$ has priority over $\parallel$, and assume left associativity for syntactic purposes.	
	Intuitively, a term is merely a way to describe a pomset: as an example, $a (b \parallel b) c (ba \parallel bb)$ represents the pomset of Figure~\ref{fig:sp_pomset}.

	Due to neutrality, associativity and commutativity properties, several terms may describe the same pomset: as an example, $a \parallel b = b \parallel a = a \cdot \varepsilon \parallel b$.
	Each pomset $w \in \SP$ may therefore be associated with a set $\ST{w} \subseteq \terms$ of syntactically different but semantically equivalent terms whose interpretation in $\SP$ is $w$.
	The \emph{depth} $\depth{w}$ of $w$ is the minimum of the tree depth function on $\ST{w}$.
	A term in $\ST{w}$ is said to be \emph{canonical} if its depth is minimal and, assuming $w \neq \varepsilon$, it has no leaf labelled by $\varepsilon$.
	Note that canonical terms are not unique: $\ST{w}$ may contain more than one canonical term.

	If $w \not\in \Sigma \cup \{\varepsilon\}$, given a canonical term of $w$ such that its root is labelled by $\circ \in \operators$ and its left (resp. right) subtree is a term representing pomset $z_1$ (resp. $z_2$), $z = z_1 \circ z_2$ is called a \emph{canonical decomposition} of $z$.
	Obviously, $\depth{z_1} < \depth{z}$ and $\depth{z_2} < \depth{z}$.
	Figure~\ref{fig:synt_tree} displays a canonical term of $bc \parallel a$ of depth $2$.

	The use of terms is a consequence of our counter-example handling algorithm and test suite for equivalence queries that manipulate syntactic trees.
	Nevertheless, most concepts and algorithms on pomsets outlined in this article still remain term-agnostic.


	\subsection{Pomset recognizers}

	\begin{definition}[Bimonoids~\cite{bloom1996free}]
		A \emph{bimonoid} $(M, \odot, \obar, \neut)$ is a set $M$ equipped with two internal associative operations $\odot$ and $\obar$, $\obar$ being commutative as well, and a neutral element $\neut$ common to $\odot$ and $\obar$.
	\end{definition}

	These constraints define a \emph{variety} of bimonoids, that is, a class of algebraic structures satisfying the same behaviour (as defined by various equations encoding associativity, commutativity, etc.).
%	Historically, \emph{SP-algebra} were first introduced as sets equipped with two associative internal operations, one of them being commutative too~\cite{lodaya98kleene, LW98:Algebra, LW00:sp, lodaya01kleene}. Providing a common neutral element results in a bimonoid.
	Note that there is no distributivity property.
	The set $\SP$ endowed with $\cdot$, $\parallel$ and the neutral element $\varepsilon$ is a bimonoid.

	A set $A$ \emph{generates} a bimonoid $(M, \odot, \obar, \neut)$ if $A \subseteq M$ and any element of $M \setminus \{\neut\}$ can be obtained by inductively applying $\odot$ and $\obar$ to $A$.
	Moreover, $A$ \emph{freely} generates $M$ if any element of $M \setminus \{\neut\}$ admits a unique (up to commutativity and associativity) decomposition according to $A \setminus \{\neut\}$, $\odot$, and $\obar$.

	\begin{theorem}[Freeness of $\SP$~\cite{bloom1996free}]
		$(\SP, \cdot, \parallel, \varepsilon)$ is freely generated by $\Sigma$ in the variety of bimonoids.
	\end{theorem}

%	Actually, $\terms$ equipped with $\cdot,\parallel$ and $\varepsilon$ is the well-known \emph{term algebra} of the corresponding type of which $\SP$ is a homomorphic image.
%	More precisely, for all $w \in \SP$, $\ST{w} \in \terms$ is mapped to $w \in \SP$.
	In particular, all the $\varepsilon$-free terms of the same pomset are equivalent up to commutativity and associativity:
	as an example, the terms $a \parallel bc$ and $bc \parallel a$ describe the same pomset, and no other $\varepsilon$-free term exists.

%	\begin{definition}[Recognizable pomset languages]
%		A set (or \emph{language}) $L \subseteq \SP$ is said to be \emph{recognizable} if there exists a \emph{finite} bimonoid $(M, \odot, \obar, \neut)$ and a homomorphism $\psi \colon \SP \to M$ such that $L = \psi^{-1}(\psi(L))$.
%	\end{definition}
%
%	Intuitively, there exists a subset $F \subseteq M$ of elements of $M$ such that the elements of $L$ are \emph{exactly} the elements $x \in \SP$ such that $\psi(x) \in F$: we may equate $F$ with the accepting states of an automaton, the elements of the recognized language being exactly the ones leading to an accepting state.

	We rely on bimonoids to recognize languages of SP-pomsets.
	As is customary, we define homomorphisms of bimonoids as mappings between two bimonoids preserving identity and both internal operations.
	Note that, $\SP$ being freely generated by $\Sigma$, any function $i \colon \Sigma \to M$ for some bimonoid $(M, \odot, \obar, \neut)$ can be (inductively) extended in a unique way to a bimonoid homomorphism $\eval : \SP \to M$ so that for all $a \in \Sigma$, $\eval(a) = i(a)$ and $\eval(\varepsilon) = \neut$. This leads to the following definition of a pomset recognizer~\cite{DBLP:conf/fossacs/HeerdtKR021}.

	\begin{definition}[Pomset recognizer]
		The tuple $\PR = (M, \odot, \obar, \neut, i, F)$ is said to be a \emph{pomset recognizer} (PR) on $\Sigma$ if $(M, \odot, \obar, \neut)$ is a finite bimonoid, $i \colon \Sigma \to M$, and $F \subseteq M$. The carrier $M$ is also called the set of \emph{states} of $\PR$.
		The language of $\PR$, denoted $\Lang{\PR}$, is the set $\{w \in \SP \mid \eval(w) \in F\}$.
		Finally, we introduce the predicate $\PR(u) = ``\eval(w) \in F"$.
	\end{definition}

	PRs act as bottom-up deterministic finite tree automata on terms: each letter in $\Sigma$ has an image in a set of states $M$, that we combine by using the images $\odot$ and $\obar$ of the operators $\cdot$ and $\parallel$.
	Due to the freeness of $\SP$ and $\eval$ being a homomorphism, we can apply PRs to pomsets, as $\eval(t)$ always return the same result, regardless of the term $t \in \ST{w}$ chosen.

	\begin{definition}[Recognizable pomset languages]
		A set (or \emph{language}) $L \subseteq \SP$ is said to be \emph{recognizable} if there exists a PR $\PR$ such that $L = \Lang{\PR}$.
	\end{definition}

	\begin{example}
		\label{ex:PR}
		Let $L$ be the language containing singleton $c$ and every pomset $(a \parallel bu)$ where $u \in L$, i.e. $L = \{c, a \parallel (bc), a \parallel (b (a \parallel (bc))), \dots\}$.
		This language is accepted by the PR $\PR = (M, \odot, \obar, \neut, i, F)$ where 
		$M = \{r_a, r_b, r_c, r_{bc}, r_0, \neut\}$,
		$i(x) = r_x$ for $x \in \{a, b, c\}$,
		$F = \{r_c\}$, and
		$\obar$ and $\odot$ are such that $r_b \odot r_c = r_{bc}$, $r_a \obar r_{bc} = r_c$,
		$\neut$ is the neutral element for both operations, and all the other possible products return $r_0$.
	\end{example}

	\begin{definition}[Equivalence]
		Two PRs $\PR_1$ and $\PR_2$ on a common alphabet $\Sigma$ are \emph{equivalent} if $\Lang{\PR_1}$ = $\Lang{\PR_2}$.
	\end{definition}

	We define the set of \emph{evaluation trees} $\evat{\PR}{w}$ of a pomset $w$ in a PR $\PR = (M, \odot, \obar, \neut, i, F)$ by relabelling the nodes of $w$'s terms in $\ST{w}$ with states of $\PR$ inductively:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item If node $\iota$ is labelled with $x \in \Sigma \cup \{\varepsilon\}$ in $\ST{w}$, then we relabel it with $\eval_\PR(x)$ instead.

		\item If node $\iota$ is labelled with $\circ \in \operators$ and its left (resp. right) subtree represents pomset $w_1$ (resp. $w_2$) and $m_1 = \eval(w_1)$ (resp. $m_2 = \eval(w_2)$), then we relabel it with state $\eval_\PR(m_1 \circ_\PR m_2)$ instead for the appropriate $\circ_\PR \in \{\odot, \obar\}$.
	\end{itemize}

