	\subsection{A Myhill-Nerode theorem}

	Let $L \subseteq \SP$ and $u, v \in \SP$.
	$u \sim_L v$ if for all $c \in \Ct{1}$, $c[u] \in L \iff c[v] \in L$.
	The relation $\sim_L$ is an equivalence relation;
	we say that it is a \emph{congruence} relation on $\SP$ if it is preserved by $\cdot$ and $\parallel$.
	$[w]_{\sim_L}$ stands for the equivalence class of $w$ in the quotient space $\qspace{L}$ of $\SP$ w.r.t. $\sim_L$.
	It induces a syntactic homomorphism $\SP \to \SP_{/\sim_L}$ and there exists a Myhill-Nerode characterization of recognizable languages of $\SP$:

	\begin{theorem}[Characterizing recognizable languages~\cite{LW00:sp}]
		$L$ is recognizable if and only if $\sim_L$ is a congruence relation of finite index.
		\label{th:myhill_nerode}
	\end{theorem}

	Let $w_1, w_2 \in \SP$.
	Given a pomset language $L$, we say that $c \in \Ct{1}$ is a \emph{distinguishing context} in $L$ for $w_1$ and $w_2$ if $c[w_1] \in L \iff c[w_2] \notin L$, that is, one of $c[w_1]$ and $c[w_2]$ is in $L$ while the other is not.
	If we assume $L$ is recognized by a pomset recognizer $\PR$, this necessarily implies that $m_1 = \eval(c[w_1]) \neq m_2 = \eval(c[w_2])$: one state must be in $F$ while the other is not.
	We then say that $c$ \emph{distinguishes} the states $m_1$ and $m_2$.
	If there is no such $c$, we say that $m_1$ (resp. $w_1$) and $m_2$ (resp. $w_2$) are \emph{indistinguishable}.

	\begin{definition}[Reachable and minimal pomset recognizers]
		A pomset recognizer $\PR = (M, \odot, \obar, 1, i, F)$ is said to be \emph{reachable} if, for all $m \in M$, there exists $w \in \SP$ such that $\eval(w) = m$; $w$ is said to be an \emph{access sequence} of $m$.

		Moreover, it is \emph{minimal} if it is reachable and for all $w_1, w_2 \in \SP$ such that $\eval(w_1) \neq \eval(w_2)$, there exists $c \in \Ct{1}$ such that $\PR(c[w_1]) \neq \PR(c[w_2])$. 
	\end{definition}

	Intuitively, $\PR$ is minimal if any pair of states in $M$ can always be distinguished by some context. If $L$ is recognizable, $\sim_L$ induces an obvious minimal recognizer $\PR_L = (\SP_{/\sim_L}, \cdot, \parallel, [\varepsilon]_{\sim_L}, i_L, F_L)$ such that $\forall a \in \Sigma$, $i_L(a) = [a]_{\sim_L}$ and $F_L = \{[w]_{\sim_L} \in \qspace{L} \mid w \in L\}$.



	\section{An Introduction to Active Learning}


	\subsection{The active learning framework}

	Consider a recognizable pomset language $L$ on an alphabet $\Sigma$. Let $\Mod$ be a minimal PR called the \emph{model} such that $\Lang{\Mod} = L$.
	\emph{Active learning} is a cooperative game between a learner and a \emph{minimally adequate teacher} (MAT).
	It consists for the learner in computing a minimal pomset recognizer for $L$ by asking two types of queries on $L$ to the MAT:
	\begin{description}
		\item[Membership queries.] Given $w \in \SP$, does $w \in L$, i.e. what is $\Mod(w)$?

		\item[Equivalence queries] Given a pomset recognizer $\Hyp$ (called the hypothesis) on $\Sigma$, does $\Lang{\Hyp} = L = \Lang{\Mod}$?
		If it does not, return a \emph{counter-example} $w \in \SP$ such that $\Hyp(w) \neq \Mod(w)$.
	\end{description}

	The ability to infer the model $\Mod$ stems from Theorem \ref{th:myhill_nerode}.
	Active learning algorithms compute an \emph{under-approximation} $\sim_\Hyp$ of ${\sim_L} = {\sim_\Mod}$ such that $w_1 \not\sim_\Hyp w_2 \implies w_1 \not\sim_L w_2$.
	To do so, they maintain a finite set $S$ of pomsets and a finite set $\C$ of contexts.
	Each pair of elements of $S$ is distinguished by at least one element of $\C$, thus bearing witness to the existence of at least $|S|$ equivalence classes of $\sim_L$, the set $S$ being their representatives.

	Obviously, $w_1 \sim_\Hyp w_2 \implies w_1 \sim_L w_2$ may not hold if the hypothesis is too coarse, thus, $\Hyp$ may have to be refined several times.
	Nevertheless, each refinement increases the number of equivalences classes of $\sim_L$ distinguished by $\sim_\Hyp$, until the classes of $\sim_\Hyp$ are exactly the classes of $\sim_L$, at which point ${\sim_\Hyp} = {\sim_\Mod} = {\sim_L}$ and $\Lang{\Hyp} = \Lang{\Mod} = L$.


	\subsection{Common structures and invariants}

	\subsubsection{Data structures.}

	We maintain a finite set $S$ of pomsets called the set of \emph{representatives} or \emph{access sequences}, meant to store the representatives of $\sim_L$'s equivalence classes.
	By design, $S$ will be closed by the subpomset operation and contain the empty pomset $\varepsilon$.
	We also introduce the \emph{frontier} set $S^+ = \left(\Sigma \cup \{u \circ v \mid \circ \in \{\cdot,\parallel\}, u, v \in S\}\right) \setminus S$ that contains combinations of elements of $S$ and single letters:
	its purpose is to infer the internal operations $\cdot_\Mod$ and $\parallel_\Mod$ of the model.

	A \emph{pack of components} $\B = \{B_1, \ldots, B_m\}$ partitions $S \cup S^+$ in such a manner each component contains at least one $s \in S$.
	For $s \in S \cup S^+$, $\B_s$ stands for the only component of $\B$ $s$ belongs to.
	Given $B \in \B$, $\as_\B(B) = S \cap B$ is called the set of \emph{access sequences} of $B$.
	For $s \in S \cup S^+$, we define $\as_\B(s) = \as_\B(\B_s)$.
	$\B$ under-approximates the classes of $\sim_\Mod$.

	Finally, we maintain a \emph{discrimination tree} $\D$:
	it is a full binary tree, its inner nodes being labelled by contexts in $\Co$, and its leaves, either unlabelled or labelled by a component of $\B$ in such a fashion $\D$'s set of labelled leaves is in bijection with $\B$.
	In particular, $\D$'s root is labelled by $\square$.
	The labels of $\D$'s inner nodes form a set of contexts $\C$.
	Given $B \in \B$, $\C_B$ is defined as the set of contexts that appear along the branch that runs from the root of $\D$ to the leaf labelled by $B$.
	In particular, note that for all $B \in \B$, $\square \in \C_B$.
	$\D$'s use is to posit which class of $\B$ a pomset belongs to.	

	\subsubsection{Operations and invariants.}

	For any pomset $w \in \SP$, we define the \emph{sifting} operation of $w$ through $\D$: starting at the root of $\D$, at every node labelled by a context $c$ of $\D$ we branch to the right (resp. left) child if $c[w] \in L$ (resp. $c[w] \not\in L$).
	We iterate this procedure until a leaf is reached: the matching component $B \in \B$ is the result of the sifting operation.
	We define $\D(w) = B$.	
	Note that $\D(w)$ may be undefined if $w$ is sifted into an unlabelled leaf. Thus, $\D$ can be viewed as a partial function $\SP \to \B$.
	Sifting requires a number of membership queries bounded by the height of $\D$.
	Intuitively, the discrimination tree is used to classify pomsets:
	pomsets that behave similarly w.r.t. the finite set of distinguishing contexts $\C_B$ are lumped into the same component $B \in \B$.	

	\begin{property}
		\label{prop:invariants}
		By design, the learning algorithm maintains the following invariants:
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item For any $s \in S \cup S^+$, $\D(s) = \B_s$.

			\item For any $B \in \B$, $s_1, s_2 \in B$, $c \in C_B$, $\Mod(c[s_1]) = \Mod(c[s_2])$.

			\item Let $B_1, B_2 \in \B$ such that $B_1 \neq B_2$; then for any $s_1 \in B_1$, $s_2 \in B_2$, there exists $c \in \C_{B_1} \cap \C_{B_2}$ such that $\Mod(c[s_1]) \neq \Mod(c[s_2])$; $c$ labels $B_1$ and $B_2$'s deepest common ancestor in $\D$.

			\item Let $\sim_\B$ be the equivalence relation on $S \cup S^+$ inferred from the partition $\B$.
			Then it is an under-approximation of $\sim_L$ on $S \cup S^+$: $\forall s_1, s_2 \in S \cup S^+$, $s_1 \not\sim_\B s_2 \implies s_1 \not\sim_L s_2$.
			As a consequence, $|\B| \leq |\SP / {\sim_L}|$.
		\end{enumerate}
	\end{property}

	For $w \in \SP$, if $\D(w)$ is defined, we write $\B_w = \D(w)$.
	Thanks to Invariant $1$, this notation doesn't invalidate the previous notation $\B_s$ for $s \in S \cup S^+$.


	\subsection{Building the hypothesis}

	\subsubsection{Properties of the partition.}

	$\B$ is said to be \emph{consistent} if for any $B_1, B_2 \in \B$, $u_1, v_1 \in \as_\B(B_1)$, $u_2, v_2 \in \as_\B(B_2)$, and $\circ \in \operators$, $u_1 \circ u_2$ and $v_1 \circ v_2$ belong to the same component of $\B$.
	Intuitively, no matter the representatives of $B_1$ and $B_2$ we consider, their composition will belong to the same component.
	Moreover, $\B$ is \emph{$\circ$-associative} for $\circ \in \operators$ if for any $s_1, s_2, s_3 \in S$ and $s_l \in \as_\B(s_1 \circ s_2)$, $s_r \in \as_\B(s_2 \circ s_3)$, $\B_{s_l \circ s_3} = \B_{s_1 \circ s_r}$.
	Finally, $\B$ is said to be \emph{sharp} if for any $B \in \B$, $|S \cap B| = 1$.

	Thus, we can extend the operators $\cdot$ and $\parallel$ to components of $\B$, and the resulting laws will be internal and associative.
	Finally, for any $B \in \B$, since $\square \in \C_B$ and for any $u \in B$, $\square[u] = u$, $\Mod$ is constant on $B$: this shared value is written $\Mod(B)$.

	\subsubsection{Defining the hypothesis.}

	If $\B$ is \emph{consistent}, \emph{$\cdot$-associative}, and \emph{$\parallel$-associative}, then we design the hypothesis $\Hyp = (H, \cdot_\Hyp, \parallel_\Hyp, \neut_\Hyp, i_\Hyp, F_\Hyp)$ as follows:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item $H = \B$. $\Hyp$'s states are the postulated equivalence classes of $\sim_L$.

		\item Given $u, v \in S$, since $\B$ is \emph{consistent}, we can define $\B_u \cdot_\Hyp \B_v = \B_{u \cdot v}$ (resp. $\B_u \parallel_\Hyp \B_v = \B_{u \parallel v}$). We use $S^+$ and $\B$ to build $\Hyp$'s internal operations.

		\item $\neut_\Hyp = \B_\varepsilon$. The neutral element is the class of the empty pomset.

		\item Given $a \in \Sigma$, $i_\Hyp(a) = \B_a$. We rely on $\Sigma \subseteq S \cup S^+$ to build a pomset homomorphism.

		\item $F_\Hyp = \{B \in \B \mid \Mod(B) = 1\}$. A component is accepting if its members are accepted by $\Mod$. $F_\Hyp$ corresponds to the leaves of $\D$ belonging to its right subtree.
	\end{itemize}

	For $w \in \SP$, we define the component $\B_w = \eval_\Hyp(w)$ $w$ evaluates to in $\Hyp$ and its set of access sequences $\as_\Hyp(w) = \as_\B(\B_w)$.
	As proven later in Lemma~\ref{lem:reach}, this notation is compatible with the earlier definition of $\B_w$ for $w \in S \cup S^+$.
	By design of $\Hyp$, freeness of pomset recognizers, and consistency of $\B$, the hypothesis handles pomsets and their access sequences similarly:

	\begin{property}[Substitution by access sequences]
		\label{prop:freeness_as}
		$\forall c \in \Co$, $\forall w \in \SP$, $\forall p \in \as_\Hyp(w)$, $\Hyp(c[w]) = \Hyp(c[p])$ and $\eval_\Hyp(c[w]) = \eval_\Hyp(c[p])$.
	\end{property}


	\subsubsection{Compatibility of the hypothesis.}

	Given a set $X \subseteq \SP$ of pomsets, hypothesis $\Hyp$ is $X$-\emph{compatible} if for any $w \in X$, $\Hyp(w) = \Mod(w)$.
	$\Hyp$ is said to be \emph{compatible} with $\B$ if it is compatible with $\underset{B \in \B}{\bigcup} \{c[s] \mid s \in B, c \in \C_B\}$.

	Active learning algorithms such as TTT~\cite{isberner14ttt}, $L^\#$~\cite{vaandrager22lsharp}, or van Heerdt et al.'s adaptation of $L^*$~\cite{DBLP:conf/fossacs/HeerdtKR021} to pomset recognizers may not always immediately result in a compatible hypothesis.
	However, incompatibilities provide a 'free' counter-example $c[s]$ such that $\Hyp(c[s]) \neq \Mod(c[s])$ without requiring an extra membership or equivalence query.
	We should therefore guarantee that $\Hyp$ is compatible with $\B$ before submitting an equivalence query.


	\subsection{Handling counter-examples}

	The Rivest-Schapire counter-example handling method on finite words consists in studying all the possible splits $w = u \cdot a \cdot v$, $a \in \Sigma$, of a counter-example $w \in \Sigma^+$,
	then trying to find one such that the hypothesis and the model agree on the input $p' \cdot v$ where $p' \in \as_\Hyp(u \cdot a)$ but disagree on $p \cdot a \cdot v$ where $p \in \as_\Hyp(u)$,
	thus proving that the successor of state $\B_p$ in the hypothesis has been incorrectly identified as $\B_{p'}$, suffix $v$ being witness to this error.

	Intuitively, replacing a prefix $u$ of $w$ by its access sequence $p$ is akin to feeding $u$ to the hypothesis, then letting either the model or the hypothesis handle the rest of the computation,
	iterating on all possible splits until the algorithm witnesses the model and hypothesis no longer being in agreement.
	This change of behaviour, called a \emph{breaking point}, yields a distinguishing suffix and a further refinement of the partition $\B$ and its matching hypothesis.

	Extending breaking points to pomsets is non-trivial due to the branching nature of terms.
	Assume that $\B$ is a consistent, associative partition from which a hypothesis $\Hyp$ is inferred.
	We define breaking points w.r.t. canonical decomposition:

	\begin{definition}[Agreement]
		\label{def:agree}
		Given $c \in \Co$ and $z \in \SP$, we define the \emph{agreement} predicate $\agree(c, z) = ``\forall p \in \as_\Hyp(z), \Hyp(c[p]) = \Mod(c[p])"$.
	\end{definition}

	\begin{definition}[Breaking point]
		\label{def:bp}
		Given a counter-example $w \in \SP^+$ such that $\Hyp(w) \neq \Mod(w)$, and a split $(c, z)$ of $w$ such that $\agree(c, z) = 1$, a (left) \emph{breaking point} is either:
		\begin{itemize}
			\item the pair $(c, z)$ if $z \in \Sigma$;

			\item a quadruplet $(c, \circ, z_1, z_2)$ where $\circ \in \operators$, $z_1, z_2 \in \SPplus$, $z = z_1 \circ z_2$ is a canonical decomposition of $z$, and $\agree(c[\square \circ z_2], z_1) = 0$.
		\end{itemize}
	\end{definition}

	We seek a split $(c, z)$ of $w$ such that there exists $p \in S^+ \cap \B_z$, for any $p' \in \as_\Hyp(z)$, $\Mod(c[p']) \neq \Mod(c[p])$ as it guarantees that $p$ belongs to another class of $\sim_L$ than the current elements of $S$, resulting in a refinement of $\B_z$. 	
	Unlike the Rivest-Schapire decomposition, we may however not directly be able to infer a distinguishing context from every breaking point.
	Indeed, given a breaking point $(c, \circ, z_1, z_2)$, while $c$ distinguishes $p_1 \circ z_2$ for some $p_1 \in \as_\Hyp(z_1)$ from any $p' \in \as_\Hyp(z)$, $p_1 \circ z_2$ may not belong to $S^+$.
	Nevertheless, Algorithm \ref{algo:find_ebp} can alter an original counter-example $w$ until a breaking point can be used to infer a new class and a refinement.
	We call such a breaking point \emph{effective}.



	\section{Adapting the $\Llambda$ Algorithm}

	We detail here the various components of the $\Llambda$ active learning algorithm, some of them being somewhat data agnostic, others being peculiar to pomset languages.


	\subsection{Expanding components}

	Algorithm \ref{algo:expand} inserts a new pomset $w$ belonging to the frontier $S^+$ or equal to $\varepsilon$ into the set~$S$ of access sequences then updates $S^+$ by exploring $w$'s successors (i.e. the pomsets that we can build by combining $w$ with another element of $S$) and using $\D$ to sift them into the existing partition $\B$.
	\begin{algorithm}
		\caption{$\mathtt{Expand}(w)$ where $w \in S^+$ or $w = \varepsilon$ if $\B = \emptyset$}
		\label{algo:expand}
		\begin{algorithmic}[1]
			\State $S \gets S \cup \{w\}$
			\For{$p \in \{w\} \cup \{p' \circ w, w \circ p' \mid \circ \in \operators, p' \in S\} \cup \Sigma$} \label{algo:expand:letters}
				\If{$p$ does not belong to any class of $\B$}
					\State $B \gets \D(p)$
					\If{$B$ is defined}
						\State $B \gets B \cup \{p\}$
					\Else \label{algo:expand:init_start}
						\State $B_p \gets \{p\}$
						\State $\B \gets \B \cup \{B_p\}$
						\State $\mathtt{UpdateTreeLeaf}(\D, p, B_p)$
						\State $\mathtt{Expand}(p)$ \label{algo:expand:init_end}
					\EndIf
				\EndIf
			\EndFor
		\end{algorithmic}
	\end{algorithm}

	By design, building a PR requires sorting the letters of $\Sigma$ into $\B$, hence Line $\ref{algo:expand:letters}$, despite elements of $\Sigma$ not being successors of $w$. However, we only insert them once, during the very first call.
	Expanding a pomset may result in a new class being created if the leaf $p$ was sifted into is unlabelled.
	Indeed, $\D$ initially consists of a root labelled by the identity context $\square$ and two children that have yet to be labelled by components of $\B$ due to $S \cup S^+$ being empty.
	Either leaf may even end up not being labelled at all if the PR is trivial (i.e. has language $\SP$ or $\emptyset$).
	Thus, if a pomset $p$ is sifted into an unlabelled leaf, Lines \ref{algo:expand:init_start} to \ref{algo:expand:init_end} result in a new class $B_p$ being created and $\D$ being updated by labelling said leaf with $B_p$.


	\subsection{Refining components}

	Algorithm \ref{algo:refine} refines a component $B$ into two new components $B_0$ and $B_1$, assuming a context~$c$ distinguishes two access sequences of $B$.
	$S$, $\B$ and $\D$ are updated accordingly.
	Lines $\ref{algo:refine:b0_no_as}$ and $\ref{algo:refine:b1_no_as}$ guarantee that the new components have at least one access sequence in $S$.
	Line \ref{algo:refine:tree} consists in replacing leaf $B$ of the discrimination tree $\D$ with an inner node labelled by $c$ whose left (resp. right) child is a new leaf labelled by $B_0$ (resp. $B_1$).
	\begin{algorithm}
		\caption{$\mathtt{Refine}(B, c)$ where $B \in \B$, $c \in \Co$, and $\exists z_1, z_2 \in B$, $\Mod(c[z_1]) \neq \Mod(c[z_2])$}
		\label{algo:refine}
		\begin{algorithmic}[1]
			\State $B_0 \gets \{w \in B \mid \Mod(c[w]) = 0\}$
			\State $B_1 \gets \{w \in B \mid \Mod(c[w]) = 1\}$
			\State $\B \gets (\B \setminus \{B\}) \cup \{B_0, B_1\}$
			\State $\mathtt{RefineTree}(\D, B, c, B_0, B_1)$ \label{algo:refine:tree}
			\If{$S \cap B_0 = \emptyset$}
				$\mathtt{Expand}(p_0)$ for some $p_0 \in B_0$ \label{algo:refine:b0_no_as}
			\EndIf
			\If{$S \cap B_1 = \emptyset$}
				$\mathtt{Expand}(p_1)$ for some $p_1 \in B_1$ \label{algo:refine:b1_no_as}
			\EndIf
		\end{algorithmic}	
	\end{algorithm}

	Algorithm \ref{algo:make_consistent} refines partition $\B$ whenever it encounters a consistency issue, e.g. class $B$ contains two representatives $p_1$ and $p_2$ such that $p_1 \circ p$ and $p_2 \circ p$ in $S \cup S^+$ do not belong to the same class.
	This inconsistency yields a context $c[\square \circ p]$ that distinguishes $p_1$ and $p_2$, where $c \in \C$ is the label of the deepest common ancestor in $\D$ of $p_1 \circ p$ and $p_2 \circ p$.
	This algorithm returns Boolean $\top$ if and only if $\B$ was already consistent in the first place.
	It could also be that $p \circ p_1$ and $p \circ p_2$ do not belong to the same class, resulting in a distinguishing context $c[p \circ \square]$: we omit this case here for brevity's sake.
	\begin{algorithm}[H]
		\caption{$\mathtt{MakeConsistent}()$}
		\label{algo:make_consistent}
		\begin{algorithmic}[1]
			\State $\mathtt{already\_consistent} \gets \top$
			\While{$\exists \circ \in \operators$, $\exists B \in \B$, $\exists p_1, p_2 \in \as_\B(B)$, $\exists p \in S$, $\D(p_1 \circ p) \neq \D(p_2 \circ p)$}
				\State Let $c \in \C$ be such that $\Mod(c[p_1 \circ p]) \neq \Mod(c[p_2 \circ p])$.
				\State $\mathtt{Refine}(B, c[\square \circ p])$ \label{algo:make_consistent:refine}
				\State $\mathtt{already\_consistent} \gets \bot$
			\EndWhile
			\State \Return $\mathtt{already\_consistent}$
		\end{algorithmic}	
	\end{algorithm}

	Algorithm \ref{algo:make_assoc} refines partition $\B$ whenever it encounters an associativity issue:
	if $(s_1 \circ s_2) \circ s_3$ and $s_l \circ s_3$ do not behave similarly, as witnessed by the deepest common ancestor $c \in \C$ in $\D$ of $s_1 \circ s_r$ and $s_l \circ s_3$, since $s_1 \circ s_2 \in S^+$ and $s_l \in S$ both belong to a same class $B$, the context $c[\square \circ s_3]$ refines $B$.
	A similar test is performed to detect right associativity issues.
	This algorithm returns Boolean $\top$ if and only if $\B$ was already associative in the first place.

	\begin{algorithm}
		\caption{$\mathtt{MakeAssoc}()$}
		\label{algo:make_assoc}
		\begin{algorithmic}[1]
			\State $\mathtt{already\_assoc} \gets \top$
			\While{$\exists \circ \in \operators$, $\exists s_1, s_2, s_3 \in S$, $\exists s_l \in \as_\B(s_1 \circ s_2)$, $\exists s_r \in \as_\B(s_2 \circ s_3$), $\D(s_1 \circ s_r) \neq \D(s_l \circ s_3)$}
				\State Let $c \in \C$ be such that $\Mod(c[s_1 \circ s_r]) \neq \Mod(c[s_l \circ s_3])$.
				\State Let $p \in \as_\B(s_1 \circ s_2 \circ s_3)$.
				\State $\mathtt{query} \gets \Mod(c[p])$
				\If{$\Mod(c[s_l \circ s_3]) \neq \mathtt{query}$}
					\State \texttt{Refine}$(\B_{s_1 \circ s_2}, c[\square \circ s_3])$ \label{algo:make_assoc:refine_1}
				\Else
					\State \texttt{Refine}$(\B_{s_2 \circ s_3}, c[s_1 \circ \square])$ \label{algo:make_assoc:refine_2}
				\EndIf
				\State $\mathtt{already\_assoc} \gets \bot$
			\EndWhile
			\State \Return $\mathtt{already\_assoc}$
		\end{algorithmic}
	\end{algorithm}


	\subsection{Using counter-examples to identify new components}

	Algorithm \ref{algo:find_ebp} is an important contribution as it differs from existing counter-example handling algorithms on finite words and pomsets.
	Its arguments are a context $c$ and a pomset $z$ such that $w = c[z]$ is a counter-example.
	It returns a context $c'$ and a pomset $p$ belonging to the frontier such that $c'$ distinguishes $p$ from all the existing access sequences of $\B_p$.

	\begin{algorithm}
		\caption{$\mathtt{FindEBP}(c, z)$ where $c \in \Co$, $z \in \SPplus$, $\Hyp(c[z]) \neq \Mod(c[z])$ and $\agree(c, z) = 1$}
		\label{algo:find_ebp}
		\begin{algorithmic}[1]
			\If{$z \in \Sigma$} 
				\Return $(c, z)$ \label{algo:find_ebp:base}
			\ElsIf{$z = z_1 \circ z_2$ is a canonical decomposition of $z$}
				\If{$\agree(c[\square \circ z_2], z_1)$} \label{algo:find_ebp:find_bp_start}
					\State \Return $\mathtt{FindEBP}(c[\square \circ z_2], z_1)$ \label{algo:find_ebp:find_bp_end}
				\Else				
					\State Let $p_1 \in \as_\Hyp(z_1)$ be such that $\Hyp(c[p_1 \circ z_2]) \neq \Mod(c[p_1 \circ z_2])$. \label{algo:find_ebp:check_ebp_start}
					\If{$\agree(c[p_1 \circ \square], z_2)$} \label{algo:find_ebp:check_ebp_end}
						\State \Return $\mathtt{FindEBP}(c[p_1 \circ \square], z_2)$ \label{algo:find_ebp:look_for_ebp}
					\Else
						\State Let $p_2 \in \as_\Hyp(z_2)$ be such that $\Hyp(c[p_1 \circ p_2]) \neq \Mod(c[p_1 \circ p_2])$. \label{algo:find_ebp:ebp_found_start}	
						\State \Return $(c, p_1 \circ p_2)$ \label{algo:find_ebp:ebp_found_end}
					\EndIf
				\EndIf
			\EndIf
		\end{algorithmic}
	\end{algorithm}

	\begin{itemize}
		\item Line \ref{algo:find_ebp:base} handles the base case: if $z$ is a letter, we can trivially infer a distinguishing context and a new representative.
		Property \ref{prop:exists_bp} guarantees that, by the time the algorithm reaches a leaf, it is indeed a breaking point.

		\item Lines \ref{algo:find_ebp:find_bp_start} to \ref{algo:find_ebp:find_bp_end} consist in inductively finding a breaking point along the leftmost branch of a canonical term $t$ of $w$.
		By Property \ref{prop:exists_bp}, such a breaking point always exists.
		Figure~\ref{fig:breaking_point} displays how the algorithm explores $t \in \ST{c[z]}$:
		if $\iota$ is the insertion point of $z$ in $t$, let $\mu$ and $\nu$ be the children of $\iota$.
		The exponent of each node stands for the local value of predicate $\agree(c^x, z^x)$ for $x \in \{\iota, \mu, \nu\}$, where $(c^x, z^x)$ stands for the split of $w$ induced by node $x$.
		Here, $(c, \circ, z_1, z_2)$ is a breaking point.
		If it is not the case, the algorithm inductively explores the (purple) leftmost sub-branch rooted in $\mu$ instead.

		\item Lines \ref{algo:find_ebp:check_ebp_start} to \ref{algo:find_ebp:check_ebp_end} determine whether a refinement can be inferred from this breaking point. Our intuition is that witnessing a conflict when the left branch is replaced by an access sequence $p_1$ is not enough; we need to check if feeding both branches to the hypothesis still result in a conflict.
		\begin{itemize}
			\item If it does (Lines \ref{algo:find_ebp:ebp_found_start} to \ref{algo:find_ebp:ebp_found_end}), then $c$ distinguishes $p_1 \circ p_2$ for some $p_1 \in \as_\Hyp(z_1)$, $p_2 \in \as_\Hyp(z_2)$ from any access sequence in $\as_\Hyp(p_1 \circ p_2)$.
			Then the algorithm returns $c$ and $p_1 \circ p_2$.

			\item Otherwise, the algorithm no longer explores the leftmost branch rooted in $\iota$.
			Line \ref{algo:find_ebp:look_for_ebp} instead replaces $z_1$ with an access sequence $p_1$ and restarts the exploration process from $\nu$, as shown by Figure \ref{fig:changing_ce}.
			The pre-condition is respected, as $c[p_1 \circ z_2]$ is still a counter-example due to $\agree(c_w^\mu, z_w^\mu) = 0$ by definition of breaking points.
		\end{itemize}
	\end{itemize}

	By Theorem \ref{th:find_ebp}, Algorithm \ref{algo:find_ebp} ends and does return a context $c'$ that distinguishes a representative $p$ of a new component from the access sequences of its previous component.
	Due to each inductive call descending deeper into the term, we can intuit that Algorithm~\ref{algo:find_ebp} performs at most $\bigo(\depth{z})$ inductive calls.

