\subsection{AMD GPU Hardware}
AMD GPUs consist of multiple Compute Units (CUs) \cite{amdhwbasics}, which are equivalent to Streaming Multiprocessors (SMs) in Nvidia terminology. Multiple CUs are organized into Shader Engines (SEs), typically in clusters of 15 CUs for the MI50 architecture. 
Unlike Nvidia's MPS or MIG, which applies spatial partitions at the process level, AMD's \textit{CU Masking} feature within the ROCm runtime enables spatial partitioning at a finer granularity of streams by specifying which CUs to execute kernels on. This provides more flexible resource allocations for inference servers. %CUs play an essential role in GPU resource partitioning since it is the smallest granularity we can control for co-located workloads. 


Additional architectural details on AMD GPUs can be found in technical whitepapers \cite{GCNarchitecture, Polarisarchitecture, CDNA2architecture} and academic papers \cite{Alternatives}. 
AMD's ROCm documentation \cite{ROCmDoc} and the HSA Foundation's Programmer Manual \cite{HSADoc} provide essential information about AMD's runtime backend. 

% In order for the GPU to schedule incoming kernels, AMD hardware employs Asynchronous Compute Engines (ACEs) \cite{amdhwbasics}, typically 4 ACEs per GPU~\cite{CDNA2architecture}. ACEs are responsible for receiving workloads from hardware queues and dispatching workgroups (WGs)\footnote{Also called thread blocks (TBs) in Nvidia terminology. WGs and TBs may be used interchangeably in this work.} to SEs, according to their resource allocation given by the CU masking mechanism. Then a Workload Manager (WLM) schedules a workgroup to a specific CU based on the CU mask. Figure~\ref{fig:queuebehavior} illustrates how hardware queues, ACEs, SEs, and CUs are organized in GPU hardware. 
%These ACEs compute queues and schedules each kernel's thread blocks. 

%The runtime prioritizes linking one HSA queue per hardware queue, but permits the oversubscription of these queues, i.e., multiple HAS queues linked to one hardware queue.\footnote{\cite{Oversub} explores the behaviors of oversubscribing AMD GPU queues, and demonstrates how different command queue scheduling techniques can yield performance benefits.} The hardware scheduler assigns HSA queues to hardware queues in a round-robin fashion, although this may change depending on some microcode within the GPU. 

\subsection{GPU Energy-efficiency Techniques}\label{sec:gpu_energy}
GPUs can save power through DVFS and resource scaling techniques. 
While DVFS can scale frequency and voltage to save dynamic power, its impact is limited by underutilization of idle resources which typically suffer from leakage power.
Resource scaling, such as limiting workload to a subset of CUs, can in theory save power by allowing unused resources to be power-gated to save leakage power. However, power gating techniques in production GPUs are limited to clock-gating or gating of the entire GPU~\cite{kandiah2021accelwattch,towardsenergy,cofris,abdel2013warped,jahanshahi2020gpu}. %\daniel{(Yidi's paper and cofris)}. 
Hence, CU masking alone does not lead to significant energy-efficiency gains. To address under-utilization, it is favorable to co-locate and increase the utilization of the GPU to improve energy efficiency. 

