Based on the observations made in Section~\ref{sec:hardware_characteristics}, we propose ECLIP, a framework for \textit{practical} and \textit{energy-efficient} co-location of inference kernels. ECLIP aims to trade off spatial repartitioning granularity with repartitioning overheads. 
ECLIP achieves effective kernel-granular spatial partitioning by (i) creating pools of streams with pre-defined CU masks, (ii) a runtime scheduler that intercepts and redirects kernels to appropriate CU-masked streams while maintaining inter-kernel data dependencies, and (iii) a resource allocation optimizer that assigns optimal CU assignments for \textit{groups of kernels} while considering co-location scenarios, and other hardware constraints. 
Figure~\ref{fig:overview} shows an overview of ECLIP, with require modifications to the ROCm runtime in blue boxes. 


