To implement this, our dependency enforcer maintains a two-dimensional vector tracking kernel execution status: one dimension for user streams and the other for kernel completion signals.\footnote{A completion signal is the runtime's way to determine whether a kernel has finished. If it is 0, it is still running. If it is 1, the old kernel was completed. We also use this same signal for profiling.} For each incoming kernel, the enforcer checks the completion signal of the previous kernel from the same stream. If that kernel is still running, the enforcer creates and dispatches a barrier packet dependent on its completion signal, followed by the new kernel (\circled{4}). Otherwise, the new kernel is dispatched immediately. In both cases, the new kernel's completion signal is logged for future reference.


