% Symbolic regression works by constructing programs in a language of mathematical expressions. Unlike linear regression, symbolic regression can represent many complex combinations of programs. However, symbolic regression is generally an intractable problem. Recently, PySR and derivatives have proven that such tools can accelerate scientific discovery in various areas. However, a core bottleneck of SR based techniques is that the only directed grounding an end user can provide to PySR is by modifying the DSL. Our main hypothesis is that using natural language as an intermediate grounding technique allows us to speed up synthesis of equations faster. We present a neurosymbolic algorithm that leverages a mixture of symbolic mutation/crossover functions and LLM based mutation/crossover functions, along with maintaining a concept library to summarize and promulgate high level concepts. Our method outperforms baselines in three tasks.


We present a novel method for symbolic regression (SR), the task of searching for compact programmatic hypotheses that best explain a dataset. The problem is commonly solved using genetic algorithms; we show that we can enhance such methods by inducing a library of abstract textual concepts. Our algorithm, called \name, 
uses zero-shot queries to a large language model (LLM) to discover and evolve abstract concepts occurring in known high-performing hypotheses. We discover new hypotheses using a mix of standard evolutionary steps and LLM-guided steps (obtained through zero-shot LLM queries) conditioned on discovered concepts. Once discovered,  hypotheses are used in a new round of concept abstraction and evolution. We validate \name on the Feynman equations, a popular SR benchmark, %standard SRBench suite of SR tasks 
as well as a set of synthetic tasks. On these benchmarks, \name substantially outperforms a variety of state-of-the-art SR approaches based on deep learning and evolutionary algorithms. Moreover, we show that \name can be used to discover a new and powerful scaling law for LLMs.
