\subsection{Comparison on Benchmarks}






To ensure a fair comparison, we reproduce six well-known time series forecasting models: Time-MoE \cite{shi2024time}, TimeMixer \cite{wang2024timemixer++}, TimeXer \cite{wang2024timexer}, TimesNet \cite{wu2023timesnettemporal2dvariationmodeling}, and AutoFormer \cite{wu2021autoformer}. These methods encompass both models employing a pre-training and fine-tuning architecture and networks that learn using frequency domain features.
% We reproduced those prominent models and evaluate on the datastet.
% Our approach demonstrates state-of-the-art results across the evaluated datasets, with the detailed metrics provided in Table \ref{mainresult}.

In our evaluation setup, prediction lengths varied among {12, 24, 48, 96, 192, 336, 720}, corresponding to context lengths of {64, 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048, 3072}. Notably, most existing studies focus on prediction lengths of 96 or larger, with corresponding context lengths of at least 256. We introduce additional evaluations with three shorter prediction lengths to provide a more comprehensive analysis. As illustrated in Figure \ref{main_result_vision}, except for the ETTm2 dataset, MoFE-Time outperforms its competitors. On average, MoFE-Time achieves MSE and MAE of \textbf{0.2755} and \textbf{0.3226}, reflecting reductions of \textbf{6.95\%} and \textbf{6.02\%} against the next best model, Time-MoE.

% We evaluated the performance of MoFE-Time on six public datasets and one proprietary dataset. To ensure a fair comparison, we selected and reproduced six mainstream time series forecasting models: Time-Moe\cite{shi2024time}, TimeMixer\cite{wang2024timemixer++}, TimeXer\cite{wang2024timexer}, TimesNet\cite{wu2023timesnettemporal2dvariationmodeling} and AutoFormer\cite{wu2021autoformer}. The results demonstrate that our model exhibits state-of-the-art performance on both public and proprietary datasets. As shown in Figure\ref{mainresult}, for the public datasets, we used various prediction lengths of \{12, 24, 48, 96, 192, 336, 720\}, corresponding to context lengths of \{64, 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048, 3072\}. So far as we known, most papers only conduct evaluations with prediction lengths equal or larger than 96 and corresponding to context lengths equal or larger than 256. For more comprehensive evaluation, we introduce 3 shorter prediction and context lengths. For the NEV-sales, we used various prediction lengths of \{ 8, 16, 24\}, corresponding to context lengths of \{56, 112, 180\}. The evaluation metrics adopt Mean Square Error(MSE) and Mean Absolute Error(MAE). The detailed results are presented in Tables \ref{mainresult}, \ref{main_result_vision} and Figure \ref{main_result_vision}.

% The evaluation results of MoFE-Time on the public datasets are presented in Table \ref{mainresult}. The results demonstrate that, except for  ETTm2, MoFE-Time exhibited \textbf{state-of-the-art} performance on the remaining five public datasets. On average across the six datasets, MoFE-Time achieved MSE and MAE values of \textbf{0.2755} and \textbf{0.3226}, respectively, representing about \textbf{6.95\%} and \textbf{6.02\%} reduction compared to the runner-up, Time-MoE. The evaluation results of MoFE-Time on the NEV-sales datasets are presented in Table \ref{Nevs-sales}. The results indicate that in real-world commercial applications, the MoFE-Time model also achieves optimal performance, with average MSE and MAE values reaching 0.1956 and 0.3284, respectively, across the entire dataset.

