We begin by analyzing the convergence behavior of the proposed controller for Parameter Set 1. With a conservatively small gain \(\alpha = 0.008\), the controller is expected to exhibit stable behavior, consistent with the theoretical guarantees of OF0. This is confirmed by the results shown in \autoref{fig:traj_008}, where stable convergence to all vertices of \(\mathcal{F}\) is observed. The performance of the controller near active constraints is influenced by the low gain, resulting in slower convergence rates. Additionally, differences in convergence speed are observed depending on the type of active constraint along the trajectories. For regions of $\mathcal{F}$ that are bounded by few individual constraints like the transformer limits at the PCC the convergence rate of the controller to $s_{\text{set}}$ is higher than for the upper region of $\mathcal{F}$ which is limited by the upper voltage band of the system. As numerous constraints become active the controller has to actuate more flexibility in order to ensure the feasibility of the resulting system states. This results in the slower convergence for the upper vertices of $\mathcal{F}$.

\begin{figure}[tb]
