In a multihead self-attention block, attention heads act independently and additively \parencite{elhage2021mathematical}, processing different aspects of the input in parallel (for example in the visual system: shape, color, motion, etc.).
The output of the block, the context-aware representations, can then be written as $\widetilde{\bm x}_i = \sum_h \bm W_O^h\widetilde{\bm v}_i^h$, with $\bm W_O^h$ of dimension $d_e \times d_v$.
In a thalamo-cortico-thalamic pathway, the sequence elements $\bm x_i$, originating from a thalamic nucleus, are processed in parallel by a subset of $H$ cortical areas, and their outputs are integrated in another thalamic nucleus to yield $\widetilde{\bm x}_i$, see \cref{fig:sketches}c.
In such a pathway, the thalamo-cortical projections are assigned the synaptic weights $\bm W_Q^h$, $\bm W_K^h$, and $\bm W_V^h$, while the cortico-thalamic projections are assigned the weights $\bm W_O^h$.
\Cref{alg:MHA} sums up the necessary computation with an exact implementation of multihead linear self-attention annotated with the mapping to cortico-thalamic circuits.
This general organization into `transcortical' pathways connecting two thalamic nuclei through a subset of cortical areas is consistent with the observation that single thalamic cells integrate information from different cortical areas \parencite{sampathkumar2021integration}, and that deep PT pyramidal cells avoid driving thalamic nuclei projecting to their cortical area \parencite{cassidy2025complementary}, see \cref{fig:exp}e.
Moreover, in our interpretation, query projections need to be dense, focused, and target cortical layers 3 and 4, matching the observed structure of core thalamo-cortical projections.
Key and value projections need to be sparse, diffuse, and target cortical layers 1 and 5a, matching the observed structure of matrix thalamo-cortical projections \parencite{jones1998core,sermet2019pathway}, see \cref{fig:exp}f and \cref{fig:bio}. 

\begin{algorithm}
    \caption{MultiHead linear Self-Attention (MHSA) in cortico-thalamic circuits.}
    \begin{algorithmic}[1]
    \Procedure{mhsa}{$\bm x_1, \dots, \bm x_n$} \Comment{MHSA input ($n$ tokens) --- Thalamus (e.g.~via L4)}
    \State $\widetilde{\bm x}_1, \dots, \widetilde{\bm x}_n = \text{zeros}(d_e), \dots, \text{zeros}(d_e)$  \Comment{ $d_e = \,$token embedding dim --- \#neurons per token}
    \State $\bm A = \text{ones}(d_v,d_k)$ \Comment{Local weights from L23 to Apical L5}
    \For{$h=1:H$} \Comment{For all heads / cortical areas}
    \For{$i=1:n$} \Comment{For all query / macrocolumn indices}
    \State $Z_i = 0\,,\,\widetilde{\bm v}_i = \text{zeros}(d_v)$ \Comment{ $d_v = \text{value dimension}$, typically, $d_v=d_e/H$}
    \For{$j=1:n$} \Comment{For all key / microcolumn indices} 
        \State $Z_i = Z_i + \bm 1 ^T(\phi(\bm W_K^h \bm x_j) \odot \phi(\bm W_Q^h \bm x_i))$ \Comment{Divisive normalization --- Interneurons L23}
    \EndFor \Comment{ End summing all L23 activities within macrocolumn }
    \For{$j=1:n$} \Comment{For all key / microcolumn indices}
      \State $\bm q_i = \bm W_Q^h \bm x_i$ \Comment{Query --- Basal L23   (in general: $\bm q_i^{h,j} = \bm W_Q^{h,ij} \bm x_i$)}
      \State $\bm k_j = \bm W_K^h \bm x_j$ \Comment{Key --- Apical L23  (in general: $\bm k_j^{h,i} = \bm W_K^{h,ij} \bm x_j$)}
      \State $\bm s_{ij} = \phi(\bm k_j) \odot \phi(\bm q_i) / Z_i$ \Comment{Salience --- Soma L23 ($\bm s_{ij} = \bm s_{ij}^{h}$)}
      \State $\bm v_j = \bm W_V^h \bm x_j$ \Comment{Value -- Basal L5IT  (in general: $\bm v_j^{h,i} = \bm W_V^{h,ij} \bm x_j$)}
      \State $\bm \alpha_{ij} = \bm A \bm s_{ij}$ \Comment{Attention / summed salience --- Apical L5IT}
      \State $\widetilde{\bm v}_{ij} = \bm \alpha_{ij} \odot \bm v_j$ \Comment{Attention-modulated value --- Soma L5IT (L5a)}
      \State $\widetilde{\bm v}_i = \widetilde{\bm v}_{i} + \widetilde{\bm v}_{ij}$ \Comment{Sum $\widetilde{\bm v}_{ij} $ across keys/values ($j$) = macrocolumn output --- L5PT (L5b)}
    \EndFor    \Comment{ End summing attention-modulated values within macrocolumn } 
    \State $\widetilde{\bm x}_i = \widetilde{\bm x}_i + \bm W_O^h \widetilde{\bm v}_i$ \Comment{Sum across queries ($i$) and heads ($h$) --- Higher-order Thalamus}
    \EndFor   \Comment{ End summing context-aware values across macrocolumns / queries } 
    \EndFor   \Comment{ End summing context-aware values across areas / heads } 
    \State \Return $\widetilde{\bm x}_1, \dots, \widetilde{\bm x}_n$  \Comment{Output of MHSA --- Higher-order Thalamus}
    \EndProcedure    \Comment{ End MHSA for the {\small\#}$h\,$ of cortical areas } 
    \end{algorithmic}
    \label{alg:MHA}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Formal gradients of multihead self-attention parameters}
\label{sect:formal}
After showing how neurons could be wired to realize the computation of a multihead self-attention block, a pressing question is the one of learning the synaptic weights $\bm W^h_{\{K,Q,V,O\}}$. 
Energy-based approaches offer a formalism to derive local learning rules in neuronal circuits by jointly minimizing a layerwise loss and a cost on the outputs (\cite{scellier2017equilibrium,senn2024neuronal,song2024inferring,ellenberger2024backpropagation}; see also \cite{hoover2023energy} for inference as energy minimization). We focus here on a single multihead self-attention block.
The energy is a sum of the square norm of the local errors $\bm e_i = \bm y_i-\widetilde{\bm x}_i$, where the i-th output token $\widetilde{\bm x}_i$ is compared to a target $\bm y_i$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:E}
  E = \sum_i \tfrac12\|\bm e_i\|^2 = \sum_i\tfrac12 \|\bm y_i-\widetilde{\bm x}_i\|^2 \;,
\end{equation}
with again $\widetilde{\bm x}_i = \sum_h \bm W_O^h\widetilde{\bm v}_i^h\,$, $\;\widetilde{\bm v}_i^h = \sum_j \kappa(\bm k_j, \bm q_i) \, \bm v_j\,$, $\;\kappa(\bm k_j, \bm q_i) = \phi(\bm k_j)^T\phi(\bm q_i)/Z_i\,$, and $Z_i=\sum_{j'} \phi(\bm k_{j'})^T\phi(\bm q_i)$.
When processing language, for instance, the target is given by the next token (`word') $\bm y_i = \bm x_i$ that is predicted based on the previous tokens $\bm x_{i'}$ for $i'<i$. When processing visual inputs, the target consists of the foveal input of the current saccade, that is predicted based on the foveal inputs of the previous saccades. 

Quantities of interest in the derivation of cost-minimizing synaptic learning rules are the negative energy gradients, along which the synaptic weights are changed,
\begin{align}
  \dot{\bm W }_O \propto -\partial_{\bm W_O} E &= \sum_i \bm e_i \widetilde{\bm v}_i^{T} \;, \label{eq:dW1}\\
  \dot{\bm W }_V \propto -\partial_{\bm W_V} E &= \sum_{i,j} \kappa(\bm k_j, \bm q_i)\bm \Delta_{ij}\;, \label{eq:dW2}\\
   \dot{\bm W }_Q \propto-\partial_{\bm W_Q} E &= \sum_{i,j} Z_i^{-1} [\bm \Gamma^{q}_{ijj}-\kappa(\bm k_j, \bm q_i)\sum_{j'}\bm \Gamma^{q}_{ijj'}]\;,
    \label{eq:dW3}\\
   \dot{\bm W }_K \propto-\partial_{\bm W_K} E &= \sum_{i,j} Z_i^{-1} [\bm \Gamma^{k}_{ijj}-\kappa(\bm k_j, \bm q_i)\sum_{j'}\bm \Gamma^{k}_{ijj'}]\;. 
   \label{eq:dW4}
\end{align}
Here, $\bm\Delta_{ij} = \bm W_O^{T}\bm e_i\bm x_j^T\,$, $\;\bm \Gamma^{q}_{ijj'} = [\phi'(\bm q_i) \odot \phi(\bm k_{j'})]\bm v^{T}_j\bm\Delta_{ii}\,$, $\;\bm \Gamma^{k}_{ijj'} = [\phi(\bm q_i) \odot \phi'(\bm k_{j'})]\bm v_j^{T} \bm\Delta_{ij'}$, and we omit the head index which is always $h$.
These are the formal gradient of a tokenwise mean squared error loss backpropagated through one multihead linear self-attention block. 
We check these results against numerical approximations based on finite differences and forward automatic differentiation (\nolinkurl{https://github.com/arnogranier/Formal-MHSA-gradient-numerics}).

