\section{Poset towers and resolutions} \label{sec:background}

In this section, we introduce the basic notions of poset towers, persistence modules, and projective resolutions. 


\textbf{Notation:} In the following, we denote by $P$ a finite partially ordered set (poset) with its points referred to as \emph{grades}. For $x,y\in P$, we use the notation $x\prec y$ if $x<y$ and there is no grade $z\in P$ such that $x<z<y$. We call such an $x$ a predecessor of $y$. We assume that $P$ is represented with a directed graph, its nodes being the grades and edges being the
relations $x\prec y$ in the Hasse diagram.

We also view $P$ as a category with objects the elements of $P$ and a unique morphism $x\rightarrow y$ if $x\leq y$. 
For a functor $F\colon P\rightarrow\mathbf{C}$, we denote by $F(x)$ the image of $x\in P$ under $F$ and by $F(x\leq y)$ the image of $x\rightarrow y$ under $F$ for $x\leq y\in P$. Let the category of functors $P\rightarrow\mathbf{C}$ and their natural transformations be denoted by $\mathbf{C}^P$. We will use the notation $\mathbf{Vec}$ for the category of vector spaces over $\mathbb{Z}_2$ and $\mathbf{SCpx}$ for the category of simplicial complexes and simplicial maps.

\subsection{Poset Towers}

Generalizing the notion of simplicial tower~\cite{dey2014computing,kerber2019barcodes}, we introduce poset towers: 

\begin{definition}[Poset tower] \label{def:poset_tower}
A poset tower is a functor $K\colon P\rightarrow \mathbf{SCpx}$; see, e.g., Figure~\ref{fig:posettower}.
\end{definition}

\noindent
We denote by $K_\ell(x)$ the set of all $\ell$-simplices in $K(x)$.
In most applications, it is not economical to store a poset tower as a simplicial complex for every node of the poset $P$. For example, in the case of multi-filtered simplicial complexes, represented in our setting by a poset tower over a finite grid with inclusion maps, we only need a list of birth grades for every simplex in the complex over the maximum grade. We generalize this compact representation to general poset towers by introducing simplex generators and edge events.

\begin{definition}[Simplex generators] \label{def:simplex_generators}
Let $K\colon P\rightarrow \mathbf{SCpx}$ be a poset tower. We call $g^x_\sigma$ a generator of the simplex $\sigma$ at $x\in P$, if $\sigma \in K(x)$ and $\sigma\notin \text{im }K(y\prec x)$ for all $y\prec x\in P$. We denote by $\mathcal{S}$ the set of all simplex generators in $K$. Formally:
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{S}\coloneqq\Big\{g^x_\sigma\vert x\in P\text{,}\sigma\in K(x)\setminus\bigcup_{y\prec x}\text{im }K(y\prec x)\Big\}.
\end{equation*}
\end{definition}

\noindent
Note that, for the special case of a multi-filtered simplicial complex $K$, the birth grades of a simplex $\sigma\in K(max)$, in the complex at the maximum, are in one-to-one correspondence with the simplex generators $g^x_{\sigma}$. An edge event over an edge $x\prec y$ is a vertex in $K(x)$ that is mapped to a vertex different from itself along the edge. These edge events account for possible collapses and permutations that can happen with arbitrary simplicial maps which are simulated by vertex identifications. The collapse $v\mapsto w$ on poset edge $x_4x_5$ in Figure~\ref{fig:posettower} is an edge event.

\begin{definition}[Edge events] \label{def:edge_events}
Let $K\colon P\rightarrow \mathbf{SCpx}$ be a poset tower. We call $c_{v\mapsto w}^{x\prec y}$ an edge event if $K(x\prec y)(v)=w\neq v$ for $x\prec y\in P$ and $v\in K_0(x)$. Let $\mathcal{C}$ be the set of edge events.
\end{definition}

\noindent
Instead of recording all simplicial maps, we just record when they change a simplex. A multi-filtered simplicial complex, for example, has no edge events. The simplex generators and edge events completely determine the poset tower.

\begin{proposition} \label{prop:tower_from_generators_events}
A poset tower $K\colon P\rightarrow \mathbf{SCpx}$ is completely described by the list of simplex generators $\mathcal{S}$ and edge events $\mathcal{C}$.   
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $\mathcal{S}$ and $\mathcal{C}$ be as in Definitions \ref{def:simplex_generators} and \ref{def:edge_events}. We extend $P$ to a linear order $P_{lin}=\{x_1,\ldots,x_n\}$ and construct $K'$ recursively: For $i=1,\ldots, n$: Define $K'(x_i)\coloneqq \{\sigma\vert \exists g_\sigma^{x_i}\in\mathcal{S}\}\cup\bigcup_{y\prec x_i}\text{im }K'(y\prec x_i)$. For all $v\in K'_0(x_i)$ and $x_i\prec y$, define $K'(x_i\prec y)(v)\coloneqq w$ if there exists a $c_{v\mapsto w}^{x_i\prec y}\in \mathcal{C}$ and $K'(x_i\prec y)(v)\coloneqq v$ otherwise. 

We now show $K=K'$: Suppose $K(x_i)=K'(x_i)$, $v\in K'_0(x_i)=K_0(x_i)$ and $x_i\prec y$. If $K(x_i\prec y)(v)=v$, there is no edge event $c_{v\mapsto w}^{x_i\prec y}$  in $\mathcal{C}$ and, thus, $K'(x_i\prec y)(v)=v$. If $K(x_i\prec y)(v)=w\neq v$, there is an edge event $c_{v\mapsto w}^{x_i\prec y}\in \mathcal{C}$ and, thus, $K'(x_i\prec y)(v)=w$. Since the images of the vertices completely determine a simplicial map, we get $K(x_i\prec y)=K'(x_i\prec y)$.

The first element $x_1$ must be minimal in $P$. Hence, by definition, for all $\sigma\in K(x_1)$ we have $g^{x_1}_\sigma\in\mathcal{S}$. Therefore, $K(x_1)=K'(x_1)$. Suppose $K(x_j)=K'(x_j)$ for all $j<i$. Every predecessor $y\prec x_i$ satisfies $y=x_j$ for some $j<i$ in the linear extension. Hence, by assumption, we have $K(x_i)=\{\sigma\vert \exists g_\sigma^{x_i}\in\mathcal{S}\}\cup\bigcup_{y\prec x_i}\text{im }K(y\prec x_i)=\{\sigma\vert \exists g_\sigma^{x_i}\in\mathcal{S}\}\cup\bigcup_{y\prec x_i}\text{im }K'(y\prec x_i)=K'(x_i)$. 
\end{proof}

\subsection{Projective modules and resolutions}
\noindent
We now define the main algebraic objects, the persistence modules, and discuss how we can work with them in a computational framework.

\begin{definition}[$P$-Persistence module] \label{def:persistence_module}
 A $P$-persistence module is a functor $M\colon P\rightarrow \mathbf{Vec}$.
\end{definition}

\noindent
Persistence modules naturally arise as the homology of poset towers. Homology, with coefficients in $\mathbb{Z}_2$, can be viewed as a functor $H_\ell\colon\mathbf{SCpx}\rightarrow \mathbf{Vec}$ for every $\ell\geq 0$. If we take the composition of the homology functor with a poset tower $K\colon P\rightarrow\mathbf{SCpx}$, we obtain the persistence module $H_\ell(K)\coloneqq H_\ell\circ K\colon P\rightarrow\mathbf{Vec}$. The homology of a simplicial complex is computed from the simplicial chain complex which can be viewed as an algebraic representation of the complex. For $x\leq y\in P$ we denote by $C_\bullet\big(K(x)\big)$ the simplicial chain complex and by $C_\bullet\big(K(x\leq y)\big)$ the morphism induced by the simplicial map $K(x\leq y)$. The vector space $C_\ell\big(K(x)\big)$ has a basis consisting of the $\ell$-simplices in $K(x)$ and $C_\ell\big(K(x\leq y)\big)$ sends $\sigma\in K_\ell(x)$ to $K(x\leq y)(\sigma)$ if $K(x\leq y)(\sigma)\in K_\ell(y)$ and to zero otherwise:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=large, column sep=large]
\cdots & C_{\ell\minus 1}\big(K(x)\big) \arrow[l] \arrow[d,"C_{\ell\minus 1}\big(K(x\leq y)\big)"] & C_\ell\big(K(x)\big) \arrow[l,swap,"\partial_{\ell}"] \arrow[d,"C_{\ell}\big(K(x\leq y)\big)"] & C_{\ell+1}\big(K(x)\big) \arrow[l,swap,"\partial_{\ell+1}"] \arrow[d,"C_{\ell+1}\big(K(x\leq y)\big)"] & \cdots \arrow[l] \\
\cdots & C_{\ell\minus 1}\big(K(y)\big) \arrow[l] & C_\ell\big(K(y)\big) \arrow[l,swap,"\partial_{\ell}"] & C_{\ell+1}\big(K(y)\big) \arrow[l,swap,"\partial_{\ell+1}"] & \cdots \arrow[l] 
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}

\noindent
Let $\mathbf{Ch}\big(\mathbf{Vec}\big)$ and $\mathbf{Ch}\big(\mathbf{Vec}^P\big)$ denote the category of chain complexes over vector spaces and $P$-persistence modules, respectively. We can view $C_\bullet(K)\colon P\rightarrow \mathbf{Ch}(\mathbf{Vec})$ as a persistent chain complex or, equivalently, as a chain complex of persistence modules in $\mathbf{Ch}\big(\mathbf{Vec}^P\big)$:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}
\cdots & C_{\ell\minus 1}(K) \arrow[l] & C_\ell(K) \arrow[l,swap,"\partial_{\ell}"] & C_{\ell+1}(K) \arrow[l,swap,"\partial_{\ell+1}"] & \cdots \arrow[l]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
where each $C_\ell(K)\colon P\rightarrow\mathbf{Vec}$ is a persistence module.  

\cancel{
\begin{remark}
We note that the category of $P$-persistence modules $\mathbf{Vec}^P$ is equivalent to the category $\mathbf{Mod}_{\field P}$ of modules over the so-called incidence algebra $\field P$ of the poset $P$. This equivalence justifies treating persistence modules as actual modules. For example, we can speak of elements $x\in M$ and submodules $S\subseteq M$.  
\end{remark}
}

To carry out computations with persistence modules, we need to represent them with matrices. As in poset towers, it is not economical to represent a persistence module by a collection of matrices $M(x\leq y)$ for all $x\leq y\in P$. Instead, we represent persistence modules by elementary building blocks called projective modules (generalizing \emph{free} modules). 

\begin{definition}[Projective modules] \label{def:elementary_module}
We denote by $\text{Proj}[x]\colon P\rightarrow\mathbf{Vec}$ the elementary projective module at $x\in P$ defined, for all $y\leq z\in P$, by
\begin{equation*}
\text{Proj}[x](y)=\begin{cases} \mathbb{Z}_2 &\text{if}~ x\leq y \\ 0 & \text{else} \end{cases} \quad , \qquad \text{Proj}[x](y\leq z)=\begin{cases} \text{id} &\text{if}~x\leq y\leq z \\ 0 & else \end{cases}
\end{equation*}   
A direct sum $\bigoplus_{i=1}^n\text{Proj}[x_i]$ is called a projective module ; see Figure \ref{fig:projective} for an example.
\end{definition}


\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth]{Figure/Projective.pdf}}
\caption{An illustration of a projective module with three elementary projective summands; $\text{Proj}[x_0]$ shaded green, $\text{Proj}[x_1]$ shaded yellow, and  $\text{Proj}[x_2]$ shaded violet.}
\label{fig:projective}
\end{figure}

\noindent
Projective modules are well-suited for computations because morphisms between them are simple and can be described by $P$-graded matrices.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:elementary_projective}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item For $x,y\in P$:
    \begin{equation*}
    \text{Hom}_{\mathbb{Z}_2}\big(\text{Proj}[x],\text{Proj}[y]\big)\cong\begin{cases}\mathbb{Z}_2 & \text{ if } y\leq x \\ 0 & \text{ else} \end{cases}
    \end{equation*}
    \item For $Q$ and $W$ projective modules:
    \begin{equation*}
    \begin{aligned}
    \text{Hom}_{\mathbb{Z}_2}\big(Q,W\big)&\cong \text{Hom}_{\mathbb{Z}_2}\Big(\bigoplus_{i=1}^m\text{Proj}[x_i],\bigoplus_{j=1}^n\text{Proj}[y_j]\Big)\\&\cong \bigoplus_{j=1}^n \bigoplus_{i=1}^m \text{Hom}_{\mathbb{Z}_2}\big(\text{Proj}[x_i],\text{Proj}[y_j]\big)\\&\cong \big\{(\lambda_{ji})_{j,i}\in\mathbb{Z}_2^{n\times m}\vert \lambda_{ji}=0\text{ if }y_j\nleq x_i\big\} .
    \end{aligned}
    \end{equation*}
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\noindent
Let $f\colon Q\rightarrow W$ be a morphism of projective modules. By Proposition \ref{prop:elementary_projective}, $f$ is completely determined by a matrix $(f_{ji})_{i=1,j=1}^{m,n}\in\mathbb{Z}_2^{n\times m}$ whose columns and rows are labeled with the grades of the elementary projective modules in $Q$ and $W$. Every persistence module and also every morphism between persistence modules can be expressed by projective modules and morphisms between them. 

\begin{definition}[Projective resolution] \label{def:projective_resolution}
Let $\phi\colon M\rightarrow N$ be a morphism of $P$-persistence modules. A projective resolution of $M$ is a chain complex, as in the upper row of \eqref{eq:projres_def},
\begin{equation}\label{eq:projres_def}
\begin{tikzcd}
0 & M \arrow[l,swap,"q_{\minus 1}"] \arrow[d,swap,"\phi"] & Q_0 \arrow[l,swap,"q_0"] \arrow[d,"f_0"] & Q_1 \arrow[l,swap,"q_1"] \arrow[d,"f_1"] & Q_2 \arrow[l,swap,"q_2"] \arrow[d,"f_2"] & \cdots \arrow[l,swap,"q_3"] \\
0 & N \arrow[l,swap,"w_{\minus 1}"] & W_0 \arrow[l,swap,"w_0"] & W_1 \arrow[l,swap,"w_1"] & W_2 \arrow[l,swap,"w_2"] & \cdots \arrow[l,swap,"w_3"]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
where $Q_i$ is a projective module and $\text{ker }q_{i\minus 1}=\text{im }q_i$ for all $i\geq 0$. We call the restriction $0\leftarrow M\xleftarrow{q_0} Q_0\xleftarrow{q_1} Q_1$ of a projective resolution a (projective) presentation of $M$ (determined essentially by $q_1$). If $W_\bullet$ is a projective resolution of $N$, we call a family of morphisms $f_\bullet\colon Q_\bullet\rightarrow W_\bullet$, as in \eqref{eq:projres_def}, a lift of $\phi$ to the projective resolutions, if all squares commute.
\end{definition}

\noindent
Since we only consider persistence modules $M\colon P\rightarrow\mathbf{Vec}$ over finite posets valued in finite-dimensional vector spaces, every persistence module has a projective resolution of finite length consisting of finite sums of elementary projective modules. Moreover, every morphism of persistence modules $\phi\colon M\rightarrow N$ can be lifted to projective resolutions of $M$ and $N$.

\cancel{
\begin{proposition} \label{def:existence_lift}
If $\phi\colon M\rightarrow N$ is a morphism of persistence modules, then a lift of $\phi$ to projective resolutions of $M$ and $N$ is unique up to homotopy. This means if $f_\bullet,g_\bullet\colon Q_\bullet\rightarrow W_\bullet$ are lifts of $\phi$:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}
0 & M \arrow[l,swap,"q_{\minus 1}"] \arrow[d,swap,"\phi"] & Q_0 \arrow[l,swap,"q_0"] \arrow[d,xshift=2pt,"f_0"] \arrow[d,swap,xshift=-2pt,"g_0"] \arrow[dr,dashed,"h_0"] & Q_1 \arrow[l,swap,"q_1"] \arrow[d,xshift=2pt,"f_1"] \arrow[d,swap,xshift=-2pt,"g_1"] \arrow[dr,dashed,"h_1"] & Q_2 \arrow[l,swap,"q_2"] \arrow[d,xshift=2pt,"f_2"] \arrow[d,swap,xshift=-2pt,"g_2"] \arrow[dr,dashed,"h_2"] & \cdots \arrow[l,swap,"q_3"] \\
0 & N \arrow[l,swap,"w_{\minus 1}"] & W_0 \arrow[l,swap,"w_0"] & W_1 \arrow[l,swap,"w_1"] & W_2 \arrow[l,swap,"w_2"] & \cdots \arrow[l,swap,"w_3"]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
then there exists a chain homotopy $h_\bullet\colon Q_\bullet\rightarrow W_\bullet[-1]$ such that $f_i-g_i=h_{i\minus 1}\circ q_i+w_{i+1}\circ h_i$ for all $i\geq 1$ and $f_0-g_0=w_1\circ h_0$.
\end{proposition}
}

\noindent
Since presentations and resolutions are not unique up to isomorphism, they can contain superfluous information. For computational efficiency, we want resolutions that are as small as possible. 

\begin{definition}\label{def:minimal_resolution}
A projective resolution $M\leftarrow Q_\bullet$, as in \ref{def:projective_resolution}, is called minimal if $Q_i$ minimizes the number of elementary projective summands for all $i\geq 0$. A presentation is called minimal if it is the restriction of a minimal projective resolution.
\end{definition}


\section{Computing presentations and projective resolutions of poset towers}\label{sec:algorithm}

In this section, we describe an algorithm to compute projective resolutions, as depicted in the rows of \eqref{eq:chaincomplex2}, of the chain complex of persistence modules $C_\bullet(K)\in\mathbf{Ch}\big(\mathbf{Vec}^P\big)$, depicted in the leftmost column, arising from a poset tower $K\colon P\rightarrow \mathbf{SCpx}$. 
%Given a finite poset $P$ by its Hasse diagram and a poset tower $K\colon P\rightarrow \mathbf{SCpx}$, compute a projective resolution (red) of the chain complex of persistence modules $C_\bullet(K)\in \mathbf{Ch}\big(\mathbf{Vec}^P\big)$ (blue):
\begin{equation} \label{eq:chaincomplex2}
\begin{tikzcd}
\vdots \arrow[d] & \vdots \arrow[d]  \\
C_{\ell+1}(K) \arrow[d,swap,"\partial_{\ell+1}"] & G_{\ell+1} \arrow[l,swap,"\alpha_{\ell+1}"] \arrow[d,"f_{\ell+1}"] & R_{\ell+1} \arrow[l,swap,"p_{\ell+1}^1"] & RR_{\ell+1} \arrow[l,swap,"p_{\ell+1}^2"] \\
C_{\ell}(K) \arrow[d,swap,"\partial_{\ell}"] & G_\ell \arrow[l,swap,"\alpha_{\ell}"] \arrow[d,"f_\ell"] & R_\ell \arrow[l,swap,"p_\ell^1"] & RR_\ell \arrow[l,swap,"p_\ell^2"] \\
C_{\ell\minus 1}(K) \arrow[d] & G_{\ell\minus 1} \arrow[l,swap,"\alpha_{\ell\minus 1}"] \arrow[d] & R_{\ell\minus 1} \arrow[l,swap,"p_{\ell\minus 1}^1"] & RR_{\ell\minus 1} \arrow[l,swap,"p_{\ell\minus 1}^2"] \\
\vdots & \vdots 
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}

\noindent
\textbf{Input:} A poset tower $K\colon P\rightarrow \mathbf{SCpx}$ represented by:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The poset $P$ as a directed (transitively reduced) graph (Hasse diagram). 
    \item A list 
    $\mathcal{S}=\{g_{\sigma_1}^{x_1},\ldots,g_{\sigma_{n'}}^{x_{n'}}\}$ of simplex generators (${\mathcal S}_\ell$ denoting restrictions to $\ell$-simplices).
    \item A list $\mathcal{C}=\{c^{y_1\prec z_1}_{v_{i_1}\mapsto v_{j_1}},\ldots, c^{y_{n''}\prec z_{n''}}_{v_{i_{n''}}\mapsto v_{j_{n''}}}\}$ of edge events. 
\end{itemize}


\noindent
We first observe that the set of simplex generators $\mathcal{S}_\ell$ gives rise to minimal set of (projective) generators of $C_\ell(K)$ defined by: $G_\ell\coloneqq\bigoplus_{g_\sigma^x\in\mathcal{S}_\ell}\text{Proj}[x]$. This means that with the right choice of $\alpha_\ell$, as discussed in detail in Section \ref{sec:p1correctness}, $\alpha_\ell\colon G_\ell\rightarrow C_\ell(K)$ defines the first step of a minimal resolution. Given this set of generators, we only have to figure out how these generators are related. In a poset tower there are three kinds of relations:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Two generators $g_\sigma^x$ and $g_\sigma^y$ of the same simplex $\sigma$ born at different grades can join in a grade $b>x,y$ where they must be identified by a relation $r^b\mapsto g_\sigma^x+g_\sigma^y$.
    \item An $\ell$-simplex $\sigma\in K(a)$ represented by a generator $g_\sigma^x$ can be collapsed into an $\ell$-simplex $\tau\in K(b)$ represented by a generator $g_\tau^y$ via a simplicial map such that $K(a<b)(\sigma)=\tau$. In this case, they also have to be identified by a relation $r^b\mapsto g_\sigma^x+g_\tau^y$.
    \item An $\ell$-simplex $\sigma\in K(a)$ represented by a generator $g_\sigma^x$ can be collapsed into a simplex $\tau\in K(b)$ of dimension $<\ell$ via a simplicial map such that $K(a<b)(\sigma)=\tau$. In this case, the generator has to be killed by a relation $r^b\mapsto g_\sigma^x$.
\end{enumerate}
The complexity of finding these relations arises because they depend on each other. Two generators $g_\sigma^x$ and $g_\sigma^y$ should not be identified in a common join if one of them is already killed by a relation at this point. In this case, they are different simplices. But we can also not just treat them as different simplices because they might have to be identified in some other join. Moreover, many generators can meet multiple times at multiple points along the poset tower. Therefore, we need an efficient method to keep track of which generators have to be related, which simplices are already related, and which simplices are already dead. Our key observation is that we can model the relations by a $P$-filtered graph. The generators in a poset tower always get related in pairs (Case 1 and 2) except in the case of simplices getting totally collapsed (Case 3). But we can model the third case by a generator that gets related to a distinguished ``graveyard'' vertex $\Omega$. Together with this distinct vertex $\Omega$ we obtain a $P$-filtered graph $\mathcal{G}\colon P\rightarrow\mathbf{SCpx}$ (Definition \ref{def:boundary_p}) whose vertices are the generators $g_\sigma^x\in\mathcal{S}$, born at grade $x$, and whose edges are the relations $r^b\mapsto g_\sigma^x+g_\tau^y$ or $r^b\mapsto g_\sigma^x+\Omega$, born at grade $b$. The following theorem will be a direct consequence of the proof of correctness, in Section \ref{sec:correctness}, of the upcoming algorithm.
\begin{theorem} \label{thm:graph_presentation_matrix}
Let $K\colon P\rightarrow \mathbf{SCpx}$ be a poset tower. Then, there exists a $P$-filtered graph $\mathcal{G}$ with boundary matrix $\overline{p}^1_\ell$ such that the matrix $p^1_\ell$, obtained from $\overline{p}^1_\ell$ by removing the row corresponding to distinguished vertex $\Omega$, is a minimal presentation of $C_\ell(K)$.
\end{theorem}
At every point $x\in P$ the connected component of $\Omega$ in $\mathcal{G}(x)$ represents the simplices that already died. Each remaining connected component represents a simplex in $K(x)$. Moreover, the cycles in $\mathcal{G}$ represent the kernel of the presentation matrix $p^1_\ell$ which corresponds to the relations of the relations. In the following algorithm, we successively build the graph $\mathcal{G}$ by adding generators and relations between them in the form of vertices and edges. If a new relation is added that forms a cycle in $\mathcal{G}(x)$, then this new relation is a linear combination of other existing relations, which should not be added to the minimal presentation. Nevertheless, a cycle that is generated by the inclusion of edges from predecessors implies a relation of relations.    

\vspace{0.1in}
\noindent
\textbf{Matrices.}
Our algorithm computes a presentation matrix $p^1_\ell$ and also has the option
to generate another matrix $p^2_\ell$ presenting relations of relations.
The row and column indices of both of these matrices are associated
with various values and are indexed accordingly.

The rows of the presentation matrix $p_\ell^1$ 
are associated to generators $g_\sigma^{x}$, i.e., we maintain a bijective map $I\rightarrow \{g_\sigma^{x}\}$ for the index set $I$ for rows. Similarly, the columns of $p_\ell^1$ are indexed by a graded identifier. A global counter denoted $\rsf$ generates
this identifier $\rsf^x$ if the column is added at the grade $x$. This means that
we maintain a bijective map $J\rightarrow \{\rsf^x\}$ for the column index set $J$ of
$p_\ell^1$. Therefore, we write $p_\ell^1[g_\sigma^x,\rsf^y]$ for $p_\ell^1[i,j]$
if $i\mapsto g_\sigma^x$ and $j\mapsto \rsf^y$. 

The rows of the matrix $p^2_\ell$ get their identifiers from the columns of
$p^1_\ell$. Similar to $p^1_\ell$, its columns are indexed by a graded global counter
$\rrsf$. A column gets the identifier $\rrsf^x$ if the column is generated at grade
$x$. We write $p^2_\ell[\rsf^x,\rrsf^y]$ in place of $p^2_\ell[i,j]$ if
$i\mapsto \rsf^x$ and $j\mapsto \rrsf^y$.

Our algorithm also computes the matrices $f_\ell$ that represent the boundary
maps between chain modules in consecutive dimensions. These are the matrices
$f_\ell$ annotating the vertical arrows in \eqref{eq:chaincomplex2}.
The rows and columns are indexed with graded simplices $g_\sigma^x$ just like the
rows of $p^1_\ell$.


\vspace{0.1in}
The main routine {\sc Presentation} computes a linear extension $x_0,\ldots, x_{t_0}$
of the poset $P$. Then, it iterates over each grade $x_i$ maintaining the invariant
that, for every degree $\ell\geq 0$, it has computed the matrices $G_\ell\stackrel{p_\ell^1}{\leftarrow} R_\ell$ of relations and $R_\ell \stackrel{p_\ell^2}{\leftarrow} RR_\ell$ of relations of relations for the module $C_\ell(K)$ restricted to the subposet induced by grades $x_0,\ldots, x_i$.
It maintains a set $L_{act}^{x_i}$ of active generators and a forest $\F_{act}^{x_i}$
of active relations. The set $L_{act}^{x_i}$ helps to determine
the relations $R_\ell$ (columns of $p_\ell^1$) that may arise due to
vertex collapses handled by the subroutine {\sc Collapse} or by
identification of generators handled by the subroutine {\sc Generator}.
The forest $\F_{act}^{x_i}$ initialized with a special vertex $\Omega$ helps to determine superfluous relations
in $R_\ell$ and thus guaranteeing minimality of $p_\ell^1$ and also
to determine (optionally) relations of relations $RR_\ell$ registered in the matrix $p_\ell^2$ by the subroutine {\sc RelRel}. The special vertex $\Omega$ 
plays the role of a ``graveyard'' vertex, which gets attached to any edge in the
relation forest generated by a `vanishing' simplex due to collapse.
Another matrix $f_\ell$ maintains boundaries. The algorithms to follow
handle all degrees $\ell\geq 0$ together and thus maintain single matrices $p^1$, $p^2$, $f$ accounting for all degrees.

\vspace{0.1in}
\noindent
{\bf Algorithm {\sc Presentation}} ($P$, $\mathcal C$, $\mathcal S$)
\begin{itemize}
    \item Compute a linear extension $x_0,\ldots,x_{t_0}$ of $P$.
    \item Initialize empty matrices $p^1$, $p^2$, and $f$; Initialize $L_{act}^{x_0}:=\emptyset$ and counters $\rsf=0$, $\rrsf=0$;
    \item For $i:=0$ to $t_0$ do 
    \begin{enumerate}
%        \item $\G_{act}^{x_i}=\emptyset$
        \item Call {\sc Collapse}($P$, $\mathcal C$, $x_i$)
        \item Call {\sc Generator}($P$, $\mathcal S$, $x_i$)
        \item (Optional) Call {\sc RelRel}($x_i$)
    \end{enumerate}
\end{itemize}

In routine {\sc Collapse}, over every poset edge $y\prec x$, we handle collapsing a vertex $v$ to a vertex $w$. In step 1, we compute the union of forests
$\cup_{y\prec x}\F_{act}^{y}$ which results in a graph $\G_{act}^{x}$
that may not be a forest. This graph (relation graph) is used and updated 
in the successive steps and routines (Figure~\ref{fig:relgraph}). In step 2(a), every simplex $\sigma\in K(y)$ 
gets its vertices updated either remaining the same ($v\mapsto v$)
or renamed to $w$ ($v\mapsto w$); e.g. in Figure~\ref{fig:posettower}, the edges
$uv, uw$ and $vw$ at $x_4$ become $uw, uw$ and $ww$ at $x_5$ respectively.

If a simplex $\sigma$ in $K(y)$ after vertex renaming contains none or
only one of $v$ and $w$: it is included in $L_{act}^{x}$ with appropriate generator (step 2(b.i));
e.g. $uv$ from $x_4$ becoming $uw$ at $x_5$ is added to $L_{act}^{x_5}$ with generator $g_{uv}^{x_1}$. Similarly, the edge $uw$ from $x_4$ remaining $uw$ at $x_5$ is added
with the generator $g_{uw}^{x_2}$. Now, $L_{act}^{x_5}$ has two copies of
$uw$ with two different generators. They are identified by the routine {\sc Generator}.

Otherwise (step 2(b.ii)): the vanishing simplex $\sigma$ like the edge $vw$ becoming $ww$ is not included in $L_{act}^{x}$ and instead a relation is generated for $p^1$ unless
addition of this relation to the graph $\G_{act}^{x_i}$ generates a cycle; also in preparation
a row is added to $p^2$. For the vanishing edge $vw$, we add the edge $e^{x_5}_{(\Omega,g_{vw}^{x_3})}$ to the graph $\G^{x_5}_{act}$
which does not create any cycle (Figure~\ref{fig:relgraph} (bottom right)). Therefore, we create
a column indexed by $\rsf^{x_5}$ for the relation $r_1^{x_5}\mapsto g_{vw}^{x_3}$ setting
$p^1[g_{vw}^{x_3},\rsf^{x_5}]=1$.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{Figure/RelationGraph2.pdf}}
\caption{(Figure~\ref{fig:posettower} example) Relation graphs $\G^{x_i}_{act}$ for vertices on left and for edges on right.}
\label{fig:relgraph}
\end{figure}

\vspace{0.1in}
\noindent
{\bf Algorithm {\sc Collapse}} ($P$, $\mathcal C$, $x$)
\begin{enumerate}
\item  If $x\not=x_0$, compute $\G_{act}^{x}:= \cup_{y\prec x} \F_{act}^y$ else initialize $\G_{act}^{x_0}$ with vertex $\Omega$
\item For all $y\prec x$:
        \begin{enumerate}
            \item Compute $K(y\prec x)$ as $v\mapsto w$ if $c_{v\mapsto w}^{y\prec x}\in \mathcal{C}$ and $v\mapsto v$ else 
            \item For all $(\sigma, g_\rho^z)\in L^y_{act}$:
            \begin{enumerate}
                \item If $K(y\prec x)(\sigma)$ has no repeated vertices, \\ add  $\big(K(y\prec x)(\sigma),g_\rho^z\big)$ to $L_{act}^{x}$.
                \item Otherwise, if adding $e^{x}_{(\Omega,g_\rho^{z})}$ to $\G_{act}^{x}$ does not make a cycle
                 \begin{itemize}
                    \item $E(\G_{act}^{x})$:= $E(\G_{act}^{x})\cup  \{e^{x}_{(\Omega,g_\rho^{z})}\}$ \,\, **$E(\cdot)$ denotes edge set**
                    \item add a column with index $\rsf^x$ in $p^1$ with $p^1[\rho^{z},\rsf^{x}]=1$ and as a row in $p^2$; $\rsf\texttt{++}$
                 \end{itemize}
            \end{enumerate}
        \end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}

In routine {\sc Generator}, we add a pair $(\sigma, g_\sigma^x)$ into the active set
$L_{act}^{x}$ if $\sigma$ has a generator at the current grade $x$. We also
record its boundary map in $f$ by calling the routine {\sc Boundary} (step 1).
If the active set contains a simplex $\sigma$ with two different generators, they are
identified into one generator triggering a relation. If this relation does not
create a cycle in the relation graph $\G_{act}^{x}$, we add it to both
$\G_{act}^{x}$ and $p^1$. Otherwise, we ignore it as a superfluous relation.

Again, consider the example in Figure~\ref{fig:posettower}. First, consider
the case for edges ($\ell=1$). At $x_5$, we have $(uw,g_{uv}^{x_1})$ and
$(uw,g_{uw}^{x_2})$ in $L_{act}^{x_5}$ from the routine {\sc Collapse}.
We identify two copies of $uw$, and add edge $e^{x_5}_{(g_{uv}^{x_1}, g_{uw}^{x_2})}$
in the graph $\G^{x_5}_{act}$ whose addition does not create cycle; see Figure~\ref{fig:relgraph} (bottom right). Therefore, we
generate a relation $r_1^{x_5}\mapsto g_{uv}^{x_1}+g_{uw}^{x_2}$ (step 2(a)), and delete, say, $(uw,g_{uw}^{x_2})$
(step 2(b)). A column for $r_1^{x_5}$ is added to $p^1$ along with a row in $p^2$.

Next, consider the case for vertices ($\ell=0$). At $x_4$, three
generators $g_v^{x_1}$, $g_v^{x_2}$, and $g_v^{x_3}$ of $v$ generate the pairs
$(v,g_v^{x_1}), (v,g_v^{x_2})$, and $(v,g_v^{x_3})$ in $L_{act}^{x_4}$ after the
{\sc Collapse} call. Step 2 potentially creates 3 relations $r_1^{x_4}\mapsto g_v^{x_1}+g_v^{x_2}$, $r_2^{x_4}\mapsto g_v^{x_2}+g_v^{x_3}$, and
$r_3^{x_4}\mapsto g_v^{x_1}+g_v^{x_3}$. Assuming that edges for relations
$r_1^{x_4}$ and $r_2^{x_4}$ are added first in $\G_{act}^{x_4}$ (Figure~\ref{fig:relgraph} (bottom left)), the edge for $r_3^{x_4}$ (shown dotted) creates a cycle. So, it is not added to $\G^{x_4}_{act}$, and equivalently
only columns for relations $r_1^{x_4}$ and $r_2^{x_4}$ are added in $p^1$
(corresponding rows in $p^2$).

At the end (step 2(c)), we compute a spanning forest $\F_{act}^{x}$ of $\G_{act}^{x}$
which we keep associated with the degree $x$ for subsequent use. 
In Figure~\ref{fig:relgraph} (bottom left), $\F^{x}_{act}$ happens to be the
same with $\G^{x}_{act}$ for every $x$. However, it is not difficult
to imagine that we could have a node where relation forests from predecessors
come together to form a cycle, see e.g. node $x_4$ in Figure~\ref{fig:relrelgraph}. In this case, the relation forest and the
relation graph would be different and the edges ($e^{x_4}_{(g_v^{y_1},g_v^{y_2})},
e^{x_4}_{(g_v^{y_2},\Omega)})$ not included in the forest
would give rise to relations of relations adding two columns in $p^2$.


\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth]{Figure/RelRel.pdf}}
\caption{Relation graphs $\G^{x_i}_{act}$ for vertices ($\ell=0$), poset tower not shown, relation forest $\F_{act}^{x_4}$ is a proper subgraph of $\G^{x_4}_{act}$ which
is passed to $x_5$.}
\label{fig:relrelgraph}
\end{figure}

\vspace{0.1in}
\noindent
{\bf Algorithm {\sc Generator}} ($P$, $\mathcal S$, $x$)
\begin{enumerate}
    \item For all $g_\sigma^x\in \mathcal{S}$ add $(\sigma,g_{\sigma}^x)$ to $L_{act}^{x}$, add vertex
    $g_\sigma^{x}$ to $\G_{act}^{x}$, add row with index $g_\sigma^{x}$ to $p^1$ and $f$,
    Call {\sc Boundary}($x$, $\sigma$)
    \item  For every ($(\sigma,g_\tau^y), (\sigma,g_\rho^z)\in L_{act}^{x}$) and ($g_\tau^y \neq g_\rho^z$) do
            \begin{enumerate}
                \item If adding edge $e^{x}_{(g_\tau^y,g_\rho^z)}$ to $\G_{act}^{x}$
                does not create a cycle
                \begin{itemize}
                    \item Add column $\rsf^{x}$ in $p^1$ with $p^1[g_\tau^{y},\rsf^{x}]=1$ and
                $p^1[g_\rho^{z},\rsf^{x}]=1$; add a row $\rsf^{x}$ to $p^2$;
                    \item $\rsf\texttt{++}$;
                    \item $E(\G_{act}^{x}):=E(\G_{act}^{x})\cup \{e^{x}_{(g_\tau^y,g_\rho^z)}\}$ 
                \end{itemize}
                \item Delete $(\sigma,g_\rho^z)$ from $L_{act}^{x}$
            \end{enumerate}
    \item Compute a spanning forest $\F_{act}^{x}$ of $\G_{act}^{x}$ 
\end{enumerate}

\noindent
{\bf Algorithm {\sc Boundary}} ($x$, $\sigma$)
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Find $(\tau_1,g_{\kappa_1}^{y_1}),\ldots,(\tau_k,g_{\kappa_k}^{y_k})$ in $L_{act}^{x}$ s.t. $\sum_{i=1}^k \tau_i=\partial \sigma$.
    \item Add column $g_\sigma^x$ in $f$ with $f[g_{\kappa_j}^{y_j},g_\sigma^{x}]=1$ $\forall j\in \{1,\ldots, k\}$
\end{enumerate}

The routine {\sc RelRel} creates relations of relations adding columns to
the matrix $p^2$. It uses the spanning forest $\F_{act}^{x}$ of
the relation graph $\G_{act}^{x}$ available
at this stage. For every edge in $\G_{act}^{x}$ that is not in
$\F_{act}^{x}$, it computes a cycle, say $\gamma$, and
registers a relation of relations using the edges on $\gamma$. 

It is registered as a column
in $p^2$. For example, in Figure~\ref{fig:relrelgraph}, the two edges
$e^{x_4}_{(g_v^{y_1},g_v^{y_2})},
e^{x_4}_{(g_v^{y_2},\Omega)}$ add two columns $rr_1^{x_4}$ and $rr_2^{x_4}$
in $p^2$.
Finally, there is an option to call {\sc Reduce} which minimizes $p^2$.

\vspace{0.1in}
\noindent
{\bf Algorithm {\sc RelRel}} ($x$)
\begin{itemize}
      % \item Compute $\G'=\cup_{y\prec x_i} F_{act}^y$
     %  \item Compute $\G'$ deleting marked edges from $\G_{act}^{x_i}$ 
      % \item Compute a spanning forest $F$ of $\G'$. 
      \item For every edge $e_*^{z_0}\in  \G_{act}^{x}\setminus \F_{act}^{x}$:
 %\item Compute $\G'$ as the union of all cycles stored as marked edges
 %in the graphs $\G^y_{act}$ for all $y\prec x_i$.
 %\item Compute a spanning forest $F$ of $\G_{act}^{x_i}$. For
 %every edge $e_*^{z_0}\not\in \G'\cup F$... creating a cycle do:
        \begin{enumerate}
            \item let $e_*^{z_1},\ldots, e_*^{z_s}$ be a sequence of edges in $\F_{act}^{x}$
            connecting two vertices of $e_*^{z_0}$
            \item Add a relation of relations $rr^{x}$ in $p^2$ where
            $p^2[\rsf^{z_j},\rrsf^{x}]=1$ $\forall j\in\{0,\ldots,s\}$;
            \item $\rrsf\texttt{++}$;
          %  \item $\F_{act}^{x_i}:=\G'$
            \item (Optional) Call {\sc Reduce} ($x$)
        \end{enumerate}
\end{itemize}

The routine {\sc Reduce} deletes extra relations of relations by left-to-right column reduction of an appropriate submatrix of $p^2$. This makes $p^2$ minimal as we show in Proposition~\ref{prop:proj_cover_p2}.

\vspace{0.1in}
\noindent
{\bf Algorithm {\sc Reduce}} ($x$)
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Compute the submatrix $A$ of $p^2$ consisting of columns with indices
    $\rrsf^y$ where $y\leq x$. 
    \item Reduce $A$ to column echelon form so that either each column has
    a unique row as a pivot or is completely zeroed out (column orders 
    respect the chosen linear extension of grades)
    \item Remove a column with index $\rrsf^{x}$ from $p^2$ if the column is reduced
    to a zero column in reduced matrix $A$
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Time complexity analysis of the algorithm {\sc Presentation}} \label{subsec:complexity_analysis}
We analyze how much time each of the {\sc Collapse} and {\sc Generator}
takes for every grade $x_i$ and then for all grades. Let $t_0$ and $t_1$ be the total number of vertices (grades) and edges, respectively, in the directed acyclic graph with which the poset $P$
is represented and let $t=t_0+t_1$. Also, let $n$ denote the number $|\Scal|$ of generators plus the number $|\Ccal|$ of edge events input to the algorithm. 
So, the maximum number of simplices
in the complex $K(x)$ is $O(n)$ for any $x$. The maximum dimension
$d$ of a simplex is assumed to be constant for this analysis.

Let $n_y\leq n$ denote the number of active simplices
in grade $y$ and $n_{y\prec x}$ denote the number of edge events
on the poset edge $y\prec x$. Then, steps 1, 2(a), 2(b.i) in {\sc Collapse} cannot take more than $\sum_{y\prec x} O(n_y+n_{y\prec x})$.
This can be done using a dictionary for $y\prec x$ initialized as $v\mapsto v$ for all vertices in $L_y^{act}$, which has length at most $n_y$. Then, we go over at most $n_{y\prec x}$ edge events and set $v\mapsto w$ for $c_{v\mapsto w}^{x\prec y}$. Then we update at most $n_y$ simplices using this dictionary which can be done in constant time because we assume the max dimension $d$ of a simplex to be a constant. The same is true for checking if there is a repeated vertex. 
In total, the three steps 1, 2(a), 2(b.i) take $\sum_{x}\sum_{y\prec x} O(n_y+n_{y\prec x})=O(nt_1)$.

For step 2(b.ii), we use a union-find data structure~\cite{CLRbook} to check efficiently
if an added edge creates a cycle or not in the graph $\G^x_{act}$. For this,
we maintain a spanning forest $\F^x_{act}\subseteq \G^x_{act}$ with 
the union-find data structure.
Since the nodes other than $\Omega$ in $\F^x_{act}$ are generators in $\Scal$,
it has size $O(n)$ and thus initially can be built in $O(n\alpha(n))$ time
where $\alpha(\cdot)$ is the 
extremely slowly growing inverse Ackermann function. 
Counting over all $n_y$ active simplices in $L_y^{act}$, the check
for cyclicity takes $O(n_y\alpha(n))$ time.
%We maintain the spanning forests $\F^{x}_{act}$ at every grade $x$ whose nodes
%are generators in $\Scal$. Therefore, each $\F^{x}_{act}$ has size $O(n)$.
%Checking
%if an added edge connected to $\Omega$ creates a cycle or not 
%can be done efficiently by maintaining a union-find data structure for
%$\F^{x}_{act}$~\cite{CLRbook}. 
Then, in total, for all grades, step 2(b.ii) takes time
$\sum_x\sum_{y\prec x}O((n_y+n)\alpha(n))=O(nt_1\alpha(n))$. So, the time complexity of {\sc Collapse} is $O(nt_1\alpha(n))$.

Step 1 in routine {\sc Generator} accesses the generators in $\Scal$ and their
boundaries. Assuming that simplices and their boundaries are maintained
with an appropriate pointer data structure, step 1 clearly does not take more than $O(nd)$ time
at each grade $x$ where $d$ is the maximum dimension of a simplex
in $\Scal$. Assuming $d$ to be a constant, we get a total
complexity of this step as $\sum_{x} O(n)=O(nt_0)$.
Step 2 can be implemented efficiently as follows: We maintain
$L_{act}^{x}$ with a sorted list where each element $(\sigma,g_\tau^{y})$ 
is sorted according to the unique \texttt{id} of 
the simplex $\sigma$. Then, the check in step 2 boils down to recognizing
adjacent pairs of elements in this sorted list that have the same \texttt{id}.
Sorting incurs a complexity of $O(n\log n)$ at $x$ giving a total complexity
of $O(nt_0\log n)$ over all grades. Step 2(a) can be done
by maintaining a union-find
data structure for $\G^x_{act}$. The total complexity of this step depends on the
size $s$ (\#vertices + \#edges) of the graph $\G_{act}^{x}$. If $s_y$ is the size of
the spanning forests $\F_{act}^{y}$, $y\prec x$, then $s\leq \sum_y s_y +O(n)$
where the $O(n)$ accounts for new vertices and edges added at grade $x$.
If $w_x$ denotes the number of $y$ with $y\prec x$, we have
$s=O(nw_x)$ because each forest $\F_{act}^y$ has size $O(n)$. 
However, the number of vertices in $\G_{act}^x$ is at most
$O(n)$ because each generator in $\mathcal S$ can contribute no more than
one vertex in this graph.
Then, step 2(a) takes $O(s\alpha(n))=O(nw_x\alpha(n))$ time. Since
$\sum_xw_x=O(t_1)$, counting over all grades we get that, in total,
step 2(a) takes $\sum_x O(nw_x\alpha(n))=
O(nt_1\alpha(n))$ time. Steps 2(b) and 3 cannot take more than $O(s)$ time giving a total complexity
of $O(nt_1)$. 

Accounting for all steps, {\sc Generator} takes time $O(nt_0\log n+nt_1\alpha(n))=O(nt\log n)$. 

Notice that the matrix $p^1$ gets at most $O(n)$ columns in every grade $x$
because they correspond to the edges of the forest $\F^{x}_{act}$. Therefore,
$p^1$ has a size of $O(nt_0)$.

\vspace{0.1in}
\noindent
{\bf Analysis of {\sc RelRel}}: Steps 1 and 2 take at most $O(n)$ time per edge
$e_*^{z_0}$ since $\F_{act}^{x}$ has size $O(n)$. Counting over all edges of $\G^{x}_{act}$ we get a 
complexity of $O(n^2w_x)$ where $w_x$ is the number of
predecessors of $x$. Then, over all grades, we get a bound of $O(n^2t_1)$.
Notice that the matrix $p^2$ cannot have more columns than the number of edges
in relation graphs $\G^{x}_{act}$ over all grades. Therefore, $p^2$ has size
$O(\sum_x nw_x )=O(nt_1)$.
%Next, we analyze the complexity of {\sc Reduce} that is called at step 1(c).

\vspace{0.1in}
\noindent 
{\bf Analysis of {\sc Reduce}}: The most costly step in {\sc Reduce} is the matrix
reduction to column echelon form. This can be done in $O(s^\omega)$ time 
if the matrix has $O(s)$ columns and rows where $\omega< 2.373$ is the exponent for the
matrix multiplication time complexity. The matrix $A$ being a submatrix
of $p^2$, cannot have more columns and rows than $p^2$. We prove that
{\sc RelRel} with the option of calling {\sc Reduce} minimizes $p^2$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:minimal_resolution}).
Then, $p^2$ cannot have more than $\beta_2$ columns and $\beta_1$ rows
where $\beta_1=O(nt_0)$ and $\beta_2=O(nt_1)$ are the sum of the
Betti numbers of the resolution of
$C_\ell(K)$ over all $\ell\geq 0$ at degrees 1 and 2 respectively. Then, {\sc Reduce} takes $O(\max(\beta_1,\beta_2)^\omega)$ per grade and $O(\max(\beta_1,\beta_2)^\omega)t$ time for all grades. 

Theorem~\ref{thm:p1min} and Theorem~\ref{thm:minimal_resolution} in Section~\ref{sec:p1minimality} and Section~\ref{sec:p2correctness} respectively
establish that {\sc Presentation} computes $p_\ell^1$ and $p_\ell^2$ correctly and minimally for each $\ell\geq 0$. We have:

\begin{theorem} \label{thm:algorithm_complexity}
    Given a poset tower $K\colon P\rightarrow \mathbf{SCpx}$ of size $n$ (total number of generators and edge events) with $P$ being represented by $t_0$ nodes and $t_1$ edges $(t=t_0+t_1)$,  
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item {\sc Presentation} computes a minimal presentation matrix $p_\ell^1$ 
        of dimensions $O(n)\times O(nt_0)$ for $C_\ell(K)$ in $O(nt\log n)$ time;
        \item {\sc Presentation} with {\sc RelRel} computes a matrix $p_\ell^2$ of dimensions $O(nt_0)\times O(nt_1)$ in time $O(n^2t)$. The number of columns $O(nt_1)$ is asymptotically minimal because a minimal $p_\ell^2$ can have $\Theta(nt_1)$ columns;
        \item {\sc Presentation} with {\sc Reduce} computes
        a minimal $p_\ell^2$ in time
        $O(\max(\beta_1,\beta_2)^\omega t)$.
    \end{enumerate} 
\end{theorem}


\section{Proof of correctness} \label{sec:correctness}

\subsection{Correctness of computing presentation $p_\ell^1$}
\label{sec:p1correctness}

In this section, we prove that $p^1_\ell$, as constructed in the algorithm {\sc Presentation}, for a poset tower $K\colon P\rightarrow\mathbf{SCpx}$, is a presentation of $C_\ell(K)$. While the algorithm processes all degrees $\ell$ simultaneously, for the proof we will w.l.o.g. fix a degree $\ell$.  In the following, we will abbreviate $C_\ell(K)$ by $C_\ell$. We start by showing that $G_\ell$ generates $C_\ell$.  

\begin{proposition} \label{prop:correct_generators}
For $K\colon P\rightarrow\mathbf{SCpx}$, let $G_\ell$ be the set of generators of the $\ell$-simplices constructed by the algorithm. Then 
\begin{equation*}
G_\ell=\bigoplus_{g^x_\sigma\in\mathcal{S}_\ell}\text{Proj}[x] . 
\end{equation*}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The algorithm adds every generator in $ g^x_\sigma\in\mathcal{S}_\ell$ at the point $x\in P$ in the step GENERATOR.    
\end{proof}

\noindent
At every point $x\in P$, the vector space $G_\ell(x)$ has a basis $\{g_\sigma^y\in \mathcal{S}_\ell\vert y\leq x\}$ where $g^y_\sigma$ is the contribution of the summand $\text{Proj}[y]$. The vector space $C_\ell(x)$ has a basis generated by the $\ell$-simplices $K_\ell(x)$. We denote the basis elements of $C_\ell(x)$ simply by $\sigma$.   


\begin{definition} \label{def:alpha}
Define the morphism $\alpha_\ell\colon G_\ell\rightarrow C_\ell$ in the following way:
\begin{equation*}
\alpha_\ell(x)(g^y_\sigma)=\begin{cases} K(y\leq x)(\sigma) & \mbox{ if } K(y\leq x)(\sigma)\in K_\ell(x) \\ 0 &  else \end{cases}
\end{equation*}   
\end{definition}

\noindent
In the following, we will use the convention $K(y\leq x)(\sigma)=0$, for $\sigma\in K_\ell(y)$, if $K(y\leq x)(\sigma)\notin K_\ell(x)$. Also note that $g^y_\sigma\in\mathcal{S}_\ell$ implies $\sigma\in K_\ell(y)$.


\begin{proposition} \label{prop:alpha_well_defined}
The morphism $\alpha_\ell$ is well-defined.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $x\leq y\in P$ and $g^z_\sigma\in \mathcal{S}_\ell$ such that $z\leq x$. Then 
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
C_\ell(x\leq y)\circ \alpha_\ell(x)(g^z_\sigma)&=C_\ell(x\leq y)\big(K(z\leq x)(\sigma)\big) \\
&=K(x\leq y)\circ K(z\leq x)(\sigma)\\&=K(z\leq y)(\sigma).    
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
On the other hand 
\begin{equation*}
\alpha_\ell(y)\circ G_\ell(x\leq y)(g^z_\sigma)=\alpha_\ell(y)(g^z_\sigma)=K(z\leq y)(\sigma).    
\end{equation*}
Hence, the diagram
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}
C_\ell(y) & G_\ell(y) \arrow[l,swap,"\alpha_\ell(y)"] \\
C_\ell(x) \arrow[u,"C_\ell(x\leq y)"] & G_\ell(x)  \arrow[l,swap,"\alpha_\ell(x)"] \arrow[u,swap,"G_\ell(x\leq y)"]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
commutes and $\alpha_\ell$ is well-defined.    
\end{proof}


\begin{proposition} \label{prop:alpha_epi}
The morphism $\alpha_\ell$ is an epimorphism.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $\sigma\in K_\ell(x)$. If $\sigma\notin \bigcup_{y\prec x}\text{im }K(y\prec x)$, then $g^x_\sigma\in\mathcal{S}_\ell$ and $\alpha_\ell(x)(g^x_\sigma)=\sigma$. Otherwise, $\sigma=K(y\prec x)(\tau)$ for some $\tau\in K_\ell(y)$. If $\tau$ is generated at $y$, there is $g^y_\tau$ in $\mathcal{S}_\ell$, otherwise we can iterate this argument until we find $\rho\in K_\ell(z)$ such that $g^z_\rho\in\mathcal{S}_\ell$ and $K(z\leq x)(\rho)=\sigma$. Note that this works because we reach a minimal element $a\in P$ after a finite number of steps and, by definition, every $\pi\in K_\ell(a)$ is a generator $g^a_\pi\in \mathcal{S}_\ell$. This implies
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
\alpha_\ell(x)(g^z_\rho)&=\alpha_\ell(x)\circ G_\ell(z\leq x)(g^z_\rho)\\&=C_\ell(z\leq x)\circ \alpha_\ell(z)(g^z_\rho) \\ &=C_\ell(z\leq x)(\rho)\\&=K(z\leq x)(\rho)=\sigma
\end{aligned}    
\end{equation*}
Since $C_\ell(x)$ has a basis $K_\ell(x)$, $\alpha_\ell(x)$ is an epimorphism for all $x\in P$ and, thus, $\alpha_\ell$ is an epimorphism.    
\end{proof}

\noindent
Proposition \ref{prop:alpha_epi} shows that $G_\ell$ does indeed generate $C_\ell$. Next we show that $p_\ell^1$ is a presentation of $C_\ell$. 

\begin{proposition} \label{lemma:algo_1}
Suppose that after the call of the routines {\sc Collapse} and {\sc Generator} in the algorithm, $(\tau,g^y_\sigma)$ is active at $x\in P$. Then $K(y\leq x)(\sigma)=\tau$. 
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Since $g^y_\sigma\in\mathcal{S}$, we have that $(\sigma,g^y_\sigma)$ is added to $L_{act}^y$ by {\sc Generator} at $y\in P$. Since active simplices are added from $\mathcal{S}$ by {\sc Generator} or inherited from predecessors by {\sc Collapse}, $(\tau,g^y_\sigma)$ can only be active at $x$ if $y\leq x$ and if there is a path $y=z_0\prec\cdots \prec z_k=x$ in $P$ along which $g^y_\sigma$ is active as $(\rho_i,g^y_\sigma)$ at $z_i$ with $\rho_0=\sigma$ and $\rho_k=\tau$. By the update procedure of the {\sc Collapse} routine in the algorithm, $\rho_i=K(z_{i\minus 1}\prec z_i)(\rho_{i\minus 1})$. Therefore, $K(y\leq x)(\sigma)=K(z_{k\minus 1}\prec z_k)\circ\cdots\circ K(z_0\prec z_1)(\rho_0)=\rho_k=\tau$.      
\end{proof}

\noindent
Let $r^{y_1},\ldots,r^{y_n}$ be the relations added by the algorithm for the $\ell$-simplices. Then $R_\ell=\bigoplus_{i=1}^n\text{Proj}[y_i]$. As for the generators $G_\ell$, at each point $x\in P$ the vector space $R_\ell(x)$ has a basis $\{r^{y}\vert y\leq x\}$.   

\begin{proposition} \label{prop:p_exact_1}
Let $G_\ell\xleftarrow{p_\ell^1}R_\ell$ be the matrix for the $\ell$-simplices constructed by the algorithm. Then $\alpha_\ell\circ p_\ell^1=0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
First let $r^y\in R_\ell$ be a relation, added by the algorithm in the COLLAPSE step, such that $p_\ell^1(r^y)=g^z_\sigma$. The algorithm adds the relation at $y\in P$ because there exists $a\prec y\in P$ such that $(\tau,g^z_\sigma)$ is active at $a$ but $K(a\prec y)(\tau)=0$. For $y\leq x\in P$ we obtain:
\begin{equation*}
\alpha_\ell(x)\circ p_\ell^1(x)(r^y)=\alpha_\ell(x)(g^z_\sigma)=K(z\leq x)(\sigma).    
\end{equation*}
Since $(\tau,g^z_\sigma)$ is active at $a$, Proposition~\ref{lemma:algo_1} implies that $z\leq a$ and 
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
K(z\leq x)(\sigma)&=K(y\leq x)\circ K(a\leq y)\circ K(z\leq a)(\sigma)\\ &=K(y\leq x)\circ K(a\leq y)(\tau)=0 
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
Thus, $\alpha_\ell(x)\circ p_\ell^1(x)(r^y)=0$. 

Now, let $r^y\in R_\ell$ be a relation, added by the algorithm, such that $p_\ell^1(r^y)=g^z_\sigma+g^w_{\tau}$. The algorithm adds the relation $r^y$ in the GENERATOR routine at $y\in P$ because there exist $a\prec y$ and $b\prec y$ such that $(\rho,g^z_\sigma)$ is active at $a$, $(\kappa,g^w_{\tau})$ is active at $b$ and $K(a\prec y)(\rho)=K(b\prec y)(\kappa)=\pi$. For $y\leq x$, we get 
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
\alpha_\ell(x)\circ p_\ell^1(x)(r^y)&=\alpha_\ell(x)(g^z_\sigma+g^w_{\tau})\\ &=\alpha_\ell(x)(g^z_\sigma)+\alpha_\ell(x)(g^w_{\tau})\\ &=K(z\leq x)(\sigma)+K(w\leq x)(\tau).    
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
By Proposition~\ref{lemma:algo_1}, we further obtain 
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
K(z\leq x)(\sigma)&=K(y\leq x)\circ K(a\leq y)\circ K(z\leq a)(\sigma)\\ &=K(y\leq x)\circ K(a\leq y)(\rho)\\ &=K(y\leq x)(\pi)   
\end{aligned} 
\end{equation*}
and analogously 
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
K(w\leq x)(\tau)&=K(y\leq x)\circ K(b\leq y)\circ K(w\leq b)(\tau)\\ &=K(y\leq x)\circ K(b\leq y)(\kappa)\\ &=K(y\leq x)(\pi).    
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
Therefore, $\alpha_\ell(x)\circ p_\ell^1(x)(r^y)=0$. 

We showed that for all $x\in P$ and all relations $r^y$ of $R_\ell$ such that $y\leq x$ we have $\alpha_\ell(x)\circ p_\ell^1(x)=0$. This implies $\alpha_\ell\circ p_\ell^1=0$ and $\text{im }p_\ell^1\subseteq \text{ker }\alpha_\ell$.    
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{lemma:algo_2}
Let $g^x_\sigma\in\mathcal{S}_\ell$ and $K(x\leq y)(\sigma)=\tau\in K_\ell(y)$. Then after the call of {\sc Collapse} and {\sc Generator} in the algorithm, there exists $(\tau,g^z_\rho)\in L^y_{act}$ and $r\in R_\ell$ such that $g^x_\sigma+g^z_\rho=p_\ell^1(r)$. For $g^z_\rho=g^x_\sigma$, we can choose $r=0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $x=\beta_0\prec\cdots\prec\beta_m=y$ be a path from $x$ to $y$ in $P$. Moreover, let $\pi_i=K(\beta_0\leq \beta_i)(\sigma)$ for $0\leq i\leq m$. In particular, $\pi_0=\sigma$ and $\pi_n=K(x\leq y)(\sigma)=\tau$ and, since $\tau\in K_\ell(y)$, also $\pi_i\in K_\ell(\beta_i)$. At $x\in P$, the generator $(\sigma,g^x_\sigma)$ is active. At $\beta_1$, either $(\pi_1,g^x_\sigma)$ is active or $g^x_\sigma$ is deactivated, i.e.\ deleted from the list of active generators in the GENERATOR routine. In the latter case, the algorithm adds the relation $r^{\beta_1}\mapsto g^x_\sigma+g^{a_1}_{\kappa_1}$ for the active generator $(\pi_1,g^{a_1}_{\kappa_1})$ representing $\pi_1$ at $\beta_1$ if the edge $e^{\beta_1}_{(g^x_\sigma,g^{a_1}_{\kappa_1})}$ does not close a cycle. If the edge $e^{\beta_1}_{(g^x_\sigma,g^{a_1}_{\kappa_1})}$ closes a cycle, then there is a path from $g^x_\sigma$ to $g^{a_1}_{\kappa_1}$ in $\mathcal{G}_{act}^{\beta_1}$. The edges on this path correspond to relations $r^{\gamma_1},\ldots,r^{\gamma_q}\in R_\ell$ with $\gamma_j\leq \beta_1$ such that $p_\ell^1(r^{\gamma_1}+\cdots+r^{\gamma_q})=g^x_\sigma+g^{a_1}_{\kappa_1}$. Therefore, in any case, there exists $r_1\in R_\ell$ such that $p_\ell^1(r_1)=g^x_\sigma+g^{a_1}_{\kappa_1}$. Let $(\pi_i,g^{a_i}_{\kappa_i})$ for $0\leq i\leq m$ be the active generators representing $\pi_i$ at $\beta_i$. In particular, $(\pi_0,g^{a_0}_{\kappa_0})=(\sigma,g^x_\sigma)$ and, since $\pi_m=\tau$, we define $(\tau,g^z_\rho)\coloneqq (\pi_m,g^{a_m}_{\kappa_m})$. Whenever $g^{a_i}_{\kappa_i}\neq g^{a_{i+1}}_{\kappa_{i+1}}$, as discussed above, there exists a relation $r_j$ such that $p_\ell^1(r_j)= g^{a_i}_{\kappa_i}+g^{a_{i+1}}_{\kappa_{i+1}}$. Therefore, $p_\ell^1(r_1+\cdots+r_q)=g^{a_0}_{\kappa_0}+g^{a_m}_{\kappa_m}=g^x_\sigma+g^{z}_\rho$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{lemma:algo_3}
Let $g^y_\sigma\in$ and $y\leq x\in P$ such that $K(y\leq x)(\sigma)\notin K_\ell(x)$. Then there exists $r\in R_\ell$ such that $p_\ell^1(r)=g^y_\sigma$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $y=\beta_0\prec\cdots\prec\beta_m=x$ be a path from $y$ to $x$ in $P$. Moreover, let $\pi_i=K(\beta_0\leq \beta_i)(\sigma)$ for $0\leq i\leq m$. Since $K(y\leq x)(\sigma)=K(\beta_{m\minus 1}\prec \beta_m)\circ\cdots\circ K(\beta_0\prec \beta_1)(\sigma)\notin K_\ell(x)$, there exists a minimal $0<i\leq m$ such that $\pi_i\notin K_\ell(\beta_i)$. By Proposition \ref{lemma:algo_2}, there exists an active generator $(\pi_{i\minus 1},g^z_\rho)$ at $\beta_{i\minus 1}$ representing $\pi_{i \minus 1}$ and $r'\in R_\ell$ such that $g^y_\sigma+g^z_\rho=p_\ell^1(r')$. Since $K(\beta_{i\minus 1}\prec \beta_i)(\pi_{i\minus 1})\notin K_\ell(\beta_i)$ the algorithm adds the relation $r^{\beta_i}\mapsto g^z_\rho$ in the COLLAPSE routine if the edge $e_{(\Omega,g^z_\rho)}^{\beta_i}$ does not close a cycle in $\mathcal{G}_{act}^{\beta_i}$. If the edge $e_{(\Omega,g^z_\rho)}^{\beta_i}$ does close a cycle, then there is a path from $\Omega$ to $g^z_\rho$ in $\mathcal{G}_{act}^{\beta_i}$. This path corresponds to relations $r^{\gamma_1},\ldots,r^{\gamma_q}$ such that $p_\ell^1(r^{\gamma_1}+\cdots+r^{\gamma_q})=g^z_\rho$. Hence, in any case, there exists a relation $r''\in R_\ell$ such that $p_\ell^1(r'')=g^z_\rho$. Therefore, $p_\ell^1(r'+r'')=g^y_\sigma+g^z_\rho+g^z_\rho=g^y_\sigma$. 
\end{proof}


\begin{proposition} \label{prop:p_exact_2}
Let $G_\ell\xleftarrow{p_\ell^1}R_\ell$ be the matrix for the $\ell$-simplices constructed by the algorithm. Then $\text{im }p_\ell^1=\text{ker }\alpha_\ell$. 
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $g^{y_1}_{\sigma_1},\ldots,g^{y_m}_{\sigma_m}\in G_\ell$ such that $y_i\leq x$ and $g^{y_i}_{\sigma_i}\neq g^{y_j}_{\sigma_j}$ for all $1\leq i<j \leq m$ and assume $\alpha_\ell(x)(g^{y_1}_{\sigma_1}+\cdots+g^{y_m}_{\sigma_m})=\sum_{i=1}^m K(y_i\leq x)(\sigma_i)=0$. Suppose $\tau\in K_\ell(x)$ appears in this sum and $K(y_i\leq x)(\sigma_i)=\tau$ for $1\leq i\leq q$ and $K(y_i\leq x)(\sigma_i)\neq\tau$ for $q+1\leq i\leq m$. This implies that $q$ is even. Let $(\tau,g^z_\rho)\in L_{act}^x$ be the active generator at $x$ representing $\tau$. By Proposition \ref{lemma:algo_2}, there are relations $r_i\in R_\ell$ such that $p_\ell^1(x)(r_i)=g^z_\rho+g^{y_i}_{\sigma_i}$ for $1\leq i\leq q$. Therefore, $p_\ell^1(x)(r_1+\cdots+r_q)=q\cdot g^z_\rho+g^{y_1}_{\sigma_1}+\cdots+g^{y_q}_{\sigma_q}=g^{y_1}_{\sigma_1}+\cdots+g^{y_q}_{\sigma_q}$. For every other simplex $\tau'\in K_\ell(x)$ appearing in this sum we can argue analogously. Now assume $K(y_i\leq x)(\sigma_i)=0$, i.e.\ $K(y_i\leq x)(\sigma_i)\notin K_\ell(x)$. By Proposition \ref{lemma:algo_3}, there exists $r'_i\in R_\ell$ such that $p_\ell^1(x)(r'_i)=g^{y_i}_{\sigma_i}$. We conclude that $g^{y_1}_{\sigma_1}+\cdots+g^{y_m}_{\sigma_m}\in\text{im }p_\ell^1(x)$. Therefore, $\text{ker }\alpha_\ell(x)\subseteq \text{im }p_\ell^1(x)$ for all $x\in P$ 
which together with Proposition~\ref{prop:p_exact_1} implies that $\text{ker }\alpha_\ell=\text{im }p_\ell^1$. 
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition} \label{prop:f_correct}
The morphism $f_\ell\colon G_\ell\rightarrow G_{\ell\minus 1}$, constructed by the algorithm, lifts the morphism $\partial_\ell\colon C_\ell\rightarrow C_{\ell\minus 1}$, i.e.\ the following square commutes:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:lift_diagram_algo}
\begin{tikzcd}
C_{\ell} \arrow[d,swap,"\partial_\ell"] & G_\ell \arrow[l,swap,"\alpha_\ell"] \arrow[d,"f_\ell"] \\
C_{\ell\minus 1} & G_{\ell\minus 1} \arrow[l,swap,"\alpha_{\ell\minus 1}"]
\end{tikzcd}.
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $x\in P$ and $g_\rho^y\in$ such that $y\leq x$. When $(\rho,g^y_\rho)$ is added to $L^y_{act}$ by {\sc Generator} at $y\in P$, the {\sc Boundary} routine of the algorithm finds active generators $(\pi_1,g^{y_1}_{\kappa_1}),\ldots,(\pi_k,g^{y_k}_{\kappa_k})$ such that $y_i\leq y$ and $\partial\rho=\sum_{i=1}^k\pi_i$ and defines $f_\ell(g^y_\rho)=\sum_{i=1}^kg^{y_i}_{\kappa_i}$. By Proposition \ref{lemma:algo_1}, $K(y_i\leq y)(\kappa_i)=\pi_i$ for all $1\leq i\leq k$. First, assume $K(y\leq x)(\rho)=\sigma\in K_\ell(x)$. Then 
\begin{equation*}
\partial_\ell(x)\circ \alpha_\ell(x)(g_\rho^y)=\partial_\ell(x)\big(K(y\leq x)(\rho)\big)=\partial_\ell(x)(\sigma)=\sum_{i=1}^k\tau_i    
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
\alpha_{\ell\minus 1}(x)\circ f_\ell(x)(g^y_\rho)&=\alpha_{\ell\minus 1}(x)(\sum_{i=1}^kg^{y_i}_{\kappa_i})=\sum_{i=1}^k \alpha_{\ell\minus 1}(x)(g^{y_i}_{\kappa_i})=\sum_{i=1}^k K(y_i\leq x)(\kappa_i)  \\ &=\sum_{i=1}^k K(y\leq x)\circ K(y_i\leq y)(\kappa_i)=\sum_{i=1}^k K(y\leq x)(\pi_i) \\ & =\sum_{i=1}^k C_{\ell\minus 1}(y\leq x)(\pi_i)=C_{\ell\minus 1}(y\leq x)(\sum_{i=1}^k \pi_i)\\ &=C_{\ell\minus 1}(y\leq x)\circ\partial_\ell(y)(\rho)=\partial_\ell(x)\circ C_{\ell}(y\leq x)(\rho) \\ &=\partial_\ell(x)\big(K(y\leq x)(\rho)\big)=\partial_\ell(x)(\sigma)=\sum_{i=1}^k \tau_i 
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
where we use that
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large]
C_\ell(y) \arrow[r,"C_\ell(y\leq x)"] \arrow[d,swap,"\partial_\ell(y)"] & C_\ell(x) \arrow[d,"\partial_\ell(x)"] \\
C_{\ell\minus 1}(y) \arrow[r,"C_{\ell\minus 1}(y\leq x)"] & C_{\ell\minus 1}(x)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
commutes. Now assume $K(y\leq x)(\rho)\notin K_\ell(x)$. Then $\partial_\ell(x)\circ \alpha_\ell(x)(g^y_\rho)=0$ and
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
\alpha_{\ell\minus 1}(x)\circ f_\ell(x)(g^y_\rho)=\partial_\ell(x)\big(K(y\leq x)(\rho)\big)=0.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
Therefore, \eqref{eq:lift_diagram_algo} commutes and $f_\ell$ is a lift of $\partial_\ell$ to the generators $G_\ell$ and $G_{\ell\minus 1}$.
\end{proof}


\begin{theorem} \label{thm:correct_presentation}
The matrices $p^1_\ell$ and $f_\ell$ constructed by the algorithm {\sc Presentation}: 
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}
& \vdots \arrow[d] & \vdots \arrow[d] &  \\
0 & C_2 \arrow[d,swap,"\partial_2"] \arrow[l] & G_2 \arrow[l,swap,"\alpha_2"] \arrow[d,"f_2"] & R_2 \arrow[l,swap,"p_2^1"] \\
0 & C_1 \arrow[l]  \arrow[d,swap,"\partial_1"] & G_1 \arrow[l,swap,"\alpha_1"] \arrow[d,"f_1"] & R_1 \arrow[l,swap,"p_1^1"] \\
0 & C_0 \arrow[l] & G_0 \arrow[l,swap,"\alpha_0"] & R_0 \arrow[l,swap,"p_0^1"]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
form a presentation of of $C_\bullet(K)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Proposition \ref{prop:alpha_epi}, $\alpha_\ell$ is an epimorphism. By Proposition \ref{prop:p_exact_2}, $\text{ker }\alpha_\ell=\text{im }p_\ell^1$. Hence, $p_\ell^1$ is a presentation of $C_\ell$. By Proposition \ref{prop:f_correct}, $f_\ell$ is a lift of $\partial_\ell$. Therefore, $f_\ell$ is a presentation of $\partial_\ell$.   
\end{proof}

\subsection{Minimality of presentation $p_\ell^1$}
\label{sec:p1minimality}
In this section we show that the matrix $p_\ell^1$, as constructed by the algorithm {\sc Presentation}, is a minimal presentation of $C_\ell$. For the proof we use an equivalent characterization of minimal resolutions based on the notion of the radical and projective cover. 

\begin{definition}[Radical] \label{def:radical}
Let $M$ be a $P$-persistence module. Then $\text{Rad}(M)\colon P\rightarrow \mathbf{Vec}$ defined, for all $x\leq y\in P$, by 
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
\text{Rad}(M)(x)&\coloneqq \sum_{u<x}\text{im }M(u<x) \\
\text{Rad}(M)(x\leq y)&\coloneqq \sum_{u<x}\text{im }M(u<x)\xrightarrow{M(x\leq y)} \sum_{v<y}\text{im }M(v<y)
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
is called the radical of $M$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Projective cover] \label{def:projective_cover}
Let $M$ be a $P$-persistence module. Then a morphism $g\colon Q\rightarrow M$ is called a projective cover of $M$, if $Q$ is projective, $g$ is an epimorphism and $\text{ker }g\subseteq \text{Rad}(Q)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:minimal_eqq_projective_cover}
A projective resolution as in \eqref{def:projective_resolution} is minimal if $q_i\colon Q_i\rightarrow\text{ker }q_{i\minus 1}$ is a projective cover for all $i\geq 0$. (See \cite{brown2024discretemicrolocalmorsetheory} for the injective case.)
\end{proposition}


\begin{proposition} \label{prop:alpha_proj_cover}
The morphism $\alpha_\ell\colon G_\ell\rightarrow C_\ell$ is a projective cover.    
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $\text{Rad}(G_\ell)$ denote the radical of $G_\ell$ as defined in Definition \ref{def:radical}. Suppose $\alpha_\ell(x)(g^{y_1}_{\sigma_1}+\cdots+g^{y_m}_{\sigma_m})=0$ for $g^{y_i}_{\sigma_i}\in \mathcal{S}_\ell$ such that $y_i\leq x$ and $g^{y_i}_{\sigma_i}\neq g^{y_j}_{\sigma_j}$ for $i\neq j$. If $y_j=x$, then $\sigma_j\in K_\ell(x)$ and $\sigma_j\notin \bigcup_{z\prec x}\text{im }K(z\prec x)$. For all $i\neq j$, if $y_i=x$, then $\sigma_j\neq\sigma_i$. For all $y_i<x$, $K(y_i\leq x)(\sigma_i)\neq \sigma_j$ since any path from $y_i$ to $x$ contains some predecessor $z\prec x$ which would imply $K(z\prec x)\circ K(y_i\leq z)(\sigma_i)=\sigma_j$. Therefore,
\begin{equation*}
\alpha_\ell(x)(g^{y_1}_{\sigma_1}+\cdots+g^{y_m}_{\sigma_m})=\sum_{i=1}^m K(y_i\leq x)(\sigma_i)=\sigma_j+\sum_{i\neq j}K(y_i\leq x)(\sigma_i):
\end{equation*}
But this sum can not be zero since $K(y_i\leq x)(\sigma_i)\neq \sigma_j$ for all $y_i<x$ and $\sigma_i\neq\sigma_j$ for all $y_i=x$ with $i\neq j$. This contradiction implies $y_i<x$ for all $1\leq i\leq m$. Therefore, $g^{y_i}_{\sigma_i}\in \text{im }G_\ell(z\prec x)$ for some $z\prec x$ and $g^{y_1}_{\sigma_1}+\cdots+g^{y_m}_{\sigma_m}\in \text{Rad}(G_\ell)(x)$. We conclude that $\text{ker }\alpha_\ell\subseteq \text{Rad}(G_\ell)$ which implies that $\alpha_\ell$ is a projective cover of $C_\ell$.    
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition} \label{lem:act_gen_simp}
At any point $x_i$ in the algorithm {\sc Presentation}, after the call of {\sc Generator}, $L^{x_i}_{act}\cong K(x_i)$ as sets.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The point $x_0$ is minimal in $P$ as any predecessor would have to come before $x_0$ in the linear extension. At a minimal element $x_0\in P$, for every $\sigma\in K(x_0)$ there is a $g_\sigma^{x_0}\in\mathcal{S}$. Therefore, the identification $(\sigma,g^{x_0}_\sigma)\sim \sigma$ yields $L_{act}^{x_0}\cong K(x_0)$.

Assume that $L_{act}^{x_j}\cong K(x_j)$ for all $0\leq j < i$. For every $\sigma\in \bigcup_{y\prec x_i}\text{im }K(y\prec x_i)$, there exists a $y\prec x_i$ and $\tau\in K(y)$ such that $K(y\prec x_i)(\tau)=\sigma$. By assumption, there exists $(\tau,g^z_\rho)$ in $L^y_{act}$. By calling {\sc Collapse}, the algorithm adds $(K(y\prec x)(\tau),g^z_\rho\big)=(\sigma,g^z_\rho)$ to $L^{x_i}_{act}$. For every $\sigma\in K(x_i)\setminus \bigcup_{y\prec x_i}\text{im }K(y\prec x_i)$ there exists $g^{x_i}_\sigma\in\mathcal{S}$ which is added as $(\sigma,g^{x_i}_{\sigma})$ to $L_{act}^{x_i}$ in step 1 of {\sc Generator}. Thus, every $\sigma\in K(x_i)$ has at least one representative in $L_{act}^{x_i}$. In step 2 of {\sc Generator}, $(\sigma,g^y_\tau)$ is deleted if there is another element $(\sigma,g^z_\rho)$ in $L_{act}^{x_i}$. Therefore, after this step, there is exactly one representative $(\sigma,g^z_\rho)$ for every $\sigma\in K(x_i)$ and $L_{act}^{x_i}\cong K(x_i)$.
\end{proof}


\begin{definition} \label{def:boundary_p}
Suppose $p^1_\ell$ is the presentation matrix generated by the algorithm. Define $\overline{p}^1_\ell\colon R_\ell\rightarrow \overline{G}_\ell$ where $\overline{G}_\ell=G_\ell\oplus \Omega$ with an additional generator $\Omega$ of grade $-\infty<x$ for all $x\in P$ and where $\overline{p}_\ell^1$ is the matrix $p_\ell^1$ with one additional row $\Omega$ and each column of the form $r^y\mapsto g^x_\sigma$ is replaced by the column $r^y\mapsto g^x_\sigma+\Omega$. Since every column of $\overline{p}_\ell^1$ has exactly two non-zero entries, it is the boundary matrix of a $\overline{P}$-filtered graph $\mathcal{G}\colon \overline{P}\rightarrow\mathbf{SCpx}$ where $\overline{P}\coloneqq P\cup\{-\infty\}$. The graph $\mathcal{G}$ is defined by the persistent vertices $\Omega$ born at $-\infty$ and $g^x_\sigma\in G_\ell$ born at $x\in P$ and by the persistent edges $r^y\in R_\ell$ born at $y\in P$. All morphisms $\mathcal{G}(x< y)$ are inclusions.
\end{definition}

\cancel{
\begin{proposition} \label{lem:boundary_matrix_p}
At any point $x_i$ of the algorithm after execution of {\sc Generator} the graph $\mathcal{G}^{x_i}_{act}$ has boundary matrix $\overline{p}^1(x_i)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
In {\sc Collapse}, the algorithm adds the edge $e^{x_i}_{(\Omega,g^z_\rho)}$ exactly when it adds the relation $r^{x_i}\mapsto g^z_\rho$. Similarly, in {\sc Generator}, the algorithm adds the edge $e^{x_i}_{(g^y_\tau,g^z_\rho)}$ exactly when it adds the relation $r^{x_i}\mapsto g^y_\tau+g^z_\rho$. If an edge $e_{(*,*)}$ is added to $\mathcal{G}_{act}^y$ then, by {\sc Collapse}, $e_{(*,*)}$ is in $\mathcal{G}_{act}^x$ for all $y\leq x$. The matrix $\overline{p}^1(x_i)$ has all columns $r^y$ of $\overline{p}^1$ with $y\leq x_i$. Therefore, the columns of $\overline{p}^1(x_i)$ are in one-to-one correspondence with the edges of $\mathcal{G}_{act}^{x_i}$. By construction, the edge $e^{x_i}_{(g^y_\tau,g^z_\rho)}$ has boundary $\overline{p}^1(r^{x_i})=g^y_\tau+g^z_\rho$ and the edge $e^{x_i}_{(\Omega,g^z_\rho)}$ has boundary $\overline{p}^1(r^{x_i})=\Omega+g^z_\rho$. Hence, $\overline{p}^1(x_i)$ is the boundary matrix of $\mathcal{G}_{act}^{x_i}$.
\end{proof}
}


\begin{remark}
The edges of the graph $\mathcal{G}(x)$ are exactly the edges added by the algorithm to the graph $\mathcal{G}_{act}^{x}$. The relations $r^z\mapsto g^x_\sigma+g^y_\tau$ correspond to the edges $e^{x}_{(g^x_\sigma,g^y_\tau)}$ added in step 2.a.\ of {\sc Generator}. The relations $r^z\mapsto g^x_\sigma$ that become $r^z\mapsto g^x_\sigma+\Omega$ in $\overline{p}_\ell^1 $ correspond to the edges $e^x_{(g^x_\sigma,\Omega)}$ added in step 2.b.\ of {\sc Collapse}. Therefore, $\mathcal{G}_{act}^x$ is a subgraph of $\mathcal{G}(x)$ for every $x\in P$.
\end{remark}


\begin{proposition}\label{prop:spanning_forest}
At any point $x_i$ of the algorithm, after execution of {\sc Generator}, $\mathcal{F}_{act}^{x_i}$ is a spanning forest of $\mathcal{G}(x_i)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By definition, it is clear that $\mathcal{F}_{act}^{x_i}$ is always a subgraph of $\mathcal{G}(x_i)$. At a minimal point $x\in P$ the statement is obviously true as there are no edges in $\mathcal{F}_{act}^{x}$ or $\mathcal{G}(x)$. Assume that the statement is true for all $0\leq j<i$. At the point $x_i$, we have that $\mathcal{G}(x_i)$ is the union of all $\mathcal{G}(y)$ with $y\prec x_i$ plus the relations $r^{x_i}$ added at $x_i$. Define $\mathcal{G}'\coloneqq\bigcup_{y\prec x_i}\mathcal{G}(y)$. By inductive assumption $\mathcal{F}_{act}^y$ is a spanning forest of $\mathcal{G}(y)$. Hence, $\bigcup_{y\prec x_i}\mathcal{F}_{act}^y$ contains a spanning forest of $\mathcal{G}'$. In {\sc Collapse}, the algorithm adds the edge $e^{x_i}_{(\Omega,g^z_\rho)}$ exactly when it adds the relation $r^{x_i}\mapsto g^z_\rho$. Similarly, in {\sc Generator}, the algorithm adds the edge $e^{x_i}_{(g^y_\tau,g^z_\rho)}$ exactly when it adds the relation $r^{x_i}\mapsto g^y_\tau+g^z_\rho$. A relation is only added to $\mathcal{G}_{act}^{x_i}$ if it does not close a cycle. This implies that at the point $x_i$ where the edge is added, it is in the spanning forest $\mathcal{F}_{act}^{x_i}$. Hence, every edge added at $x_i$ is added to $\mathcal{G}'$ and also to $\mathcal{F}_{act}^{x_i}$. Before adding edges at $x_i$, a spanning forest $\mathcal{F}_{act}^{x_i}$ of $\mathcal{G}_{act}^{x_i}$ is also a spanning forest of $\mathcal{G'}$. Since all edges at $x_i$ are added to both $\mathcal{G}'$ and $\mathcal{F}_{act}^{x_i}$, this property is preserved. Therefore, the spanning forest $\mathcal{F}_{act}^{x_i}$ of $\mathcal{G}_{act}^{x_i}$, constructed in {\sc Generator}, is also a spanning forest of $\mathcal{G}(x_i)$.  
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Since $\overline{p}^1_\ell(x)$ is the boundary matrix of $\mathcal{G}(x)$, $\text{coker }\overline{p}^1_\ell(x)$ is the space of connected components and $\text{ker }\overline{p}^1_\ell(x)$ is the space of linear independent cycles of $\mathcal{G}(x)$.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition} \label{lem:graph_boundary_kernel}
For all $x\in P$, we have $\text{ker }\overline{p}^1_\ell(x)\cong \text{ker }p^1_\ell(x)$ and $\text{coker }\overline{p}^1_\ell(x)\cong \text{coker }p^1_\ell(x)\oplus [\Omega]$ where $[\Omega]$ denotes the connected component of $\Omega$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The matrix $p^1_\ell(x)$ is the restriction of $p^1_\ell$ to all generators $g^y_\sigma$ and relations $r^z$ such that $y,z\leq x$. Suppose $p^1_\ell(x)(r^{y_1}+\cdots+r^{y_k})=(g^{a_1}_{\sigma_1}+g^{b_1}_{\tau_1})+\cdots+(g^{a_p}_{\sigma_p}+g^{b_p}_{\tau_p})+g^{c_1}_{\rho_1}+\cdots+g^{c_q}_{\rho_q}=0$. Because all generators $g^{*}_{*}$ are basis elements of $G_\ell(x)$ and a sum of basis elements over $\mathbb{Z}_2$ can only be zero if every basis element appears an even number of times in the sum, the number of single generator relations $q$ has to be even. Also, this implies $\overline{p}^1_\ell(x)(r^{y_1}+\cdots+r^{y_k})=(g^{a_1}_{\sigma_1}+g^{b_1}_{\tau_1})+\cdots+(g^{a_p}_{\sigma_p}+g^{b_p}_{\tau_p})+g^{c_1}_{\rho_1}+\cdots+g^{c_q}_{\rho_q}+q\Omega=0$. Conversely, if $\overline{p}^1_\ell(x)(r^{y_1}+\cdots+r^{y_k})=(g^{a_1}_{\sigma_1}+g^{b_1}_{\tau_1})+\cdots+(g^{a_p}_{\sigma_p}+g^{b_p}_{\tau_p})+(g^{c_1}_{\rho_1}+\Omega)+\cdots+(g^{c_q}_{\rho_q}+\Omega)=(g^{a_1}_{\sigma_1}+g^{b_1}_{\tau_1})+\cdots+(g^{a_p}_{\sigma_p}+g^{b_p}_{\tau_p})+g^{c_1}_{\rho_1}+\cdots+g^{c_q}_{\rho_q}+q\Omega=0$. We get that $q$ is even and therefore also $p^1_\ell(x)(r^{y_1}+\cdots+r^{y_k})=(g^{a_1}_{\sigma_1}+g^{b_1}_{\tau_1})+\cdots+(g^{a_p}_{\sigma_p}+g^{b_p}_{\tau_p})+g^{c_1}_{\rho_1}+\cdots+g^{c_q}_{\rho_q}=0$. Hence, $\text{ker }p^1_\ell(x)\cong\text{ker }\overline{p}^1_\ell(x)$ for all $x\in P$. 

Since $\overline{G}_\ell(x)=G_\ell(x)\oplus \Omega$ we can define a projection map $\pi\colon \overline{G}_\ell(x)\rightarrow G_\ell(x)$ that forgets $\Omega$. We obtain
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}
\text{coker }\overline{p}^1_\ell(x) \arrow[d,swap,"\text{coker }\pi"] & \overline{G}_\ell(x) \arrow[l,swap,"\phi"] \arrow[d,"\pi"] & R_\ell(x) \arrow[l,swap,"\overline{p}(x)"] \arrow[d,"="] \\
\text{coker }p^1_\ell(x) & G_\ell(x) \arrow[l,swap,"\psi"] & R_\ell(x) \arrow[l,swap,"p^1_\ell(x)"]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
Since $\overline{p}^1_\ell(x)$ is the boundary matrix of a graph, the cokernel has a basis consisting of the connected components of this graph. If $[\Omega]$ denotes the equivalence class of $\Omega$ in $\text{coker }\overline{p}^1_\ell(x)$, then  $\text{coker }\pi([\Omega])=[\pi(\Omega)]=0$. Since $\psi\circ \pi=\text{coker }\pi\circ \phi$ is an epimorphism, also $\text{coker }\pi$ is an epimorphism and $\text{coker }\overline{p}^1_\ell(x)\cong \text{im }\text{coker }\pi\oplus \text{ker coker }\pi\cong \text{coker }p^1_\ell(x) \oplus \text{ker coker }\pi$. Since $\text{dim }\text{ker }p^1_\ell(x)=\text{dim }\text{ker }\overline{p}^1_\ell(x)$, we also get $\text{dim im }p^1_\ell(x)=\text{dim im }\overline{p}^1_\ell(x)$ and, moreover, $\text{dim coker }p^1_\ell(x)=\text{dim }G_\ell(x)-\text{dim im }p^1_\ell(x)=\text{dim }\overline{G}_\ell(x)-1-\text{dim im }\overline{p}^1_\ell(x)=\text{dim coker }\overline{p}^1_\ell(x)-1$. Thus, $\text{ker coker }\pi$ is one-dimensional generated by $[\Omega]$ and $\text{coker }\overline{p}^1_\ell(x)\cong \text{coker }p^1_\ell(x) \oplus [\Omega]$.
\end{proof}


\begin{proposition} \label{prop:p1_proj_cover}
The morphism $p_\ell^1\colon R_\ell\rightarrow \text{ker }\alpha_\ell$ is a projective cover.
\end{proposition}


\begin{proof}
By construction $R_\ell$ is projective. By Proposition \ref{prop:p_exact_2}, $\text{ker } \alpha_\ell=\text{im }p_\ell^1$. Thus, $p_\ell^1\colon R_\ell\rightarrow \text{ker }\alpha_\ell$ is an epimorphism. 

Let $x\in P$ and $r^{y_1}_1,\ldots, r^{y_k}_k\in R_\ell$ such that $y_i\leq x$ and $r^{y_i}_i\neq r^{y_j}_j$ for $i\neq j$. Assume $p_\ell^1(x)(r^{y_1}_1+\cdots+r^{y_k}_k)=0$. By Proposition \ref{lem:graph_boundary_kernel}, we obtain that $\overline{p}_\ell^1(x)(r^{y_1}_1+\cdots+r^{y_k}_k) =0$
and, thus, $e^{y_1}_1,\ldots,e^{y_k}_k$ is a cycle in the graph $\mathcal{G}(x)$. For any edge $e^{y_i}_i$ such that $y_i=x$ the corresponding relation $r^x_i$ is added at the point $x$ during the algorithm. Since this only happens when the edge $e^x_i$ is added to $\mathcal{G}_{act}^x$, any edge $e^x_i$ on the cycle is in $\mathcal{G}_{act}^x$. If $y_i<x$ then there exists a $z\prec x$ such that $e^{y_i}_i\in\mathcal{G}(z)$. By Proposition \ref{prop:spanning_forest}, $\mathcal{F}_{act}^z$ is a spanning forest of $\mathcal{G}(z)$. If $e^{y_i}_i\notin\mathcal{F}_{act}^z$, then there exists a path $\gamma_1^{i},\ldots,\gamma^{i}_{l_i}$ in $\mathcal{F}_{act}^z$ connecting the endpoints of $e^{y_i}_i$. So, by construction of $\mathcal{G}_{act}^x$ in {\sc Collapse}, in any case, there exists a path in $\mathcal{G}_{act}^x$ connecting the endpoints of the edge $e^{y_i}_i$. Hence, we can replace any edge on the cycle $e^{y_1}_1,\ldots,e^{y_k}_k$ that is not in $\mathcal{G}_{act}^x$ by a path in $\mathcal{G}_{act}^x$. This yields another cycle that lies completely in $\mathcal{G}_{act}^x$. If there are edges $e^x_i$ on the initial cycle, they will not be replaced and are also on the cycle in $\mathcal{G}_{act}^x$. But this implies that there is an edge $e^x_i$, added at the point $x$, closing the cycle. This is a contradiction as the algorithm does not add edges that close cycles in $\mathcal{G}_{act}^x$. Therefore, $y_i<x$ for all $1\leq i\leq k$. Recalling Definition \ref{def:radical}, we get that $r^{y_1}_1+\cdots+r^{y_k}_k\in \text{Rad}(R_\ell)(x)$ and, moreover, $\text{ker }p_\ell^1(x)\subseteq \text{Rad}(R_\ell)(x)$. This implies that $\text{ker }p_\ell^1\subseteq \text{Rad}(R_\ell)$ and, thus, $p^1_\ell$ is a projective cover of $\text{ker }\alpha_\ell$.
\end{proof}


\begin{theorem}
The morphism $p_\ell^1\colon R_\ell\rightarrow G_\ell$ is a minimal presentation of $C_\ell$.
\label{thm:p1min}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Proposition \ref{prop:alpha_proj_cover} and \ref{prop:p1_proj_cover}, $\alpha_\ell\colon G_\ell\rightarrow C_\ell$ is a projective cover of $C_\ell$ and $p_\ell^1\colon R_\ell\rightarrow \text{ker }\alpha_\ell$ is a projective cover of $\text{ker }\alpha_\ell$. Therefore, by Proposition \ref{prop:minimal_eqq_projective_cover}, $p_\ell^1$ is a minimal presentation.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Correctness of computing relations of relations and $p_\ell^2$}
\label{sec:p2correctness}

In this section we show that the matrix $p_\ell^2$, as constructed by the algorithm with the call of {\sc RelRel}, yields the restriction of a projective resolution of $C_\ell$ to the first three terms. With the additional call of {\sc Reduce} it will be even minimal.

\begin{proposition} \label{prop:exact_at2_1}
    ${\mathrm im}\, p_\ell^2 \subseteq {\mathrm ker}\, p_\ell^1 $.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $rr^{x}$ is a relation of relations in $RR_\ell$ such that $p^2_\ell(rr^{x})= r^{z_0}+\cdots+r^{z_s}$. The relation of relations is added by the algorithm if and only if the corresponding edges $e^{z_0},\ldots,e^{z_s}$ form a cycle in the graph $\mathcal{G}_{act}^{x}$ at the point $x$. Since $\mathcal{G}_{act}^{x}$ is a subgraph of $\mathcal{G}(x)$, the edges $e^{z_0},\ldots,e^{z_s}$ also form a cycle in $\mathcal{G}(x)$. By Definition \ref{def:boundary_p}, we get that $\overline{p}_\ell^1(y)(r^{z_0}+\cdots+r^{z_s})=0$ and by Proposition \ref{lem:graph_boundary_kernel} also $p_\ell^1(y)(r^{z_0}+\cdots+r^{z_s})=0$ for all $y\geq x$. Hence, $p^1_\ell\circ p_\ell^2(rr^{x})=0$ and $\text{im }p_\ell^2\subseteq \text{ker }p_\ell^1$.
\end{proof}


\begin{proposition} \label{prop:exact_at2_2}
     ${\mathrm ker}\, p_\ell^1 \subseteq {\mathrm im}\, p_\ell^2$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Assume that $\text{ker }p_\ell^1(z)\subseteq \text{im }p_\ell^2(z)$ for all $z<x$. This is obviously true for the minimal elements in $P$.
Let $r^{y_1},\ldots,r^{y_n}\in R_\ell$ so that $y_i\leq x$ for all $1\leq i\leq n$ and $p^1_\ell(x)(r^{y_1}+\cdots+r^{y_n})=0$. By Proposition \ref{lem:graph_boundary_kernel}, $e^{y_1},\ldots,e^{y_n}$ is a cycle in $\mathcal{G}(x)$. By the proof of proposition \ref{prop:p1_proj_cover}, $\text{ker }p_\ell^1(x)\subseteq \text{Rad}(R_l)(x)$. Thus, we have $y_i<x$ and $e^{y_i}\in \mathcal{G}(y)$ for some $y\prec x$ and all $1\leq i\leq n$.  Suppose $e^{y_i}\notin \mathcal{G}_{act}^x$. Then $e^{y_i}\notin \mathcal{F}_{act}^y$. Since, by Proposition \ref{prop:spanning_forest}, $\mathcal{F}_{act}^y$ is a spanning forest of $\mathcal{G}(y)$, there exists a path $e^{z^i_1},\ldots,e^{z^i_{k_i}}$ in $\mathcal{F}_{act}^y$ that connects the endpoints of $e^{y_i}$. Hence, this path together with $e^{y_i}$ forms a cycle in $\mathcal{G}^y_{act}$. By Proposition \ref{lem:graph_boundary_kernel}, $p_\ell^1(y)(r^{y_i}+r^{z^i_1}+\cdots+r^{z^i_{k_i}})=0$. Thus, by induction, there exists $rr_i\in RR_\ell(y)$ such that $p^2_\ell(y)(rr_i)=r^{y_i}+r^{z^i_1}+\cdots+r^{z^i_{k_i}}$. We now replace the edge $e^{y_i}$ in the cycle $e^{y_1},\ldots,e^{y_n}$ by the path $e^{z^i_1},\ldots,e^{z^i_{k_i}}$ which lies in $\mathcal{G}_{act}^{x}$. If we replace each edge $e^{y_i}\notin \mathcal{G}_{act}^x$ by such a path we obtain a cycle $e^{z_1},\ldots,e^{z_l}$ in $\mathcal{G}_{act}^x$ since 
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
p_\ell^1(x)\big(r^{z_1}+\cdots+r^{z_l}\big)=&p_\ell^1(x)\big(\sum_{r^{y_i}\in\mathcal{G}_{act}^x}r^{y_i}+\sum_{r^{y_i}\notin \mathcal{G}_{act}^x}r^{z^i_1}+\cdots+r^{z^i_{k_i}}\big)\\&=p_\ell^1(x)\big(\sum_{r^{y_i}\in\mathcal{G}_{act}^x}r^{y_i}\big)+\sum_{r^{y_i}\notin \mathcal{G}_{act}^x}p_\ell^1(x)\big(r^{z^i_1}+\cdots+r^{z^i_{k_i}}\big)\\&=p_\ell^1(x)\big(\sum_{r^{y_i}\in\mathcal{G}_{act}^x}r^{y_i}\big)+p_\ell^1(x)\big(\sum_{r^{y_i}\notin\mathcal{G}_{act}^x}r^{y_i}\big)\\&=p_\ell^1(x)(r^{y_1}+\cdots+r^{y_n})=0
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
The algorithm {\sc RelRel} computes a cycle basis for $\mathcal{G}_{act}^x$ and adds the computed cycles to $p_\ell^2$. Hence, there exists $rr\in RR_\ell(x)$ such that $p^2_\ell(x)(rr)=r^{z_1}+\cdots+r^{z_l}$. Combining this cycle with the cycles for the replaced edges yields
\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
p_\ell^2(x)(rr+\sum_{r^{y_i}\notin \mathcal{G}_{act}^x}rr_i)&=p_\ell^2(x)(rr)+\sum_{r^{y_i}\notin \mathcal{G}_{act}^x}p_\ell^2(x)(rr_i)\\&=r^{z_1}+\cdots+r^{z_l}+\sum_{r^{y_i}\notin \mathcal{G}_{act}^x}r^{y_i}+r^{z^i_1}+\cdots+r^{z^i_{k_i}}\\&=\sum_{r^{y_i}\in\mathcal{G}_{act}^x}r^{y_i}+\sum_{r^{y_i}\notin \mathcal{G}_{act}^x}r^{z^i_1}+\cdots+r^{z^i_{k_i}}\\&+\sum_{r^{y_i}\notin \mathcal{G}_{act}^x}r^{y_i}+\sum_{r^{y_i}\notin \mathcal{G}_{act}^x}r^{z^i_1}+\cdots+r^{z^i_{k_i}}\\&=r^{y_1}+\cdots+r^{y_n}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
Therefore, $r^{y_1}+\cdots+r^{y_n}\in\text{im }p_\ell^2(x)$ and, moreover, $\text{ker }p_\ell^1\subseteq \text{im }p_\ell^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:proj_cover_p2}
If {\sc Reduce} is called, $p_\ell^2$ is a projective cover of $\text{ker }p_\ell^1$.  
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $x\in P$ and $rr^{y_1},\ldots,rr^{y_n}\in RR_\ell$ such that $y_i\leq x$ and $p^2_\ell(x)(rr^{y_1}+\cdots+rr^{y_n})=0$. At the point $x$ in the algorithm all $rr^{y_i}$ are already in $RR_\ell$ and at the end of {\sc Reduce} all columns $rr^{z}$ of $p_\ell^2$ with $z\leq x$ are $P$-linearly independent. If $y_i=x$, then all columns $rr^{y_j}$ for $j\neq i$ can be added to $rr^{y_i}$ in $p_\ell^2$. Since $p^2_\ell(x)(rr^{y_1}+\cdots+rr^{y_n})=0$, this contradicts the linear independence. We conclude that $y_i<x$ for all $1\leq i\leq n$ and $rr^{y_1}+\cdots+rr^{y_n}\in \text{Rad}(RR_\ell)(x)$. Therefore, $\text{ker }p_\ell^2\subseteq \text{Rad}(RR_\ell)$ and $p_\ell^2$ is a projective cover of $\text{ker }p_\ell^1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem} \label{thm:minimal_resolution}
After call to {\sc RelRel} and {\sc Reduce}, the sequence 
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}
0 & C_\ell(K) \arrow[l] & G_\ell \arrow[l,swap,"\alpha_\ell^2"] & R_\ell \arrow[l,swap,"p_\ell^1"] & RR_\ell \arrow[l,swap,"p_\ell^2"]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
is the restriction of a minimal projective resolution of $C_\ell(K)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By construction $G_\ell$, $R_\ell$ and $RR_\ell$ are projective.
By Proposition \ref{prop:alpha_epi}, \ref{prop:p_exact_2}, \ref{prop:exact_at2_1} and \ref{prop:exact_at2_2} the sequence is exact. Hence, it is the restriction of a projective resolution to the first three terms. By Proposition \ref{prop:alpha_proj_cover}, $\alpha_\ell$ is a projective cover of $C_\ell(K)$, by Proposition \ref{prop:p1_proj_cover}, $p_\ell^1$ is a projective cover of $\text{ker }\alpha_\ell$ and, by Proposition \ref{prop:proj_cover_p2}, $p_\ell^2$ is a projective cover of $\text{ker }p_\ell^1$. Therefore, it is the restriction of the minimal projective resolution.
\end{proof}



\section{Computing PiReps of the persistent homology of poset towers} \label{sec:homology}
\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=0.7\textwidth]{Figure/HomologyExample.pdf}}
\caption{A poset tower over $P=\{x_0,\ldots,x_6\}$ where all the maps are inclusions. Every simplex has a unique generator except $v$ which comes in at $x_1$ and $x_2$. The two copies of $v$ get identified at $x_3$ and $x_4$ by relations $r^{x_3},r^{x_4}\mapsto g^{x_1}_v+g^{x_2}_v$. These two relations become the same at $x_5$ and $x_6$ leading to relations of relations $rr^{x_5},rr^{x_6}\mapsto r^{x_3}+r^{x_4}$.}
\label{fig:HomologyExample}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Computing PiReps from resolutions}

Our goal is to reduce the problem of computing the persistent homology $H_\ell(K)\colon P\rightarrow \mathbf{Vec}$ of a poset tower $K\colon P\rightarrow\mathbf{SCpx}$ to the problem of computing the homology of a projective implicit representation 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:short_complex_of_projectives}
\begin{tikzcd}
D_{\ell-1} & D_{\ell} \arrow[l,swap,"d_{\ell}"] & D_{\ell +1} \arrow[l,swap,"d_{\ell+1}"]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
where the $D_\ell$ are projective modules and the $d_\ell$ are represented by $P$-graded matrices as in Proposition \ref{prop:elementary_projective} such that $\text{ker }d_\ell/\text{im }d_{\ell+1}\cong H_\ell(K)$. The algorithm in Section~\ref{sec:algorithm} enables us to compute (pointwise) projective resolutions (up to the second term) of the chain complex $C_\bullet(K)\in\mathbf{Ch}\big(\mathbf{Vec}^P\big)$ from a poset tower $K\colon P\rightarrow\mathbf{SCpx}$. In this section, we discuss how to compute a PiRep, as in \eqref{eq:short_complex_of_projectives}, from the resolutions of $C_\bullet(K)\in\mathbf{Ch}\big(\mathbf{Vec}^P\big)$ depicted in \eqref{eq:diagram_homology_from_resolutions}. The diagram shows the chain complex we are interested in the leftmost column and the resolutions computed by our algorithm in the remaining columns. Note that the rows are exact and the squares commute.
\begin{equation}\label{eq:diagram_homology_from_resolutions}
\begin{tikzcd}
0 & C_{\ell+1}(K) \arrow[l] \arrow[d,swap,"\partial_{\ell+1}"] & G_{\ell+1} \arrow[l,swap,"\alpha_{\ell+1}"] \arrow[d,swap,"f_{\ell+1}"] \arrow[ddr,"\vartheta"{xshift=-12pt,yshift=13pt},dashed] & R_{\ell+1} \arrow[l,swap,"p_{\ell+1}^1"]  & RR_{\ell+1} \arrow[l,swap,"p_{\ell+1}^2"] \\
0 & C_{\ell}(K) \arrow[l] \arrow[d,swap,"\partial_{\ell}"] & G_{\ell} \arrow[l,swap,"\alpha_{\ell}"] \arrow[d,swap,"f_{\ell}"] & R_{\ell} \arrow[l,swap,"p_{\ell}^1"{xshift=3pt},crossing over] \arrow[d,"\gamma",dashed] & RR_{\ell} \arrow[l,swap,"p_{\ell}^2"] \\
0 & C_{\ell\minus1}(K) \arrow[l] & G_{\ell\minus1} \arrow[l,swap,"\alpha_{\ell\minus1}"] & R_{\ell\minus 1} \arrow[l,swap,"p_{\ell\minus1}^1"] & RR_{\ell\minus1}\arrow[l,swap,"p_{\ell\minus1}^2"] 
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
Each of the maps $p_\ell^i$ and $f_\ell$ is represented by a $P$-graded matrix. We now need to assemble the given projective modules and matrices in such a way that we obtain a PiRep of $H_\ell(K)=\text{ker }\partial_\ell/\text{im }\partial_{\ell+1}$.
We note that this is more or less standard homological algebra but we are not aware of a good reference explaining this process. We provide detailed proofs of all the results in this section in Appendix \ref{sec:homology}. We start by discussing how to obtain $\text{ker }\partial_\ell$ from the resolutions. The example in Figure \ref{fig:HomologyExample} explains the intricacies of this process. Our algorithm from Section \ref{sec:algorithm} will produce the following minimal resolution of the poset tower in the figure:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:poset_tower_presentation}
\begin{tikzcd}[ampersand replacement=\&,every label/.append style = {font = \footnotesize},column sep=large, row sep=large]
G_2 \arrow{d}[swap,xshift=-7pt,yshift=5pt]{\begin{blockarray}{c@{\hspace{7pt}}c}
& g^{x_6}_{uvw} \\
\begin{block}{c@{\hspace{7pt}}(c)}
g^{x_0}_{uw} & 1 \\[2pt]
g^{x_1}_{uv} & 1 \\[2pt]
g^{x_2}_{vw} & 1 \\[2pt]
\end{block}
\end{blockarray}} \& 0 \arrow[l] \& 0 \arrow[l]\\
G_1 \arrow{d}[swap,xshift=-10pt,yshift=-7pt]{\begin{blockarray}{c@{\hspace{7pt}}ccc}
& g^{x_0}_{uw} & g^{x_1}_{uv} & g^{x_2}_{vw} \\
\begin{block}{c@{\hspace{7pt}}(ccc)}
g^{x_0}_{u} & 1 & 1 & 0 \\[2pt]
g^{x_0}_{w} & 1 & 0 & 1 \\[2pt]
g^{x_1}_{v} & 0 & 1 & 0 \\[2pt]
g^{x_2}_{v} & 0 & 0 & 1 \\[2pt]
\end{block}
\end{blockarray}}  \& 0 \arrow[l] \& 0 \arrow[l] \\
G_0 \& R_0 \arrow{l}[xshift=-15pt,yshift=-7pt]{\begin{blockarray}{c@{\hspace{7pt}}cc}
& r^{x_3} & r^{x_4} \\
\begin{block}{c@{\hspace{7pt}}(cc)}
g^{x_0}_{u} & 0 & 0 \\[2pt]
g^{x_0}_{w} & 0 & 0 \\[2pt]
g^{x_1}_{v} & 1 & 1 \\[2pt]
g^{x_2}_{v} & 1 & 1 \\[2pt]
\end{block}
\end{blockarray}}  \& RR_0 \arrow{l}[xshift=3pt,yshift=-7pt]{\begin{blockarray}{c@{\hspace{7pt}}cc}
& rr^{x_5} & rr^{x_6} \\
\begin{block}{c@{\hspace{7pt}}(cc)}
r^{x_3} & 1 & 1 \\[2pt]
r^{x_4} & 1 & 1 \\[2pt]
\end{block}
\end{blockarray}}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
As one can see, the matrix $f_1$ representing $\partial_1$ on the generators has no kernel. The image of the generators representing the triangle $uvw$ is $f_1\big(g^{x_0}_{uw}+g^{x_1}_{uv}+g^{x_2}_{vw}\big)=g^{x_1}_v+g^{x_2}_v$. This is because at the point $x_1$ the only available generator for the boundary vertex $v$ of the edge $uv$ is $g^{x_1}_v$ whereas at the point $x_2$ only $g^{x_2}_v$ is available for the boundary of the edge $vw$. Hence, the kernel of $f_1$ is insufficient to represent the kernel of $\partial_1$. To get the correct kernel, we have to recognize that at the points $x_3$ and $x_4$, the two copies of $v$ get identified which is witnessed by the relations $r^{x_3}$ and $r^{x_4}$. The boundary of the triangle is zero modulo these relations.


To account for this, we define the morphism: 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:kernel_generators}
\begin{pmatrix}f_\ell & p_{\ell\minus 1}^1\end{pmatrix}\colon G_\ell\oplus R_{\ell\minus 1}\rightarrow G_{\ell\minus 1}
\end{equation}
whose kernel serves as the generators of $\text{ker }\partial_\ell$. In the example, we obtain the matrix:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:kernel_example}
\begin{pmatrix}f_1 & p_0^1\end{pmatrix}=
\begin{blockarray}{cccccc}
& g^{x_0}_{uw} & g^{x_1}_{uv} & g^{x_2}_{vw} & r^{x_3} & r^{x_4} \\
\begin{block}{c(ccccc)}
g^{x_0}_{u} & 1 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
g^{x_0}_{w} & 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\
g^{x_1}_{v} & 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\
g^{x_2}_{v} & 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\    
\end{block}
\end{blockarray}
\end{equation}
whose kernel is generated by $g^{x_0}_{uw}+g^{x_1}_{uv}+g^{x_2}_{vw}+r^{x_3}$ and $g^{x_0}_{uw}+g^{x_1}_{uv}+g^{x_2}_{vw}+r^{x_4}$. This kernel represents the triangles at $x_3$ and $x_4$ in Figure \ref{fig:HomologyExample}. A problem ensues because these two triangles become the same triangle at $x_5$. Thus, the kernel of $\partial_1$ has a relation at $x_5$. Since there are no relations between the edges, this relation has to come from relations on $r^{x_3}$ and $r^{x_4}$. In general, relations on the kernel can, of course, also come from relations on the edges. We account for the relations in $G_\ell$ and $R_{\ell\minus 1}$ by defining the following morphism: 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:kernel_relations}
\begin{pmatrix}p_\ell^1 & 0 \\ \gamma & p_{\ell\minus 1}^2\end{pmatrix}\colon R_\ell\oplus RR_{\ell\minus 1}\rightarrow\text{ker}\begin{pmatrix}f_\ell & p_{\ell\minus 1}^1\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}
from the relations of the domain of \eqref{eq:kernel_generators} to the kernel of $\begin{pmatrix}f_\ell & p_{\ell\minus 1}^1\end{pmatrix}$ where we add an additional morphism $\gamma\colon R_\ell\rightarrow R_{\ell\minus 1}$. The morphism $\gamma$ is the lift of the map $f_\ell$ on the generators to the relations $R_\ell$. This morphism is needed to make \eqref{eq:kernel_relations} well defined. Since
\begin{equation*}
\begin{pmatrix}f_\ell & p_{\ell\minus 1}^1\end{pmatrix}\circ \begin{pmatrix}p_\ell^1 & 0 \\ \gamma & p_{\ell\minus 1}^2\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}f_\ell\circ p_\ell^1+p^1_{\ell\minus 1}\circ \gamma & p^1_{\ell\minus 1}\circ p_{\ell\minus 1}^2\end{pmatrix},
\end{equation*}
the second component of the composition is always zero by exactness but the first component will not vanish for $\gamma=0$ in general. The right choice of $\gamma$ to make the first component vanish is the dashed morphism $\gamma$ in \eqref{eq:diagram_homology_from_resolutions} making the square commute. In our running example, the matrix of relations on the kernel is just $p_0^2$, which leads to the identification of the two generators of the kernel at $x_5$ and $x_6$.  

\begin{proposition} \label{prop:kernel_quotient}
The following sequence is exact:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}[ampersand replacement=\&,every label/.append style = {font = \small}]
0 \& \text{ker }\partial_\ell \arrow[l] \&[15pt] \text{ker}\begin{pmatrix}f_\ell & p_{\ell\minus 1}^1\end{pmatrix} \arrow{l}[swap]{\begin{pmatrix}\alpha_\ell & 0\end{pmatrix}} \&[25pt] R_\ell\oplus RR_{\ell\minus 1} \arrow{l}[swap]{\begin{pmatrix}p_\ell^1 & 0 \\ \gamma & p_{\ell\minus 1}^2\end{pmatrix}} .
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}

\noindent
Proposition \ref{prop:kernel_quotient} shows that $\text{ker }\partial_\ell$ is the cokernel of \eqref{eq:kernel_relations} but this is not a presentation since $\text{ker}\begin{pmatrix}f_\ell & p_{\ell\minus 1}^1\end{pmatrix}$ is not projective for general $P$.  


To obtain a representation of the homology $\text{ker }\partial_\ell/\text{im }\partial_{\ell\minus 1}$ we have to construct a morphism representing $\partial_{\ell+1}\colon C_{\ell+1}\rightarrow \text{ker }\partial_\ell$. The morphism $\partial_{\ell+1}$ is represented by $f_{\ell+1}$ on the generators but as one can see in \eqref{eq:poset_tower_presentation} and \eqref{eq:kernel_example}, for the poset tower in Figure \ref{fig:HomologyExample}, $f_2$ does not go to the kernel of $\begin{pmatrix}f_1 & p_0^1\end{pmatrix}$. The problem is again that $f_1\circ f_2\neq 0$ because the boundary of the triangle is only zero modulo relations. In other words, the morphisms $(f_\ell)_\ell$ on the generators do not form a chain complex. But because they lift the differentials $(\partial_\ell)_\ell$, which compose to zero, they compose to zero up to a homotopy $\vartheta\colon G_{\ell+1}\rightarrow R_{\ell\minus 1}$. This homotopy is a morphism depicted in \eqref{eq:diagram_homology_from_resolutions} where $p_{\ell\minus 1}^1\circ \vartheta=f_\ell\circ f_{\ell+1}$. This motivates the definition of the following morphism:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:image_morphism}
\begin{pmatrix}f_{\ell+1}\\ \vartheta\end{pmatrix}\colon G_{\ell+1}\rightarrow \text{ker}\begin{pmatrix}f_\ell & p_{\ell\minus 1}^1\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}
representing $\partial_{\ell+1}$. Since 
\begin{equation*}
\begin{pmatrix}f_\ell & p_{\ell\minus 1}^1\end{pmatrix}\circ\begin{pmatrix}f_{\ell+1}\\ \vartheta\end{pmatrix}=f_\ell\circ f_{\ell+1}+p_{\ell\minus 1}^1\circ \vartheta
\end{equation*}
we see that $\vartheta=0$ will not make the composition vanish if $f_\ell\circ f_{\ell+1}\neq 0$. In the example we considered (Figure~\ref{fig:HomologyExample}), this homotopy for $f_1\circ f_2$ can be chosen as
\begin{equation*}
\vartheta=
\begin{blockarray}{cc}
& g^{x_6} \\
\begin{block}{c(c)}
r^{x_3} & 1 \\
r^{x_4} & 0 \\
\end{block}
\end{blockarray}
\end{equation*}
sending the triangle to a relation annihilating its boundary. 
We now combine \eqref{eq:kernel_relations} and \eqref{eq:image_morphism} to represent $\text{ker }\partial_\ell/\text{im }\partial_{\ell\minus 1}$. Let $\pi\colon \text{ker }\partial_\ell\rightarrow \text{ker }\partial_\ell/\text{im }\partial_{\ell+1}$ be the projection to the cokernel.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:homology_quotient}
The following sequence is exact:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}[ampersand replacement=\&,every label/.append style = {font = \small}]
0 \&[-5pt] \text{ker }\partial_\ell/\text{im }\partial_{\ell+1} \arrow[l] \&[25pt] \text{ker}\begin{pmatrix}f_\ell & p_{\ell\minus 1}^1\end{pmatrix} \arrow{l}[swap]{\pi\circ\begin{pmatrix}\alpha_\ell & 0\end{pmatrix}} \&[50pt] G_{\ell+1}\oplus R_\ell\oplus RR_{\ell\minus 1}  \arrow{l}[swap]{\begin{pmatrix}f_{\ell+1} & p^1_{\ell} & 0 \\  \vartheta & \gamma & p^2_{\ell\minus 1}\end{pmatrix}}   .
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
\end{proposition}


\noindent
Proposition \ref{prop:homology_quotient} implies that $\text{ker }\partial_\ell/\text{im }\partial_{\ell+1}$ is ismorphic to the cokernel of the matrix build from \eqref{eq:kernel_relations} and \eqref{eq:image_morphism}. Therefore, we obtain the following theorem.

\begin{theorem} \label{thm:pirep_main_result}
The following diagram
\begin{equation}\label{eq:projective_complex_homology}
\begin{tikzcd}[ampersand replacement=\&,every label/.append style = {font = \small}]
G_{\ell\minus 1} \&[25pt] G_{\ell}\oplus R_{\ell\minus 1} \arrow{l}[swap]{\begin{pmatrix}f_\ell & p^1_{\ell\minus 1}\end{pmatrix}} \&[50pt] G_{\ell+1}\oplus R_\ell\oplus RR_{\ell\minus 1} \arrow{l}[swap] {\begin{pmatrix}f_{\ell+1} & p^1_{\ell} & 0 \\  \vartheta & \gamma & p^2_{\ell\minus 1} \end{pmatrix}}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
is a projective implicit representation of $\text{ker }\partial_\ell/\text{im }\partial_{\ell+1}$.    
\end{theorem}

\noindent
Computing a presentation of the homology from a PiRep over a general poset is not trivial but there are available algorithms for this task. For completeness we will discuss this part in Appendix \ref{app:homology_presentation}.

In our example, we obtain the following PiRep of $H_1(K)$:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large,ampersand replacement=\&,every label/.append style = {font = \small}]
G_0 \&[110pt] G_1\oplus R_0 \arrow{l}[swap]{\begin{blockarray}{cccccc}
& g^{x_0}_{uw} & g^{x_1}_{uv} & g^{x_2}_{vw} & r^{x_3} & r^{x_4} \\
\begin{block}{c(ccccc)}
g^{x_0}_{u} & 1 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
g^{x_0}_{w} & 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\
g^{x_1}_{v} & 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\
g^{x_2}_{v} & 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\    
\end{block}
\end{blockarray}} \&[80pt] G_2\oplus RR_0 \arrow{l}[swap]{\begin{blockarray}{cccccc}
& g^{x_6}_{uvw} & rr^{x_5} & rr^{x_6} \\
\begin{block}{c(ccccc)}
g^{x_0}_{uw} & 1 & 0 & 0 \\
g^{x_1}_{uv} & 1 & 0 & 0 \\
g^{x_2}_{vw} & 1 & 0 & 0 \\
r^{x_3} & 1 & 1 & 1 \\    
r^{x_4} & 0 & 1 & 1 \\   
\end{block}
\end{blockarray}}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
In this case, the kernel is already projective and we obtain the following presentation: 
\begin{equation*}\small
\begin{pmatrix}f_{2} & p^1_{1} & 0 \\ h & g & p^2_{0}\end{pmatrix}\vert_{\text{ker}\begin{pmatrix} f_1 & p^1_{0}\end{pmatrix}}=
\begin{blockarray}{cccc}
& g^{x_6}_{uvw} & rr^{x_5} & rr^{x_6} \\
\begin{block}{c(ccc)}
r^{x_3} & 1 & 1 & 1 \\
r^{x_4} & 0 & 1 & 1 \\
\end{block}
\end{blockarray}
\simeq
\begin{blockarray}{cccc}
& rr^{x_5} & g^{x_6}_{uvw} & rr^{x_6} \\
\begin{block}{c(ccc)}
r^{x_3} & 1 & 1 & 0 \\
r^{x_4} & 1 & 0 & 1 \\
\end{block}
\end{blockarray}
\end{equation*}
of the homology. It is generated by the two triangular cycles coming in $x_3$ and $x_4$ in Figure \ref{fig:HomologyExample} which get identified in $x_5$ and killed in $x_6$ by the triangle. 


\subsection{Leveraging graphs for computing $\gamma$ and $\vartheta$} \label{subsec:graph_equations}

To construct a PiRep of the persistent homology of a poset tower, as established in the previous section, the only missing ingredients in the block-matrices in \eqref{eq:projective_complex_homology} are the matrices for $\gamma$ and $\vartheta$. The morphisms $\gamma$ and $\vartheta$ need to satisfy $p_{\ell\minus 1}^1\circ \gamma=f_\ell\circ p_\ell^1$ and $p_{\ell\minus 1}^1\circ \vartheta=f_\ell\circ f_{\ell+1}$, respectively. Thus, we have to solve these equations for $\gamma$ and $\vartheta$. To solve them efficiently we will make use of the special structure of $p_{\ell\minus 1}^1$.

Consider a diagram of projective modules:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:projective_object_2}
\begin{tikzcd}
U \arrow[d,swap,"B"] \arrow[dr,dashed,"X"] \\
Q  & R \arrow[l,"A",two heads]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
where $A$ is an epimorphism. It is a defining property of projective modules that there exists a morphism $X$ such that $A\circ X=B$. We call $X$ a lift of $B$ along $A$. Suppose $Q=\bigoplus_{i=1}^n \text{Proj}[x_i]$, $U=\bigoplus_{i=1}^m \text{Proj}[y_i]$, $R=\bigoplus_{i=1}^s \text{Proj}[z_i]$. By Proposition \ref{prop:elementary_projective}, the vector space $\text{Hom}_{\mathbb{Z}_2}(U,R)$ can be described as $\{(X_{ij})_{i,j}\in \mathbb{Z}_2^{s\times m}\vert X_{ij}=0\text{ if }z_i\nleq y_j\}$. Using an analogous matrix representation $(A_{ij})_{i,j}$ of $A$, we obtain the matrix representation of the composition by matrix multiplication: $(A\circ X)_{ij}=\sum_{k=1}^s A_{ik}X_{kj}$. Therefore, a lift $X$ of $B$ along $A$ has to satisfy:
\begin{equation} \label{eq:matrix_equations}
(A\circ X)_{ij}=\sum_{k=1}^s A_{ik}X_{kj}=B_{ij} \hspace{3pt},\hspace{5pt} \forall\hspace{2pt} 1\leq i \leq n \hspace{1pt}, \hspace{2pt}1\leq j\leq m.
\end{equation}
This is just a system of linear equations with variables $(X_{kj})_{kj}$ with the additional constraint that $X_{kj}=0$ if $z_k\nleq y_j$. The solution does not have to be unique, but any solution of this system determines a valid lift $X$. The fact that $U$ is projective implies that a solution satisfying the linear system and the constraints on the variables exists. For the morphisms $\gamma$ and $\vartheta$ in \eqref{eq:diagram_homology_from_resolutions} we make the following observations: Since $\alpha_{\ell\minus 1}\circ f_\ell\circ p_\ell^1=\partial_\ell\circ\alpha_\ell\circ p_\ell^1=0$ we get that $\text{im }f_\ell\circ p_\ell^1\subseteq \text{ker }\alpha_{\ell\minus 1}=\text{im }p_{\ell\minus 1}^1$. Therefore we obtain:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}
R_\ell \arrow[d,swap,"f_\ell\circ p_\ell^1"] \arrow[dr,dashed,"\gamma"] \\
\text{im }p_{\ell\minus 1} & R_{\ell\minus 1} \arrow[l,"p_{\ell\minus 1}^1",two heads]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
and $\gamma$ is the lift of $f_\ell\circ p_\ell^1$ along the epimorphism $p_{\ell\minus 1}^1$. Analogously, since $\alpha_{\ell\minus 1}\circ f_\ell\circ f_{\ell+1}=\partial_\ell\circ\partial_{\ell+1}\circ \alpha_{\ell+1}=0$ we get that $\text{im }f_{\ell}\circ f_{\ell+1}\subseteq\text{ker }\alpha_{\ell\minus 1}=\text{im }p_{\ell\minus 1}^1$. Therefore we obtain:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}
G_{\ell+1} \arrow[d,swap,"f_\ell\circ f_{\ell+1}"] \arrow[dr,dashed,"\vartheta"] \\
\text{im }p_{\ell\minus 1} & R_{\ell\minus 1} \arrow[l,"p_{\ell\minus 1}^1",two heads]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
and $\vartheta$ is the lift of $f_\ell\circ f_{\ell+1}$ along the epimorphism $p_{\ell\minus 1}^1$. We conclude that both the construction of $\gamma$ and $\vartheta$ fall into the general scheme outlined above for computing the lift of a morphism from a projective module along an epimorphism.

To summarize, we have established that to compute a matrix representation of $\gamma$ or $\vartheta$ we have to solve a system of linear equations $AX=B$ with the constraints that $X_{ij}=0$ if $z_i\nleq y_j$ and we know that a solution exists. Since $AX=A(X_1,\ldots,X_m)=(AX_1,\ldots,AX_m)=(B_1,\ldots,B_m)=B$, this is equivalent to solving $m$ linear equations with coefficient matrix $A$, a vector $X_j$ of unknown variables, and a right-hand-side vector $B_j$. Moreover, when we solve $AX_j=B_j$, we can account for the constraints $X_{ij}=0$ by ignoring the corresponding variable $X_{ij}$ from $X_i$ and the corresponding $j$th column from $A$ while solving the reduced system $A'X'_j=B_j$. Adding the constraint variables $X_{ij}=0$ to $X'_j$ we get a solution of $AX_j=B_j$. Thus, the problem can be reduced to solving $m$ ordinary systems of linear equations. Solving such a system takes cubic time in general, but in our case the coefficient matrix $A=p_{\ell\minus 1}^1$ has a special structure. By Theorem \ref{thm:graph_presentation_matrix}, we obtain $p_{\ell\minus 1}^1$ from a matrix $\overline{p}_{\ell\minus 1}^1$ by removing a distinguished row $\Omega$ where $\overline{p}_{\ell\minus 1}^1$ is the boundary matrix of a $P$-filtered graph. This means that our coefficient matrix $A$ has either one or two non-zero entries per column and its columns are labeled by the edges in the $P$-filtered graph. Since different edges can appear at different incomparable grades, there might be multiple edges between two vertices. We show that a system of linear equations with such a coefficient matrix can be solved in linear time. \\

\noindent
\textbf{Solving a system of equations with multigraph coefficients.} Suppose $A$ is a matrix that has either one or two non-zero entries per column. We can write $A=(T,S)$ where $T$ contains all the columns with exactly two non-zero entries and $S$ contains all the columns with exactly one non-zero entry. This means that $T$ is the incidence matrix of a multigraph $G$ where multiple edges between two vertices are allowed. Our goal is to solve a system of linear equations $Ax=b$ where we assume that $b\in\text{im }A$ or, in other words, the system has a solution. We start by computing a spanning forest $F$ of $G$. Let $T'$ be the incidence matrix of $F$, then the columns of $T'$ are a subset of the columns of $T$. The matrices $T$ and $T'$ have the same number of rows (the vertices of $G$ and $F$). Every edge that is not in the spanning forest closes a cycle and, thus, the corresponding column is a linear combination of edges in $F$. Hence, $\text{im }T=\text{im }T'$. Since the columns of $T$ do not add anything to the image of $T'$, we can drop all variables from $x$ that correspond to columns that are not in $T'$. In that way, we obtain a new system $A'x'=b$ with $A'=(T',S)$. Since $b\in\text{im }A=\text{im }A'$, this system also has a solution and any such solution yields a solution $Ax=b$ by extending $x'$ by zeros. It is therefore sufficient to solve $A'x'=b$. Since every column in $S$ has a unique non-zero entry, it is uniquely associated with a row of $A$ (or a vertex in $F$), the matrix $A'$ is a block-diagonal matrix where the blocks $T_1,\ldots,T_q$ are the incidence matrices of the connected components of $F$ with the additional columns from $S$ associated to the vertices in the component. Each connected component of $F$ is a tree. The block-diagonal structure of $A'$ implies that we can reduce the problem of solving $A'x'=b$ to solving the systems $T_ix_i=b_i$ where $T_i$ is the incidence matrix of a tree with some additional columns that have a single non-zero entry. 
The variables $x_i$ in $x'$ correspond to edges in $T_i$ and to the columns of $S$ associated with
the vertices in $T_i$. The entries
$b_i$ in $b$ correspond to the vertices in $T_i$.


We now focus on solving a system $Ax=b$ where $A=(T,S)$, $T$ is the incidence matrix of a tree with $n$ vertices and the columns of $S$ have a single non-zero entry. The tree $T$ has $n-1$ edges and no cycles, thus, the columns of $T$ are linearly independent and $T$ has rank $n-1$. A column of $S$ cannot be expressed as a linear combination of columns in $T$. This is because any column corresponding to an edge of $T$ has two entries corresponding to vertices and vertices in a sum of edges can only cancel pairwise, resulting in an even number of vertices in the sum. Therefore, adding any column $s$ of $S$ to $T$ yields a matrix $(T,s)$ of maximal rank $n$ implying that  remaining columns of $S$ do not add anything to the image. We can therefore discard all but one columns of $S$ and restrict the problem to $T'x'=b$ where $T'=(T,s)$ and $x'$ is the restriction of the variables to the columns in $T'$. A solution of this system yields a solution of $Tx=b$ by extending $x'$ by zeros. \\

\noindent
\textbf{Solving a system with tree coefficients:} Let $Tx=b$ be a linear system such that $b\in\text{im }T$ and $T$ is the incidence matrix of a tree that has at most one additional column $s$ with a single non-zero entry. We start by computing a "leaf-order" of the vertices of $T$. A leaf order is an order $v_1,\ldots,v_n$ of the vertices of $T$ such that $v_i$ is a leaf of $T\setminus\{v_1,\ldots,v_{i\minus 1}\}$ for all $1\leq i\leq n$. Moreover, we choose the leaf order in such a way that if $w$ is the vertex associated with the column $s$ in $T$, it comes last in the leaf-order. Because any tree has at least two leaves, this is always possible. 

A leaf-order can be computed in linear time in the following way: Given a tree $T$ with one distinguished vertex $w$ (the one associated with the column $s$). Compute the degree of each vertex, set $\text{deg}(w)=\text{deg}(w)+1$ and add all vertices of degree one to a queue. Initially, the vertices of degree one are leaves of $T\setminus \{s\}$. While the queue is non-empty, iteratively remove the vertices from the queue. If a vertex is removed from the queue, update the degree of its neighbors. If the degree of a neighbor becomes one, add it to the queue. The order in which the vertices are removed from the queue is the leaf-order. The vertex $w$ comes last in the order since we artificially increased its degree by one initially, and it only becomes a degree one vertex if it is the last remaining vertex in $T$ after removing all the leaves according to the leaf-order. 

Suppose $v_1,\ldots,v_n$ is a leaf-order of $T$. Then the row of $v_1$ in the incidence matrix $T$ has only one non-zero entry in column $e_1$ and we immediately get $x_{e_1}=b_{v_1}$. Analogously, $v_2$ is a leaf in $T\setminus \{v_1\}$. Its row either has a single non-zero entry in column $e_2$, in which case we get $x_{e_2}=b_{v_2}$, or it is also incident to $v_1$, in which case we get $x_{e_2}=b_{v_2}-x_{e_1}$. We proceed this way to obtain a solution $x$ of the entire system. Every vertex $v_j$ in the order is a leaf in $T_j\coloneqq T\setminus\{v_1,\ldots,v_{j\minus 1}\}$ which implies that there is only one edge incident to $v_j$ whose variable value is not yet determined and we can straightforwardly substitute the right value $x_{e_j}=b_{v_j}-\underset{v_j<e\notin T_j}{\sum}x_{e}$. Note that each edge in $T$ appears at most once in the sums $\underset{v_j<e\notin T_j}{\sum}x_{e}$. So computing the values $x_{e_j}$ cumulatively only takes time linear in the size of $T$. Hence, the entire process takes linear time. In other words,
this procedure allows us to solve a system $Ax=b$ where $A$ has at most two non-zero entries per column in time linear in the number of columns and rows. 

\begin{proposition} \label{prop:complexity_multigraph_equation}
Let $Ax=b$ be a linear system of equations such that $b\in\text{im }A$, and $A\in \mathbb{Z}_2^{n\times m}$ has at most two non-zero entries per column. Then we can solve this system in $O(n+m)$ time. 
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
We can write $A=(T,S)$ where $T$ is the incidence matrix of a multigraph $G$ and $S$ has exactly one non-zero entry per column. We first compute a spanning forest of $G$. This can be done in $O(n+m)$ time. Let $T_1,\ldots,T_q$ be the submatrices of $A$, consisting of the components of the spanning forest and the columns of $S$ associated with the vertices of the components, where $T_i$ has $n_i$ vertices and $\sum_{i=1}^q n_i=n$. After removing all but one column with a single non-zero entry from every $T_i$, we obtain matrices $T'_1,\ldots,T'_q$ with $n_i$ rows and at most $n_i$ columns. We now use the method outlined above and solve the equations $T'_ix'_i=b_i$ in $O(n_i)$ time. Hence, solving all these equations takes $O(\sum_{i=1}^q n_i)=O(n)$ time and the overall complexity is $O(n+m)$.
\end{proof}

\noindent
We can now combine all the above results to conclude that
$\gamma$ and $\vartheta$ can be computed in time quadratic in the size of the input matrices.

\begin{theorem} \label{thm:complexity_computing_gh}
Given a presentation of the chain complex $C_\bullet(K)$ as computed by the algorithm {\sc Presentation} and depicted in \eqref{eq:diagram_homology_from_resolutions}, a matrix representation of $\gamma$ and $\vartheta$ can be computed in $O(n^2t_0^2)$ time. 
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By the analysis in Section \ref{subsec:complexity_analysis}, $p_\ell^1$ is of size $O(n)\times O(t_0n)$, $f_\ell$ is of size $O(n)\times O(n)$, $\gamma$ is of size $O(nt_0)\times O(nt_0)$ and $\vartheta$ is of size $O(nt_0)\times O(n)$ for all $\ell$. We start with the computation of $\gamma$. We set $A=p_{\ell\minus 1}^1$, $X=\gamma$, $B=f_\ell\circ p_\ell^1$ and $k=nt_0$. We assume that the matrix representations of $f_\ell$ and $p_\ell^1$ are given for all $\ell$. They can be computed in $O(nt)$ time where $t$ is $O(t_0^2)$. Since $p_\ell^1$ has at most two non-zero entries per column, computing the matrix product $f_\ell\circ p_{\ell}^1$ takes $O(n^2t_0)$ time. We now have to solve $AX=(AX_1,\ldots,AX_k)=(B_1,\ldots,B_k)=B$. After dropping the columns corresponding to constrained variables from $X_i$, each system $A'X'_i=B_i$ becomes an ordinary system of linear equations where $A'$ is the incidence matrix of a multigraph with $O(n)$ vertices and $O(k)$ edges. By Proposition \ref{prop:complexity_multigraph_equation}, we can solve each system in $O(n+k)$ time. Hence, we can solve $AX=B$ in $O\big(k(n+k)\big)$ time. For the computation of $\vartheta$ we set $B=f_\ell\circ f_{\ell+1}$ and $X=\vartheta$. To obtain $B$, we compute the matrix product $f_\ell\circ f_{\ell+1}$. By construction of $f_{\ell+1}$, every generator of an $\ell$-simplex is mapped to exactly one generator of each of its boundary simplices. Therefore, every column of $f_{\ell+1}$ has exactly $\ell+2$ non-zero entries. Since we assume that $\ell$ is a constant, this matrix product can be computed in $O(n^2)$ time. Now we have to solve $AX=(AX_1,\ldots,AX_n)=(B_1,\ldots,B_n)=B$ where $A$ is similar to the computation of $\gamma$. In this case, we obtain a complexity of $O\big(n(n+k)\big)$. Therefore, using $k=nt_0$, we get that  $\gamma$ and $\vartheta$ can be computed in $O(n^2t_0^2)$ time.
\end{proof}


\begin{corollary} \label{col:pirep_complexity}
Let $K\colon P\rightarrow\mathbf{SCpx}$ be a poset tower, where $P$ is represented by a directed graph of size $t=t_0+t_1$ (number vertices and edges) and $K$ is represented by a set of simplex generators $\mathcal{S}$ and edge events $\mathcal{C}$ of size $\vert\mathcal{S}\vert+\vert\mathcal{C}\vert=n$. The algorithm {\sc Presentation} and the methods discussed above allow us to compute a \emph{PiRep} of $H_\ell(K)$ in $O(nt\log n+n^2t+n^2t_0^2)=O(n^2t_0^2)$ time. 
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
This follows directly from Theorem \ref{thm:algorithm_complexity}, \ref{thm:pirep_main_result}, and \ref{thm:complexity_computing_gh}.
\end{proof}


\section{Conclusion} \label{sec:conclusion}

We developed an efficient algorithm to compute minimal presentations of persistence modules $C_\bullet(K)$ and boundary morphisms $\partial_\bullet$ arising from poset towers. The algorithm can also efficiently compute an asymptotically minimal projective resolution up to the second term. We achieve this by exploiting the special structure of $C_\ell(K)$ in simplicial settings.

Given these resolutions, we also provide an algorithm to efficiently compute a projective implicit representation (PiRep) of the persistent homology of the poset tower. Given this representation by graded matrices, we can use existing methods to compute a minimal presentation of the homology.

We hope that the results and algorithms in this work will be used as a basis for the development of efficient algorithms for persistence computations over specific posets in the future. 

An interesting problem for future work is not only computing a PiRep at a certain degree, but extending it to construct an entire chain complex of projective modules quasi-isomorphic to the initial complex $C_\bullet(K)$. 


