\vspace{-0.4cm}
\subsection{Related Works}\label{sec:RelatedWork}
Linear Temporal Logic (LTL) serves as a foundational specification for specifying system behaviors in cyber-physical systems (CPS), capturing the relative order of events to guide system design, development, and verification \cite{23}. As
a highly logical and structured language, LTL is widely applied in model checking \cite{42} and formal verification \cite{25}, with practical use cases spanning program verification \cite{43,kuzmin2015construction,neyzov2023ltl}, reactive systems \cite{44,abraham2021symbolic,luttenberger2020practical}, hardware design \cite{45}, security protocols \cite{46,guedj2012bsp}, and autonomous driving \cite{52,qi2024risk,rong2020safe}. Recent advancements have extended LTL to task planning and automation, enabling correct-by-construction controller synthesis \cite{zhong2023formal,zhong2023automata}, robot and vehicle control \cite{47,48,51}, and industrial automation \cite{53}. In the context of AI, LTL is increasingly used to define complex task objectives in reinforcement learning \cite{54,55} and to coordinate interactions between multi-agent systems \cite{49,50}, highlighting its versatility across domains.


Despite the potential critical role LTL is playing in safety-critical CPSs, formulating desired tasks as LTL formulas is an error-prone and time-consuming manual task typically requiring expert knowledge.
In practice, desired tasks are often described in unstructured language—informative yet ambiguous and syntactically unbounded—driving research on automatic translation into formal language. In this context, most existing works focus on natural language \cite{1}, leading to extensive research on grammar-based approaches to handle this transition \cite{2,3}. To improve efficiency, methods leveraging machine translation have been proposed \cite{8,9}, and the advent of deep learning has significantly accelerated this process. Neural networks have been introduced into this domain, with works like DeepSTL proposing models to translate English requirements into Signal Temporal Logic \cite{10} and fine-tuning language models to refine the LTL-transition process \cite{11}. With the rise of large language models, researchers have uncovered their potential in facilitating this transition \cite{12,13,14}.


Beyond efficiency, another key focus of this transition lies in maintaining semantic consistency. 
Techniques such as synthesis methods to detect inconsistencies among LTL-formulated requirements have been proposed \cite{15}. 
Interactive approaches, particularly involving human-in-the-loop adjustments, are also prevalent \cite{16,17}. 
Deep learning approaches also incorporate data augmentation to improve robustness \cite{14}, and we adopt the six-step data generation
method from \cite{14} to enhance naturalness and semantic richness in our framework. However, unlike \cite{14}, our focus is on ensuring safety
compliance of the generated LTL specifications—a challenge given the lack of formal guarantees in LLM-generated
specifications in previous works, which conflict with the safety-critical nature of CPS.
\vspace{-0.5cm}



% We also recognized the potential of LLMs in the fields of formal methods and LTL generation, incorporating them into our framework as a driving force to achieve automated LTL generation, semantic assurance, and safety verification.
\vspace{-0.1cm}
\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{Framework.png}
    \vspace{-0.3cm}
    \caption{Overview of \texttt{AutoSafeLTL} Framework, with detailed introduction in Section~\ref{sec3}.}
    \label{fig:label}
\end{figure}
\vspace{-0.7cm}

\vspace{-0.4cm}
\subsection{Contribution}\label{sec:Contribution}

In this paper, we introduce \texttt{AutoSafeLTL}, a novel framework (as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:label}), which leverages the generative capabilities of large language models (LLMs) for automatic safety-compliant LTL specification generation. 
Concretely, consider descriptions of the environment (referred to as \emph{Environmental Information}) and the corresponding desired tasks given in (unstructured) natural languages,  the goal is to generate LTL specifications that
not only capture the  \emph{Desired Task} for later usage, e.g., trajectory planning, controller synthesis, etc., 
but also adhere to a set of predefined safety restrictions, formulated as \emph{Base Rules} in our framework. Unlike traditional approaches \cite{8,11,12,13,14}, which typically detect safety violations late in the development process, \texttt{AutoSafeLTL} integrates a self-supervised verification loop to automatically refine and validate the generated specifications during generation. It uses formal language inclusion checks to detect violations in the early stage, interpret counterexamples, suggest corrections, and iteratively verify the modified LTL specification until all safety restrictions are satisfied. To the best of our knowledge, we are the first to address the safety-compliance problem in automatic LTL specification generation with formal safety-compliance guarantees, which highlights both the novelty and necessity of our work.



The key innovation of \texttt{AutoSafeLTL} is its self-supervised automatic verification mechanism, and the technical contributions of our paper are fourfold.
\vspace{-0.5cm}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item We develop a self-supervised and extensible verification pipeline to automatically detect and correct safety violations in LTL specifications via an automata-based language inclusion check, ensuring safety-compliant guarantees over the generated LTL specifications, fostering synergy between between AI community and rigorous formal verification methodologies.
    \item We show the existence of counterexample paths when safety-compliance violations occur and propose an efficient method to construct such paths, with a complexity analysis. 
    These counterexample paths serve as key enablers for a violation-guided automatic correction procedure.
    \item We introduce two dedicated LLM agents to bridge semantic gaps and support automatic correction: 1)\emph{LLM-as-an-Aligner} resolves atomic proposition mismatches, while 2)\emph{LLM-as-a-Critic} interprets counterexample path into actionable revision feedback, enabling traceable and iterative correction toward safety compliance.
    \item We construct a tailored benchmark dataset to evaluate the safety compliance of LTL specifications derived from natural language, focusing on unstructured navigation-related instructions. Compared to existing datasets like SYNTCOMP \cite{SYNTCOMP}, ours features human-centered APs and richer temporal structures, enabling more realistic and challenging evaluation over natural-language-guided tasks.
\end{enumerate}
\vspace{-0.15cm}
Through rigorous experimentation, we demonstrate that the proposed framework provides a scalable and robust solution for bridging the gap between informal requirements and safety-compliant formal specifications in CPS.

