\section{The Framework}\label{sec3}

\subsection{Overview}
%Provide a high-level overview of the self-supervised framework, explaining its purpose, architecture, and how it integrates multimodal information and LLMs for automated LTL generation and verification.
%(Figure)

The objective of our framework is to generate LTL specifications for describing \emph{Desired Tasks} in (unstructured) natural language using LLMs, while providing \emph{formal compliance guarantees} over the resulting LTL against a set of predefined safety restrictions.
As shown in Fig.\ref{fig:label}, our framework comprises two components: \emph{LTL Extraction} and \emph{Automated Verification}, with LLMs collaborating in each part. Concretely, the framework takes \emph{Desired Tasks} for the CPS and \emph{Environmental Information} in (unstructured) language format as input and invokes the User LLM to extract the desired task in LTL format iteratively. 
The Automated Verification process is divided into two stages: \emph{Syntactic Check} and \emph{Semantic Check}. 
Syntactic Check ensures that LTL conforms to grammatical rules as described in Definition~\ref{def:LTL}.
After passing the Syntactic Check, the  Semantic Check ensures that the properties of state paths expressed by generated LTL are adhere to predefined safety restrictions. % \cite{23}. 
In particular, both the Desired Task in LTL-format and the formalized \emph{Base Rules} (the scenario safety restrictions) are converted into Büchi Automata (BA) format for the Language Inclusion check (referred to as \emph{Inclusion Check} in the remainder of the paper). 
To ensure the output LTL is compliant to the predefined safety restrictions, the principles of feedback and regeneration are integrated throughout the Automated Verification phase and executed collaboratively by both the \emph{LLM-as-an-Aligner} and \emph{LLM-as-a-Critic}. 
