Although Environmental Information is not strictly necessary to generate an initial LTL formula for a Desired Task, it becomes crucial for LLMs to regenerate LTL whenever a violation against safety restrictions occurs. Especially when the original task description is \emph{inherently unsafe}, Environmental Information helps LLMs revise the specification toward a safer alternative. Additionally, the LTL formulas representing these safety restrictions are considered as an \emph{input} of our framework and are assumed to be pre-defined. On one hand, it is reasonable to assume that safety is \emph{well-defined} when aiming to achieve formal safety compliance guarantees. On the other hand, when considering \emph{well-defined} safety restrictions, such as traffic rules, we note some recent works (e.g., \cite{gressenbuch2021predictive,esterle2020formalizing}) that focus on systematically formulating safety restrictions in traffic scenarios as formal languages. 
