\subsubsection{Syntactic Check}
The syntactic check verifies whether the LTL formula complies with the syntax rules (i.e., its grammatical correctness), as described in Definition~\ref{def:LTL}. It involves two main steps: Atomic Proposition Matching and Operator Matching.
For AP Matching, we first extract all AP from the Base Rules and build an AP library. Then, we use the \emph{LLM-as-an-Aligner} to align AP in the Desired Task with those in the library, replacing them with the most similar matches to ensure AP consistency.
For Operator Matching, a token-based algorithm (illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:syntacticcheck}) checks the correctness of operators, as well as proper use of parentheses.
If any syntax errors are found, along with their types and positions, they are sent to the User LLM for correction. Prompts for AP matching and LTL correction are provided in Table~\ref{tbl:APmathing} and Table~\ref{tbl:syntax_correction} in the Appendix.

