\section{Evaluation}\label{sec:eva}
\vspace{-0.2cm}
\subsection{Study Setup}
One of the primary challenges in the NL-to-LTL problem is the lack of sufficient data, as obtaining such data is often expensive and difficult. We consider SYNTCOMP \cite{SYNTCOMP}, a classic benchmark dataset in the domain of LTL. Since AP provide crucial context for LLMs during reprompting, we prioritized more natural, human-centered AP, while SYNTCOMP’s AP are more mathematical and abstract. Additionally, we require an unstructured language dataset for translation tasks. To address this issue, we employ GPT to generate a natural language dataset for testing purposes.
Here, we consider traffic navigation as the testing scenario and generate a corresponding dataset.
Each data entry includes two components: a single traffic navigation instruction and environmental information. 
We generated 50 such entries as the benchmark dataset. The scale of our dataset is: % using several key metrics is:
\vspace{-0.2cm}
\begin{itemize}
\begin{small}
    \item The number of atomic propositions (APs): between 4 to 12, averaging 7.
    \item The number of temporal operators: between 8 to 20, averaging 13.
    \item The number of nested temporal operators: between 2 and 13, averaging 8.
    \end{small}
\end{itemize}
\vspace{-0.2cm}
Here, we provide three randomly selected examples in Table~\ref{benchmark} in the Appendix.
Furthermore, we consider the Base Rule mentioned in the \textit{example 1} to evaluate and compare with the existing NL2LTL tool \cite{16}. 
Since our framework aims to generate LTL specifications that comply with predefined safety restrictions, we used the violation rate (i.e., percentage of output LTL violating the Base Rules) as the primary evaluation metric. 

We evaluate both the initial (before Automated Verification process) and output LTLs (after Automated Verification process), along with the number of iterations needed for modification. For efficiency, LTLs requiring over 25 iterations are treated as non-outputs, and we compared the average iteration count required for compliance across methods.

For our implementation, we utilize \begin{small}\texttt{gpt-4-32k}\end{small} as the underlying model for both the User LLM and Agent LLMs in our framework. We compare our approach with the existing LTL extraction tool \begin{small}\texttt{nl2spec}\end{small} \cite{16} using its default configuration, that is, adopting the \texttt{GPT-3.5-turbo} model, selecting the \begin{small}\texttt{minimal}\end{small} mode, and setting \begin{small}\texttt{Number of tries}\end{small} to three times. 
It is worth noting that since the natural language Desired Task does not always have a logical structure, the conversion efficiency of \begin{small}\texttt{nl2spec}\end{small} for these data is very low, namely less than 5\% Desired Tasks in our data set can be translated to a valid LTL with \begin{small}\texttt{nl2spec}\end{small}.
Therefore, in order to better utilize \begin{small}\texttt{nl2spec}\end{small} to evaluate our work, we called on \begin{small}\texttt{GPT-4o}\end{small} to re-describe the Desired Tasks in our dataset. 
The natural language format Desired Tasks with enhanced logic are used as the input for \begin{small}\texttt{nl2spec}\end{small}.
Furthermore, we also conduct ablation experiments to validate the necessity of the two Agent LLMs in our proposed framework.
\vspace{-0.4cm}
\subsection{Results}
\vspace{-0.4cm}
\subsubsection{Quality of Initial Translation.}\label{section4.1}
First, we evaluate the violation rate of the Initial LTL that has \emph{not} yet undergone the Automated Verification process. 
The benchmark dataset was processed using \texttt{nl2spec} \cite{16} and our framework’s LTL Extraction component to generate Initial LTLs. 
A single Inclusion Check was then performed: LTLs classified as \emph{Included} were considered compliant, while those marked \emph{Not Included} were deemed non-compliant.
Since prior research in LTL generation has primarily focused on syntactic correctness and semantic consistency, compliance has remained an overlooked aspect. As a result, \texttt{nl2spec} does not generate any compliant Initial LTLs, while our LTL Extraction component produced only one safety-compliant Initial LTL.
This result underscores the necessity of our framework, as it addresses a critical yet unresolved challenge in ensuring compliance—an essential aspect of safety-critical systems.
\vspace{-0.4cm}
\subsubsection{Quality of Verified Translation.}\label{section4.2}
Next, we evaluate the safety assurance component of our framework, namely the Automated Verification module. 
Here, an LTL formula is considered compliant if the final output after iterative modifications was \emph{Included}; otherwise, it was classified as non-compliant. 
Additionally, we measure the number of modification iterations required to achieve compliance as a supplementary efficiency metric.

We input the Initial LTLs (generated separately by our LTL Extraction component and \texttt{nl2spec}) into the Automated Verification module to assess their violations in the final output.
To further validate our approach, we conduct ablation experiments by selectively removing \emph{LLM-as-a-Aligner} (responsible for AP matching) and \emph{LLM-as-a-Critic} (responsible for counterexample analysis), demonstrating the necessity of our incentive-based methodology.
