Overall, through Inclusion Check and iterative refinement mechanisms, our framework ensures full compliance, achieving a final violation rate of 0\% for successfully generated LTLs. 
Comparison with other tools further validate the effectiveness of the Automated Verification component, while ablation experiments confirmed that incorporating two Agent LLMs improves framework efficiency in terms of the number of iterations for generating safety-compliant LTL.
Therefore, our approach provides verified safety-compliance with formal guarantees for the generated LTLs, making it a practical and reliable solution for safety-critical applications.
\vspace{-0.4cm}
\subsubsection{Quality of Translation consistency.}
While our primary objective is to ensure safety-compliant LTL generation, it is worth noting that our framework also maintains strong semantic alignment with the original natural language instructions, achieving high-quality translation while ensuring safety compliance. Concretely, we adopted an evaluation methodology similar to \texttt{nl2spec} and achieved an 88\% semantic consistency between the output LTL and the original natural language instructions. The remaining variations typically stem from: 1) Logical adjustments between task units based on safety restrictions, 2) Simplifications of redundant information and background details, and 3) Additions of task units to enforce safety restrictions.

{\bf Further discussion for the experimental results} Due to tool instability (including RABIT crashes and SPOT conversion limits) and the inherent property of some datas (where no safety-compliant LTL specification exists under the given Base Rule), not all 50 benchmark samples yield outputs within 25 iterations. Specifically, \texttt{AutoSafeLTL} successfully generated 36 outputs, whereas \texttt{AutoSafeLTL-C} and \texttt{AutoSafeLTL-D} exhibited a high number of cases where iterations exceeded 25, leading to both effective output rates of approximately 5\%. 
Additionally, \texttt{nl2spec} based experiments face conversion failures during the transformation of Desired Tasks into LTL due to limitations in \texttt{nl2spec} itself, further reducing the final number of valid outputs. 


%During our experiments, OpenAI released API access to the latest \texttt{GPT-4} model. We promptly integrated it into our framework and adopted it for our primary experiments. Compared to earlier experiments, the updated model reveals notable improvements in reasoning and correction, demonstrating that more advanced LLMs can further enhance the potential of our approach.



\vspace{-0.4cm}
\section{Conclusion}
We propose \texttt{AutoSafeLTL}, a self-supervised framework that integrates formal verification tools and LLMs to provide safety-compliance guarantees for LTL generation, addressing the unexplored  LTL safety-compliance issue. 
The core of \texttt{AutoSafeLTL} is a structured verification pipeline that integrates LLM-driven LTL generation with formal compliance checks, ensuring strict adherence to safety specifications. 
To enhance the semantic alignment between generated LTLs and safety restrictions, we employ an AP matching method, which also improves verification efficiency. 
Additionally, we introduce an automatic prompting scheme to refine the LLM’s understanding of verification results and correction mechanisms, facilitating an iterative regeneration process. 
Experimental results demonstrate that our approach effectively provides LTL safety-compliance guarantees, achieving fully safety-compliant outputs with zero violations.


Our framework inherently does not rely on any specific tools, but its current performance is influenced by the limitations of existing inclusion check implementation tools, as discussed in Section~\ref{section4.2}. This also highlights a direction for future work—we plan to develop customized inclusion checkers to improve robustness and autonomy. 
Additionally, due to data restrictions, our experiments were conducted in specific scenarios. 
Future research will explore more complex systems and multi-dimensional safety specifications to further expand the applicability of our approach.



