\subsection{Multi-agent Collaboration}
%多智能体协作之间的工作,任务分解
Multiple LLM-based agents can collaborate, e.g., one does scanning, another exploits, another handles exfiltration, to perform a complex attack~\cite{alshehri2024breachseek, bianou2024pentest, kong2025vulnbot}. For example, the Audit-LLM framework for insider threat detection proposed by Song \textit{et al.} in \cite{song2024audit} employs three types of agents to analyze security logs, including planner agents, specialist agents, and analyst agents. %A planner agent first decomposes the task into subtasks—parsing authentication logs and tracking file-access patterns.
% Defenders must therefore watch for coordinated activity across ostensibly unrelated incidents, which may reflect a single LLM-driven campaign.
% A specialist agent next applies bespoke tools to each subtask, for example, detecting anomalies or known IoCs.
% An analyst agent then aggregates these results and expresses potential insider threats in a human-readable form. % First, a planner agent breaks down the problem into smaller sub-tasks, e.g., parsing authentication logs and monitoring file access patterns. Therefore, defenders should monitor signs of coordination from seemingly separate incidents that might actually be parts of one orchestrated campaign driven by collaborating LLM-based agents. % Next, a specialist agent builds or applies bespoke tools to sift through data for each sub-task, for instance, searching for anomalies or known IoCs in the logs. Then, an analyst agent aggregates these findings to conclude potential insider threats, explaining the reasoning in human-readable terms. Importantly, Audit-LLM even uses an internal debate mechanism: two agents with the same role independently analyze the data and then compare results, challenging discrepancies to ensure no critical clue is missed. During multi-agent collaborative, checks-and-balances design dramatically improves accuracy and transparency, as evidenced by promising results on real-world datasets. The success of Audit-LLM underscores how dividing labor among LLM-based agents can harness their collective strengths, where one agent’s output becomes another’s input, and mutual critique reduces errors.
Furthermore, multi-agent cyberattacks can also adopt adversarial or competitive roles as a form of collaboration. For instance, one agent might act as the attacker while another acts as a defender or as a cautious evaluator, effectively red-teaming each other’s strategies. Wang \textit{et al.} in \cite{wang2024reinforcement} explore an RL-driven agent that autonomously attacks other LLM-based systems. In such scenarios, agents can iteratively improve offensive tactics and defensive countermeasures through simulation.% In a coordinated attack setting, we could imagine an exploit agent compromising a system and handing off access to another agent that focuses on lateral movement or exfiltration, each agent communicating through a shared memory or protocol. Preliminary work on multi-agent penetration testing shows that coordinating specialized roles (e.g., one agent finds vulnerabilities, another agent devises exploits, another cleans up traces) can achieve end-to-end penetration with higher success rates and broader coverage than a single agent. The key challenges in multi-agent setups include establishing a common knowledge base for agents to share state, preventing communication overhead or confusion, and assigning credit or blame for learning. Nonetheless, the potential is significant: multi-agent LLM teams can tackle large-scale or multifaceted cyber operations that exceed the cognitive capacity of any single model. By decomposing tasks, assigning roles, and enabling agents to exchange information (or even debate with each other), collaborative agent swarms are pushing the frontier of what autonomous cyberattacks can achieve. This multi-agent paradigm also provides a testbed to study emergent behaviors, for example, whether agents develop unforeseen strategies when cooperating, which is an important consideration for future safety and governance of AI-driven cyber operations.

