\subsection{Main Results}

% As shown in Table~\ref{tab:results}, we observe significant performance differences among the various voice assistants, with no single model consistently outperforming the others across all tasks. Additionally, we found that LLaMA-Omni's ASR performance was suboptimal. However, after careful analysis, we concluded that the issue was not with the ASR system itself, but rather with the model’s limited ability to follow ASR instructions, which often leads to failures in correctly interpreting the ASR outputs. In addition, during the experiment we observed that the audio output of Mini-Omni2 was truncated earlier than the text output, leading to the lowest consistency between text and speech response, as evidenced by the highest word error rate. Based on the results presented in Table~\ref{tab:results}, we draw the following key conclusions.

% Specifically, the LLaMA-Omni(S) row represents the full audio mode input test for LLaMA-Omni, while LLaMA-Omni(T+S) refers to the hybrid input mode, which combines both text and audio. In this hybrid mode, the text input consists of a question, and the model provides a response based on the content of the audio input. This approach aligns with the methodology employed by the original authors of LLaMA-Omni. 

The overall performance of tested speech LLMs is presented in Table~\ref{tab:results}. We observe considerable variation in performance, with no single model consistently outperforming across all tasks.
%We observe significant performance differences, with no single model consistently outperforming across all tasks.

Speech models with larger LLM parameters (7B, 8B) usually outperform smaller models (0.5B) on knowledge, speech understanding and the semantic quality of generated responses. These tasks largely depend on the capabilities preserved from its LLM backbone. The cascade system preserves 58.98\% accuracy on knowledge QA, while the best speech LLM, Freeze-Omni, achieves 27.27\%. LLaMA-Omni is the only model supporting the simultaneous input of both text and speech, but exhibits a performance deficit of 30\% and 50\% in both spoken QA formats compared to the cascade method. A strong performance deterioration is observed when extending to speech modality. Moshi achieves an inferior score, as its streaming modeling results in frequent interruptions and irrelevant responses. 

Most speech LLMs demonstrate comparable performance in speech recognition. The results of Freeze-Omni and GLM-4-Voice are borrowed from their papers, as the intermediate model and specific prompts are not available. LLaMA-Omni could not perform speech recognition, for it does not involve pre-training on the ASR task, resulting in irrelevant responses given ASR instructions. Influenced by the capability of instruction following, the paralinguistic part of speech understanding tasks is also affected. Besides the overall accuracy, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:accuracy}, we simultaneously evaluated the model's answer rate (AR) and conditioned accuracy (CA), with the latter representing the proportion of correct responses among the answered samples. LLaMA-Omni consistently exhibits high performance in instruction following; however, the best model in terms of conditioned accuracy is not the same across the three tasks. Most speech LLMs lack sufficient capability to capture specific paralinguistic information. LLaMA-Omni, Freeze-Omni, and GLM-4-Voice demonstrated clearly above-chance accuracy in gender prediction, while Mini-Omni and GLM-4-Voice showed notable performance in age prediction. Emotion recognition presents a special case for evaluation, as it is also significantly influenced by semantic content. All models demonstrate positive performance on the emotion recognition task, with Mini-Omni correctly identifying nearly half of the emotional labels.
%LLaMA-Omni, Freeze-Omni and GLM-4-Voice demonstrated statistically significant gender prediction accuracy above random guessing, and Mini-Omni and GLM-4-Voice on age prediction. Emotion recognition presents a special case for evaluation, as it is also significantly influenced by semantic content. All models demonstrate positive performance on the emotion recognition task, with Mini-Omni correctly identifying nearly half of the emotional labels.

Regarding the quality of generated speech, Moshi and LLaMA-Omni exhibit strong speech-text consistency. In contrast, Mini-omni2 demonstrates the poorest performance, primarily due to premature truncation occurring during the speech generation process. LLaMA-Omni and GLM-4-Voice exhibit the highest performance in terms of the semantic quality of generated responses. Theoretically, a larger parameter LLM backbone is conducive to generating higher-quality responses, while Moshi once again received a lower score due to an excessive amount of irrelevant output. As for the acoustic quality, Mini-Omni2 achieves the highest score, a little higher than Mini-Omni and LLaMA-Omni. The acoustic performance is affected by the speech token modeling method. 


% \noindent \textbf{Most voice assistants lack sufficient capability to capture paralinguistic information.} The conditioned accuracy column in Table~\ref{tab:Extensive evaluation} represents the accuracy of paralinguistic information extraction, excluding cases where the model fails to recognize the instruction. Only LLaMA-Omni(S) and GLM-4-Voice demonstrated statistically significant gender prediction accuracy above random guessing. Additionally, we observed a slight decline in Mini-Omni2's ability to capture paralinguistic information compared to Mini-Omni. Among the tested models, most were not able to capture all three paralinguistic attributes—emotion, age, and gender—simultaneously. Among the tested assistants, GLM-4-Voice demonstrated the strongest paralinguistic information capture overall, achieving an average accuracy of over 50\% across the three datasets.

% \noindent \textbf{The Cascade model outperforms in text quality.} For knowledge-based tasks, automatic speech recognition (ASR), and language-related tasks, the cascade model significantly outperforms all other voice assistants. At the language model scale, its performance on knowledge tasks far surpasses that of all tested voice assistants. This suggests that while voice assistants benefit from audio modality integration, they may experience a trade-off in knowledge retention and utilization. However, the cascade model has inherent limitations, the most notable being its inability to capture paralinguistic information. This weakness prevents it from fully interpreting speaker attributes such as emotion, age, and gender. Additionally, the model suffers from considerable latency, which restricts its practical applicability in real-time scenarios. Moreover, the experimental results suggest that current voice assistants may not fully utilize the capabilities of their underlying language models, indicating potential for further improvement in their efficiency.

\subsection{Limitations for current speech LLMs}

Based on the experimental results from SOVA-Bench, we summarize the current limitations of speech LLMs as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Limited input modalities: Only LLAMA-Omni supports simultaneous input of both speech and text. However, in voice assistant scenarios, additional text information may be necessary for optimal performance.

    \item Instruction following variability: Most models typically struggle to effectively follow instructions, particularly when dealing with tasks not involved in their training process. 

    \item Severe performance degradation: Introducing speech modalities into LLMs often leads to a marked decline in general knowledge, semantic understanding, and generation capabilities. A critical challenge is to preserve the performance of textual LLMs while supporting speech interactions.
    %Severe performance degradation: Introducing speech modalities into LLMs often results in a significant deterioration in general knowledge, semantic understanding and generation capabilities. A critical challenge is to preserve the performance of textual LLMs while supporting speech interactions.

    \item Unsatisfactory output speech quality: Some speech LLMs exhibit limited speech-text consistency and speech quality. These issues are not only related to the LLM used but also to the speech tokenization and training schemes.
\end{itemize}


\section{Conclusion}
In this paper, we introduce a comprehensive benchmark, SOVA-Bench, for evaluating generative speech LLMs from both semantic and acoustic perspectives. Evaluation experiments on novel speech-interactive models reveal variations in the quality of generated speech responses and a substantial degradation in performance compared to the cascade model, particularly in general knowledge and speech understanding. Considering the deficiencies in unified evaluation standards within the field of speech LLMs, especially voice assistants, SOVA-Bench provides a systematic framework for comparing model performance.
%The evaluation experiments on novel speech interactive models reveal variations in the quality of generated speech responses and a significant performance degradation compared to the cascade model on general knowledge and speech understanding. Considering the deficiencies in unified evaluation standards within the field of speech LLMs, especially voice assistants, SOVA-Bench provides a systematic framework for comparing model performance.


% Our evaluation of major voice assistants reveals that their ability to capture paralinguistic information remains limited. Additionally, the response quality of all tested voice assistants still lags significantly behind that of the cascade model, suggesting that they have yet to fully leverage the capabilities of their underlying language models. Future work should focus on enhancing models' ability to understand paralinguistic information and improving the extent to which voice assistants utilize their language model capabilities.

