A key attribute of sparse matrices that has a direct impact on achieved performance with cuTeSpMM is the non-zero density within non-empty $16 \times 4$ 2D regions in the full 2D index space of the sparse matrix. This is because explicit zero-filling must be used for the remaining elements in order to use the tensor core's matrix-matrix multiplication engine. Since the tensor core's peak performance of Nvidia GPUs for TF32 tends to be about an order of magnitude higher than the performance using the standard scalar ``CUDA cores'', we also assessed the relative performance of cuTeSpMM over cuSparse Blocked-ELL over a subset of the test matrices with non-zero brick density of at least 10\% (this was 133 matrices out of 356). These performance results are presented in Fig.~\ref{fig:perf-ell1}. It may be seen that the fraction of matrices achieving higher performance than cuSparse CSR is much higher. The geometric means of speedup for the subset of matrices with brick nnzero density greater than 10\% is reported in Table ~\ref{tab:geomean-1}. For such matrices, the speedup over cuSparse CSR is always greater than one. The speedup over Blocked-ELL is lower for these matrices because blocked-ELL performance is relatively higher for these denser matrices.

