Suppose $X=\spec B$ and $Y=\spec A$ are two affine schemes. Morphism $f:X\to Y$ is said to be \textbf{free}, if $B$ is a free $A$-module via the associated ring map $f^\#:A\to B$ (see \cite[Page 109]{hartshorne2013algebraic}). For readers familiar with flatness, a finite flat morphism is almost equivalent to a finite free morphism, because a finite module over a noetherian local ring is flat if and only if it is a free module \cite[Exercise 6.2]{eisenbud2013commutative}.

