        \section{Related Work}

        \subsection{Recent Advancements in Selection Operators}
        In recent years, lexicase selection has shown strong empirical performance and is widely adopted in modern symbolic regression systems~\citep{cava2018learning,zhang2025ragsr}. Inspired by lexicase selection, several variants have been developed, including probabilistic lexicase selection~\citep{ding2023probabilistic}, lexicase-like selection via diverse aggregation (DALex)~\citep{ni2024dalex}, $\epsilon$-lexicase selection with dynamic split (D-Split)~\citep{imai2024minimum}, down-sampled lexicase selection~\citep{boldi2024informed}, and random down-sampled tournament selection~\citep{geigerperformance}. These methods share the core idea of emphasizing performance on subsets of training instances to encourage specialization, and they demonstrate advantages in various scenarios~\citep{geigerperformance}. However, these operators are manually designed and do not fully satisfy all the desired properties outlined in \Cref{sec: Operator Design Principle}, highlighting the need for further exploration of selection operators.

        \subsection{Automated Evolutionary Algorithm Design}
        Before the LLM era, genetic programming had been widely used for automated evolutionary algorithm design, including the design of fitness functions~\citep{fong2024metasr}, parameter adaptation techniques~\citep{stanovov2022automatic}, and update strategies~\citep{lones2021evolving}. More recently, reinforcement learning has also been applied to designing update rules in differential evolution~\citep{chen2024symbol}. However, these approaches are typically limited to generating only small code segments, as exploring large program spaces remains challenging. The emergence of LLMs has made automated algorithm design more feasible. For example, LLMs have been used to design a differential evolution algorithm competitive with state-of-the-art continuous optimization methods~\citep{van2024llamea}. Similarly, LLMs have been employed to improve the self-organizing migrating algorithm~\citep{pluhacek2024using}. This growing interest in LLMs motivates the development of efficient LLM-based approaches for automated algorithm design, where semantic awareness and code bloat are two key issues that remain underexplored and warrant further investigation.

        \subsection{Large Language Models for Optimization}
        LLMs have been employed as optimization tools across various domains~\citep{meyerson2024language,song2024position}, including neural architecture search~\citep{chen2023evoprompting}, graph neural network design~\citep{mo2025autosgnn}, reward function design~\citep{maeureka}, the traveling salesman problem~\citep{liu2024large}, prompt optimization~\citep{yanglarge}, automated machine learning~\citep{chan2024mle}, solver code generation~\citep{JiangShu2025llmopt}, symbolic regression~\citep{shojaee2025llmsr}, and feature engineering~\citep{gong2025evolutionary}. Evolutionary search has gained considerable attention in the LLM era due to its effectiveness over random sampling~\citep{zhang2024understanding}. Various efforts have aimed to enhance LLM-based evolutionary search, including group relative policy optimization~\citep{huang2025calm}, direct preference optimization~\citep{vsurinaalgorithm}, core abstraction prompting~\citep{wu2025efficient}, quality-uncertainty optimization~\citep{chen2024qube}, and bi-dimensional co-evolution~\citep{zhao2025understanding}.

        Exploring the potential of LLMs in automating the design of improved evolutionary search strategies is a worthwhile direction. However, evaluating LLM-based evolutionary search in automated algorithm design is computationally expensive. In comparison, genetic programming is a relatively inexpensive and historically dominant method for code generation prior to the LLM era~\citep{peabody2015gef,chen2023symbolic,maudet2025search}. Therefore, leveraging LLMs for the automated design of genetic programming algorithms presents a relevant and cost-effective avenue for investigation.
